        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_65"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_63"/>
        <dc:title>Deciphering the Impact of Temperature on Pleiotropic Consequences of RNA Polymerase Mutations</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Despite occurring in an essential molecule, mutations in RNA polymerase readily emerge and elicit complex pleiotropic effects across different levels of biological organization, which are all modulated by environment. We investigated the impact of temperature on the effects of six mutations on sequence, structure, transcriptome, and organismal traits. We found temperature altered the transcriptomic response and key organismal traits such as growth rate and biofilm formation in a genotype-specific manner. Critically, mechanistic insights into the possible drivers of mutational effects emerged only when examining the relationships between different levels of organization: location of mutations in the tertiary structure and distance to key interacting molecules partly explained the observed transcriptomic differences, which in turn drove the impact of mutations on organismal traits. While falling short of capturing the full complexity of the system, our findings underscore the benefits of integrating insights across multiple biological levels to understand the relationship between environment and mutational effects in molecules with extensive pleiotropic effects.</dcterms:abstract>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>Zotero</z:libraryCatalog>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_65">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-citation-items=&quot;%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22article-journal%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22Despite%20occurring%20in%20an%20essential%20molecule%2C%20mutations%20in%20RNA%20polymerase%20readily%20emerge%20and%20elicit%20complex%20pleiotropic%20effects%20across%20different%20levels%20of%20biological%20organization%2C%20which%20are%20all%20modulated%20by%20environment.%20We%20investigated%20the%20impact%20of%20temperature%20on%20the%20effects%20of%20six%20mutations%20on%20sequence%2C%20structure%2C%20transcriptome%2C%20and%20organismal%20traits.%20We%20found%20temperature%20altered%20the%20transcriptomic%20response%20and%20key%20organismal%20traits%20such%20as%20growth%20rate%20and%20biofilm%20formation%20in%20a%20genotype-specific%20manner.%20Critically%2C%20mechanistic%20insights%20into%20the%20possible%20drivers%20of%20mutational%20effects%20emerged%20only%20when%20examining%20the%20relationships%20between%20different%20levels%20of%20organization%3A%20location%20of%20mutations%20in%20the%20tertiary%20structure%20and%20distance%20to%20key%20interacting%20molecules%20partly%20explained%20the%20observed%20transcriptomic%20differences%2C%20which%20in%20turn%20drove%20the%20impact%20of%20mutations%20on%20organismal%20traits.%20While%20falling%20short%20of%20capturing%20the%20full%20complexity%20of%20the%20system%2C%20our%20findings%20underscore%20the%20benefits%20of%20integrating%20insights%20across%20multiple%20biological%20levels%20to%20understand%20the%20relationship%20between%20environment%20and%20mutational%20effects%20in%20molecules%20with%20extensive%20pleiotropic%20effects.%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en%22%2C%22source%22%3A%22Zotero%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Deciphering%20the%20Impact%20of%20Temperature%20on%20Pleiotropic%20Consequences%20of%20RNA%20Polymerase%20Mutations%22%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Ozbilek%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Deniz%22%7D%5D%7D%7D%5D&quot; data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FHARWKQRA%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%223%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A2%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B477.70050000000026%2C47.36825%2C560.9838000000002%2C56.00775%5D%2C%5B317.7125%2C36.370000000000005%2C560.9727000000003%2C45.0095%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%223%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“the distance between the mutated residues and the nontemplate strand of DNA,”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%223%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Ozbilek, p. 3&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FHARWKQRA%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%224%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A3%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B280.96927500000004%2C706.6958476%2C294.326275%2C716.1385%5D%2C%5B51.024450000000016%2C696.50075%2C294.3198%2C705.14025%5D%2C%5B51.024450000000016%2C685.5025%2C294.29297500000007%2C694.142%5D%2C%5B51.024450000000016%2C674.5042500000001%2C294.265225%2C683.1437500000001%5D%2C%5B51.024450000000016%2C663.506%2C294.29760000000005%2C672.1455%5D%2C%5B51.024450000000016%2C652.50775%2C243.32270000000005%2C661.14725%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%224%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“We hypothesize that mutations closer to these key sites have the potential to exert a stronger effect on RNAP function and therefore have a greater influence on fitness—a hypothesis we will test in relation to transcriptomic- and organismal-level effects of rpoB mutations in subsequent sections”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%224%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Ozbilek, p. 4&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FHARWKQRA%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%224%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A3%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B377.85805000000005%2C564.461475%2C555.592175%2C573.100975%5D%2C%5B312.320875%2C553.463225%2C555.573675%2C562.102725%5D%2C%5B312.320875%2C542.46405%2C555.592175%2C551.10355%5D%2C%5B312.320875%2C531.4658%2C476.7886500000001%2C540.1052999999999%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%224%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“Instead, our data shows that the response to temperature change is dominated by the effects of rpoB mutations, which substantially alter the transcriptomic response to temperature in genotype-specific manner.”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%224%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Ozbilek, p. 4&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FHARWKQRA%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%225%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A4%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B378.7090500000001%2C344.38495%2C560.9886249999998%2C353.02445%5D%2C%5B317.70345000000003%2C333.3867%2C560.9340500000003%2C342.0262%5D%2C%5B317.70345000000003%2C322.38845000000003%2C337.4300000000001%2C331.02795000000003%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%225%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“While many factors might impact bacterial growth, overexpression of metabolic genes is certainly one of them”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%225%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Ozbilek, p. 5&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FHARWKQRA%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%225%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A4%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B400.02472500000033%2C300.3873%2C560.993225%2C309.0268%5D%2C%5B317.7062%2C289.38905%2C560.9913750000001%2C298.02855%5D%2C%5B317.7062%2C278.3908%2C560.988575%2C287.03125%5D%2C%5B317.7034%2C267.3926%2C406.5154250000003%2C276.033%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%225%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“both Q513R and S531F were found to overexpress genes involved in the production of ATP-synthase (atpA/C/G/D)—a ubiquitously expressed enzyme shown to maximize growth rate”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%225%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Ozbilek, p. 5&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FHARWKQRA%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%225%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A4%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B545.7241999999999%2C267.3926%2C560.9348999999997%2C276.033%5D%2C%5B317.70799999999997%2C256.39435000000003%2C560.9950249999999%2C265.03385000000003%5D%2C%5B317.70799999999997%2C245.39610000000002%2C560.9534249999997%2C254.03650000000002%5D%2C%5B317.7034%2C234.39875%2C560.9589749999996%2C243.03825%5D%2C%5B317.7034%2C223.4005%2C560.9830249999998%2C232.04%5D%2C%5B317.7034%2C212.40040000000002%2C560.99875%2C221.04174999999998%5D%2C%5B317.7062%2C201.4022%2C348.8824000000001%2C210.0417%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%225%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“The same two mutants also overexpressed genes that are a part of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (aceE/F) as well as genes involved in phosphorylation of pyruvate (ppsA) and conversion of L-lactate to pyruvate (lldD). Pyruvate is essential for aerobic growth and has been shown to increase the rate of aerobic respiration”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F58AVRU9Z%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%225%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Ozbilek, p. 5&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Ozbilek et al. (2025) — Summary&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Background &amp;amp; Motivation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper addresses a fundamental problem in evolutionary biology: mutations in RNA polymerase (RNAP), despite occurring in one of the most essential and conserved molecules in biology, emerge frequently under rifampicin selection and produce extraordinarily complex downstream consequences. These consequences cascade across multiple levels of biological organisation — sequence, structure, transcriptome, and organismal phenotype — and each level can be independently modulated by environment. The authors argue that studying these levels in isolation (as most prior work has done) prevents mechanistic understanding and limits predictability of evolutionary outcomes. Their goal is both to characterise mutational effects at each level &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; to find causal links connecting them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Experimental System&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The study uses &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; BW25113 ΔtolC (the tolC deletion forces resistance to emerge through &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutation rather than efflux). Six single-point &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants were generated via fluctuation assay and confirmed by whole-genome sequencing: &lt;strong&gt;V146F, Q513L, Q513R, S522F, H526Y, and S531F&lt;/strong&gt;. These were chosen to cover a range of fitness costs and to include two mutations at the same residue (Q513L and Q513R), enabling within-position comparisons. All experiments were conducted at &lt;strong&gt;37°C&lt;/strong&gt; (optimal for &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;) and &lt;strong&gt;42°C&lt;/strong&gt; (modest heat stress), allowing characterisation of both mutational effects and their temperature dependence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Level 1: Structural Effects&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors computationally assessed two structural properties for each mutant using multiple PDB crystal structures:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Protein stability (ΔΔG via FoldX):&lt;/strong&gt; Three mutants (V146F, S522F, S531F) destabilised RpoB; two (Q513L, H526Y) were stabilising; Q513R had negligible effect. Stability differed significantly across mutants.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Euclidean distances to key interaction partners:&lt;/strong&gt; The distance from each mutated residue to the β′ subunit (RpoC), σ factors, nontemplate DNA strand, template DNA strand, nascent RNA, and rifampicin was calculated. Distances to template DNA and nascent RNA did not differ significantly between mutants, but distances to &lt;strong&gt;nontemplate DNA, β′, and σ factors differed significantly&lt;/strong&gt; — implicating these interactions as potentially relevant to downstream effects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Crucially, modelling the effect of changing temperature from 37 to 42°C on structural stability (FoldX temperature parameter) produced negligible differences, and the ~0.075% isotropic thermal expansion of proteins at this temperature range is far below crystallographic coordinate error. The authors therefore conservatively assumed that the &lt;strong&gt;rank order of structural properties is maintained across temperatures&lt;/strong&gt;, while acknowledging this as a limitation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Level 2: Transcriptomic Effects (RNA-seq)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the most data-rich component of the paper. RNA-seq was performed on all six mutants and wild-type at both temperatures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key findings:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutant identity dominates over temperature.&lt;/strong&gt; A PERMANOVA on transcriptome-wide expression profiles showed that genotype-specific variation (r² = 0.30) and the genotype × temperature interaction (r² = 0.29) each explained nearly twice as much variance as temperature alone (r² = 0.17). Mutant identity, not temperature per se, was the primary driver of transcriptomic differences.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Temperature response is highly mutation-specific.&lt;/strong&gt; When comparing 42°C vs. 37°C within each genotype (i.e. the temperature effect), the number of differentially expressed genes (DEGs) ranged from 93 (Q513L) to 378 (S522F). In five of six mutants, more genes were upregulated than downregulated at the higher temperature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DEG identity is almost entirely mutation-specific.&lt;/strong&gt; Of 567 upregulated genes and 401 downregulated genes detected across all mutants, the majority were differentially expressed in only a single mutant. Even the two mutations at the same residue (Q513L and Q513R) shared only 17 DEGs. No genes were differentially expressed across &lt;em&gt;all&lt;/em&gt; mutants, which is surprising — the authors had expected some shared generic heat-stress response. Instead, &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations appeared to substantially redirect and override the canonical temperature response.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Linking Structural to Transcriptomic Effects&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a key novel contribution. When the authors tested for correlations between structural properties and transcriptomic outcomes &lt;em&gt;at a given temperature&lt;/em&gt; (i.e. does greater destabilisation predict more DEGs at 37°C or 42°C independently?), no significant relationships emerged.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, when looking at the &lt;strong&gt;temperature-induced change in DEG number&lt;/strong&gt; (i.e. how much did transcription change &lt;em&gt;in response to&lt;/em&gt; the temperature shift?), two structural properties were significantly positively correlated with the total number of DEGs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Distance to nontemplate DNA strand&lt;/strong&gt; (R² = 0.77, p = 0.022)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Distance to σ factor&lt;/strong&gt; (R² = 0.65, p = 0.051)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Both correlations were stronger for downregulated genes than upregulated. Crucially, the relationship was &lt;strong&gt;positive&lt;/strong&gt; — mutations &lt;em&gt;further&lt;/em&gt; from these interaction partners showed a &lt;em&gt;larger&lt;/em&gt; transcriptomic response to temperature change. This is counterintuitive: one might expect mutations that more directly perturb σ/DNA interaction to be more disruptive. The authors suggest this could reflect a mechanism involving &lt;strong&gt;thermal expansion&lt;/strong&gt; altering RNAP–DNA/σ factor distances, with mutations already closer to these sites potentially having already maximally disrupted the interaction, leaving less room for temperature-dependent change. They note this temperature–RNAP structure relationship has not previously been investigated.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;No significant correlation was found between ΔΔG (stability) and DEG number, suggesting protein stability per se is not the driver of transcriptomic dysregulation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Level 3: Organismal Effects&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Growth (fitness as AUC):&lt;/strong&gt; Q513R showed markedly reduced growth at both temperatures. For most mutants and wild-type, there was a slight (non-significant) increase in growth at 42°C vs. 37°C. Two mutants — &lt;strong&gt;Q513R and S531F&lt;/strong&gt; — showed a significantly larger growth increase at 42°C, with Q513R's growth deficit relative to wild-type substantially reduced at the higher temperature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Linking transcriptome to growth:&lt;/strong&gt; The total number of DEGs (42 vs. 37°C) was negatively but non-significantly correlated with the change in AUC between temperatures (adj. r² = 0.36, p = 0.09). This suggests that the &lt;em&gt;identity&lt;/em&gt; of dysregulated genes matters more than their number for fitness outcomes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GSEA (Gene Set Enrichment Analysis with KEGG):&lt;/strong&gt; To move beyond gene counts, the authors performed GSEA ranked by log-fold change. This revealed that the same two mutants with greater fitness at 42°C (Q513R and S531F) were also the &lt;strong&gt;only ones with significant overexpression of metabolic pathway genes&lt;/strong&gt; at 42°C. Specific metabolic genes upregulated included &lt;em&gt;atpA/C/G/D&lt;/em&gt; (ATP synthase subunits), &lt;em&gt;aceE/F&lt;/em&gt; (pyruvate dehydrogenase complex), &lt;em&gt;ppsA&lt;/em&gt; (pyruvate phosphorylation), and &lt;em&gt;lldD&lt;/em&gt; (L-lactate to pyruvate conversion) — all directly relevant to aerobic respiration and growth rate maximisation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Biofilm formation:&lt;/strong&gt; Three mutants (V146F, Q513L, S531F) showed significant enrichment of downregulated biofilm genes at 42°C. Experimentally, biofilm levels (crystal violet, OD550) varied significantly across mutants, and the same three showed reduced biofilm at 42°C. S522F showed a modest (non-significant) increase. The transcriptomic–organismal link for biofilm was thus reasonably linear.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ABC transporters (nickel):&lt;/strong&gt; Several mutants showed enrichment of ABC transporter genes. Intracellular nickel (measured by ICP-MS) varied significantly between mutants, but did not map cleanly onto the transcriptomic ABC transporter signal — Q513R's increased expression did correlate with higher nickel, but wild-type and S531F did not show similar correlations, and S522F had high nickel without ABC transporter gene enrichment. This demonstrated a case where the transcriptome-to-organismal link was &lt;strong&gt;nonlinear and unpredictable&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Cross-Level Synthesis (Fig. 7)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper's central contribution is demonstrating a &lt;strong&gt;through-line&lt;/strong&gt; connecting all investigated levels:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Structural distances (mutation → σ factor/DNA) predict...
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The magnitude of temperature-induced transcriptomic change, which acts in a function-specific manner to drive...
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Measurable organismal consequences (growth and biofilm), though some links (ABC transporters) proved complex and nonlinear.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors frame both the &lt;em&gt;presence&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;absence&lt;/em&gt; of linear connections as informative — the nonlinearity of the ABC transporter case, for example, points to post-transcriptional regulatory layers not captured by RNA-seq alone.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Evolutionary Implications&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors note several implications for evolutionary biology. Mutants with higher growth at 42°C could be advantaged in infection contexts where fever raises body temperature. The global distribution of rifampicin resistance alleles may be shaped in part by climate and temperature variation. Differences in biofilm formation under temperature change suggest that genotype-by-environment interactions in niche adaptation would be missed by measuring growth rate alone, underscoring the value of multi-phenotype characterisation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Limitations&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors are candid about several constraints:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The assumption that structural rank order is preserved across temperatures may not hold for all mutants.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The study does not directly measure molecular-level RNAP function (initiation, elongation, termination rates) — no tools currently allow modelling these as a function of specific mutations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Only six mutants in one genetic background limit generalisability, though the mutations studied are widespread across species.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The ABC transporter case illustrates that post-transcriptional processes can decouple mRNA expression from protein activity and metabolite levels.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
