        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_71"/>
        <dc:title>Environmental variation alters the fitness effects of rifampicin resistance mutations in &lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa&lt;/i&gt;: BRIEF COMMUNICATION</dc:title>
        <dc:date>03/2016</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Environmental variation alters the fitness effects of rifampicin resistance mutations in &lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa&lt;/i&gt;</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/evolut/article/70/3/725/6851936</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-02 03:49:21</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/tdm_license_1.1</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>725-730</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_75">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Summary: Gifford et al. (2016) — Environmental Variation and Fitness Costs of Rifampicin Resistance in &lt;em&gt;P. aeruginosa&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Background &amp;amp; Central Question&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Antibiotic resistance typically carries a &lt;strong&gt;fitness cost&lt;/strong&gt; in antibiotic-free environments. Bacteria can escape this cost in two ways: (1) acquiring &lt;strong&gt;compensatory mutations&lt;/strong&gt; that offset the cost while retaining resistance, or (2) &lt;strong&gt;reverting&lt;/strong&gt; back to the antibiotic-sensitive wild type. Prior research suggested compensatory adaptation is far more common than reversion, but most studies examined only a narrow range of environments. This paper asks: does environmental heterogeneity alter the fitness effects of resistance mutations, and could it therefore shift the balance between compensation and reversion?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Experimental System&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors used &lt;em&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa&lt;/em&gt; (strain PAO1) carrying three clinically relevant mutations in the &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; gene (which encodes the β-subunit of RNA polymerase — the target of rifampicin):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;rpoB531R&lt;/strong&gt; — high fitness cost
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;rpoB531Y&lt;/strong&gt; — lower fitness cost
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;rpoB531C&lt;/strong&gt; — a second-step mutation known to compensate for the costs of R and Y; inaccessible from wild type by a single mutation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These three form a specific mutational pathway (Fig. 1 in the paper): wild type → R or Y (costly resistance) → C (compensated resistance). The wild type and rpoB531C represent two fitness peaks separated by the fitness valleys of R and Y — a classic rugged fitness landscape.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fitness was measured competitively across:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Three temperatures:&lt;/strong&gt; 30°C, 37°C, and 42°C (in nutrient-rich LB medium)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nine carbon substrates&lt;/strong&gt; at 37°C (using Biolog EcoPlates)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Findings&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;1. Rank Order of Fitness Is Generally Preserved&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Across almost all conditions tested, the rank order &lt;strong&gt;rpoB531R &amp;lt; rpoB531Y &amp;lt; rpoB531C&lt;/strong&gt; was maintained. This means rpoB531C consistently acted as a general compensatory mutation — it was fitter than the costly resistance mutations regardless of environment. This suggests compensatory adaptation is broadly favoured, not just in standard lab conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;2. Temperature Has a Profound Effect on Fitness Effects&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While rank order was stable, the &lt;strong&gt;magnitude&lt;/strong&gt; of fitness effects changed dramatically with temperature:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At &lt;strong&gt;37°C&lt;/strong&gt; (standard growth temperature): rpoB531C was statistically indistinguishable from wild type — i.e., no detectable cost &lt;em&gt;or&lt;/em&gt; benefit.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At &lt;strong&gt;30°C&lt;/strong&gt;: rpoB531C was significantly &lt;em&gt;more fit&lt;/em&gt; than wild type — resistance actually became &lt;em&gt;advantageous&lt;/em&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At &lt;strong&gt;42°C&lt;/strong&gt;: rpoB531C was significantly &lt;em&gt;less fit&lt;/em&gt; than wild type — a genuine fitness cost emerged.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is biologically intuitive: &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; is a master regulator of gene expression, and temperature strongly shifts the expression landscape in &lt;em&gt;P. aeruginosa&lt;/em&gt; (including virulence genes at 37°C and heat shock responses at 42°C).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;3. Carbon Substrate Has Only a Weak Effect&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Across nine carbon sources, fitness effects varied but the interaction between genotype and carbon source was &lt;strong&gt;not statistically significant&lt;/strong&gt;. rpoB531C was less fit than wild type on four carbon sources individually, but the overall average difference was not significant. The weak carbon substrate effect contrasts sharply with the strong temperature effect, suggesting that metabolic context matters less than thermal environment for these mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;4. Implications for Compensatory Adaptation vs. Reversion&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because rpoB531C is generally the fittest resistant genotype, once it becomes fixed in a population it creates a situation where &lt;strong&gt;reversion to wild-type sensitivity is extremely difficult&lt;/strong&gt; — the genetic code structure means reverting from C back to H (wild type) requires passing back through the fitness valleys of R or Y. This epistatic constraint helps explain why the equivalent mutation (&lt;em&gt;rpoB445C&lt;/em&gt;) is commonly found in clinical &lt;em&gt;Mycobacterium tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; isolates resistant to rifampicin.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors note one theoretical exception: exposure to L-arginine might select for rpoB531Y from an rpoB531C background, and subsequent exposure to 42°C or putrescine (where the cost of C is most apparent) could then theoretically promote reversion to wild type — but this would be undermined by non-compensatory beneficial mutations accumulating along the way.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Broader Implications&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Environmental heterogeneity matters:&lt;/strong&gt; The fitness cost of resistance is not a fixed number — it depends on the environment. Models of resistance evolution that ignore this may produce inaccurate predictions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compensation is general, but not always complete:&lt;/strong&gt; rpoB531C is a general compensatory mutation, but it can itself be costly (at 42°C) or beneficial (at 30°C) depending on conditions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reversion is unlikely once compensation is established:&lt;/strong&gt; The rugged fitness landscape and conserved rank order mean that compensated resistant strains are evolutionarily &quot;trapped&quot; — environmental variation alone is unlikely to drive reversion to sensitivity.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Clinical relevance:&lt;/strong&gt; As resistance genes migrate between environments (e.g., within a host, between hosts, or between clinical and community settings), the changing fitness landscape may influence which evolutionary trajectories are accessible.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
