        <dc:title>The fitness effect of mutations across environments: Fisher's geometrical model with multiple optima: FISHER'S MODEL ACROSS ENVIRONMENTS</dc:title>
        <dc:date>06/2015</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>The fitness effect of mutations across environments</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/evolut/article/69/6/1433/6852012</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-02 03:51:59</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/tdm_license_1.1</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>1433-1447</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_79">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Paper overview&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Martin &amp;amp; Lenormand (2015)&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;em&gt;The fitness effect of mutations across environments: Fisher’s geometrical model with multiple optima&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The core question&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;How do the fitness effects of mutations change across environments, and what determines whether mutations are beneficial, deleterious, or conditionally beneficial?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors extend &lt;strong&gt;Fisher’s Geometrical Model (FGM)&lt;/strong&gt; to situations where:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
There are &lt;strong&gt;multiple environments&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Each environment has its &lt;strong&gt;own fitness optimum&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The same mutation affects performance in &lt;em&gt;all&lt;/em&gt; environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This lets them explain:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Why &lt;strong&gt;most mutations are deleterious&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Why &lt;strong&gt;beneficial mutations are rare&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Why &lt;strong&gt;trade-offs emerge naturally&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Why &lt;strong&gt;environmental change reshapes adaptive value&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Fisher’s Geometrical Model (FGM): the intuition first&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Imagine this picture&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
An organism has &lt;strong&gt;n traits&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g. enzyme efficiency, stability, expression level)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Each trait is a coordinate in an n-dimensional space
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;optimal phenotype&lt;/strong&gt; is a point in that space
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fitness declines smoothly as you move away from the optimum
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Think of it as a &lt;strong&gt;hill&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The peak = optimal phenotype
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Distance from the peak = how bad you are doing
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutations move the organism around in this space.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Single environment: the classic FGM&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Fitness function (simplified)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fitness depends on &lt;strong&gt;distance to the optimum&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;W=exp⁡(−αd2)W = \exp(-\alpha d^2)W=exp(−αd2)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Don’t panic — this just means:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ddd = distance from optimum
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fitness drops faster the further you are away
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
α\alphaα controls how steep the drop is
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Closer to optimum = higher fitness&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What does a mutation do?&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A mutation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Moves you by a random vector in trait space
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sometimes toward the optimum (good)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Usually away from it (bad)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This already explains why:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most mutations are deleterious
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Large mutations are more likely harmful
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Small mutations are more likely beneficial when far from optimum
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;What this paper adds: multiple environments&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Now comes the key extension.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Each environment has its own optimum&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environment 1 → optimum at point &lt;strong&gt;O₁&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environment 2 → optimum at point &lt;strong&gt;O₂&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These optima are separated by some distance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This means:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
You cannot be perfectly adapted to all environments at once
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Trade-offs are inevitable
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;The key geometric idea&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;z&lt;/strong&gt; = phenotype vector of the organism
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;z₁*&lt;/strong&gt; = optimum in environment 1
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;z₂*&lt;/strong&gt; = optimum in environment 2
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Distance to each optimum:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;d1=∣∣z−z1∗∣∣,d2=∣∣z−z2∗∣∣d_1 = ||z - z_1^*||, \quad d_2 = ||z - z_2^*||d1​=∣∣z−z1∗​∣∣,d2​=∣∣z−z2∗​∣∣&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fitness in each environment:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wi=exp⁡(−αdi2)W_i = \exp(-\alpha d_i^2)Wi​=exp(−αdi2​)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Same organism, same mutation — &lt;strong&gt;different fitness effects&lt;/strong&gt; depending on which optimum you measure distance to.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;What happens when a mutation occurs&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A mutation adds a vector &lt;strong&gt;m&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;z′=z+mz' = z + mz′=z+m&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The fitness effect depends on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Direction of m&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Size of m&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Where the optima are&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Mutation is beneficial in environment 1 if:&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;∣∣z+m−z1∗∣∣&amp;lt;∣∣z−z1∗∣∣||z + m - z_1^*|| &amp;lt; ||z - z_1^*||∣∣z+m−z1∗​∣∣&amp;lt;∣∣z−z1∗​∣∣&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But it can &lt;em&gt;simultaneously&lt;/em&gt; be deleterious in environment 2 if:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;∣∣z+m−z2∗∣∣&amp;gt;∣∣z−z2∗∣∣||z + m - z_2^*|| &amp;gt; ||z - z_2^*||∣∣z+m−z2∗​∣∣&amp;gt;∣∣z−z2∗​∣∣&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ This is the mathematical origin of &lt;strong&gt;antagonistic pleiotropy&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Correlation of fitness effects across environments&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One of the paper’s main results is about &lt;strong&gt;fitness correlations&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;If optima are close together&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutations tend to have &lt;strong&gt;similar effects&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fitness effects are positively correlated
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Generalists are possible
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;If optima are far apart&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutations good in one environment tend to be bad in another
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fitness effects become uncorrelated or negative
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Specialization is favored
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This result falls straight out of geometry — no special assumptions needed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Why beneficial mutations are rare&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The math shows that a mutation is beneficial only if:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Its direction points &lt;em&gt;toward&lt;/em&gt; the optimum
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Its magnitude isn’t too large
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In n dimensions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most directions point &lt;em&gt;away&lt;/em&gt; from the optimum
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Probability of benefit shrinks as dimensionality increases
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is why:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Adaptation slows over time
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Large-effect beneficial mutations are rare
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Adaptation depends strongly on initial maladaptation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Environment-dependent sign of mutations&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A key prediction:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The same mutation can be beneficial, neutral, or deleterious depending on the environment&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mathematically, this comes from:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Different dot products between &lt;strong&gt;m&lt;/strong&gt; and the vector to each optimum
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Intuitively:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A step toward one peak is a step away from another
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Big theoretical results&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Trade-offs emerge automatically&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You don’t need:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Resource constraints
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Explicit costs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Special assumptions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Trade-offs come from &lt;strong&gt;geometry alone&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Conditional neutrality is common&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Many mutations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Have near-zero effects in one environment
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Large effects in another
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This helps explain:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cryptic genetic variation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sudden fitness changes after environmental shifts
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Plasticity vs genetic adaptation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The model clarifies when:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Plastic responses are favored (optima far apart)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genetic generalists are possible (optima close)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Why this paper matters for your work&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Given your focus on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Temperature shifts
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Stress responses
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rpoB / transcriptional machinery
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environment-specific adaptation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This model explains &lt;strong&gt;why mutations in global regulators often show context-dependent fitness&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
RNA polymerase mutations shift many traits at once
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
High-dimensional phenotype space
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Strong environment × mutation interactions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;One-sentence takeaway&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fitness effects are not intrinsic properties of mutations — they are geometric consequences of where organisms sit relative to multiple environmental optima.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;The “big idea” takeaway&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fitness is not a property of mutations — it is a property of mutations &lt;em&gt;relative to environments&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Evolutionary constraints, trade-offs, and specialization emerge from:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
High-dimensional phenotypes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Multiple environmental optima
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Simple mutational steps
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
