    <bib:Article rdf:about="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0952-4746/22/3/702">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf rdf:resource="urn:issn:0952-4746"/>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_152"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_150"/>
        <dc:title>NRPB Chilton Seminar: Professor Bryn Bridges`Genetic Effects of Radiation: Are the Paradigms Changing?' (Chilton, November 2001)</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2002-09-01</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>NRPB Chilton Seminar</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0952-4746/22/3/702</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-17 00:31:19</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>329-330</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0952-4746">
        <prism:volume>22</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Journal of Radiological Protection</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1088/0952-4746/22/3/702</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>3</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>J. Radiol. Prot.</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0952-4746</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_152">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;experimental evolution studies have shown that the repeatability of evolution increases with population size&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Paper overview&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;van Dijk et al. (2017)&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Mutation supply and the repeatability of selection for antibiotic resistance&lt;/em&gt;, asks a deceptively simple question:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;When is evolution predictable, and when is it not?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They focus specifically on &lt;strong&gt;repeatability&lt;/strong&gt;—whether independent populations evolve the &lt;em&gt;same genetic solutions&lt;/em&gt; under the same conditions—which is a prerequisite for predictability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors investigate how &lt;strong&gt;mutation supply&lt;/strong&gt; (i.e. how many mutations are available to selection) and &lt;strong&gt;selection strength&lt;/strong&gt; interact with &lt;strong&gt;fitness landscapes&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;epistasis&lt;/strong&gt; during the evolution of antibiotic resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Experimental system and design&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Biological system&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Gene:&lt;/strong&gt; TEM-1 β-lactamase
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Organism:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Selection pressure:&lt;/strong&gt; cefotaxime (β-lactam antibiotic)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;TEM-1 is a classic model for studying fitness landscapes because:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Many resistance mutations are known
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sign epistasis between mutations has been well characterized
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Alternative adaptive trajectories are well documented
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Mutant libraries (mutation supply)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They generated mutant libraries using &lt;strong&gt;error-prone PCR&lt;/strong&gt;, producing:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Libraries of different sizes (from very small to very large)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A Poisson distribution of mutations per allele
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Library size directly controls &lt;strong&gt;mutation supply&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Small libraries → few beneficial mutations sampled
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Large libraries → many beneficial mutations sampled
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Selection regime&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Libraries were exposed to &lt;strong&gt;increasing antibiotic concentrations&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Outcomes measured:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Population survival vs extinction
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Which TEM alleles fixed in surviving populations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Conceptual framework: fitness landscapes &amp;amp; sign epistasis&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper is explicitly grounded in the &lt;strong&gt;fitness landscape&lt;/strong&gt; metaphor introduced by &lt;strong&gt;Sewall Wright&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key ideas:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genotypes map to fitness values
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Epistasis shapes landscape topology
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sign epistasis&lt;/strong&gt; determines which mutational paths are accessible
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They rely heavily on prior work showing that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sign epistasis restricts evolutionary pathways&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Initial mutations (e.g. G238S vs R164S) commit populations to &lt;em&gt;different adaptive trajectories&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Main findings&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Extinction is largely explained by mutation supply&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Small libraries and strong selection:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Frequently went extinct
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Often failed to sample &lt;em&gt;any&lt;/em&gt; beneficial mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;simple stochastic model&lt;/strong&gt; assuming:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Random sampling of beneficial mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Hard selection
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;was sufficient to explain extinction patterns.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Key point:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Survival does &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; require complex epistasis—just the presence of at least one sufficiently beneficial mutation. van_Dijk_2017_Phys._Biol._14_05…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Large-effect mutations dominate early adaptation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Across surviving populations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most fixed alleles contained &lt;strong&gt;known large-effect resistance mutations&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Especially:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
G238S
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
R164S
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
R241P
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Models that &lt;strong&gt;ignored epistasis entirely&lt;/strong&gt; but included only these large-effect mutations best fit the data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Implication:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Despite known ruggedness of the fitness landscape, &lt;strong&gt;early adaptation is surprisingly predictable&lt;/strong&gt; at the level of &lt;em&gt;which mutations appear&lt;/em&gt;. van_Dijk_2017_Phys._Biol._14_05…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Repeatability is highest at intermediate mutation supply&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They find a &lt;strong&gt;non-monotonic relationship&lt;/strong&gt; between library size and repeatability:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Library size&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Outcome&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Small&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Unpredictable (drift + extinction)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Intermediate&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Highly repeatable&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Large&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Less repeatable again&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In large libraries:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Rare multi-mutation genotypes appear
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These can outcompete single large-effect mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Different replicates fix &lt;em&gt;different&lt;/em&gt; high-fitness combinations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Key insight:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;More variation does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; always mean more predictability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4. Sign epistasis shapes long-term trajectories, not short-term outcomes&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper makes a subtle but important distinction:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Short-term selection&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;→ dominated by large-effect mutations&lt;br&gt;→ relatively predictable
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Long-term evolution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;→ initial mutations constrain future paths&lt;br&gt;→ sign epistasis leads to divergent trajectories
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fixing &lt;strong&gt;G238S&lt;/strong&gt; vs &lt;strong&gt;R164S&lt;/strong&gt; sends populations down different adaptive routes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This reflects classic &lt;strong&gt;historical contingency&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ Sign epistasis matters most for &lt;em&gt;where evolution can go next&lt;/em&gt;, not for which mutation fixes first. van_Dijk_2017_Phys._Biol._14_05…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;5. Epistasis exists—but often hides in the background&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Although epistasis is well documented in TEM-1:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most outcomes here can be explained without invoking it
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Epistasis becomes visible mainly when:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Multiple mutations combine
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Rare high-resistance genotypes arise
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors explicitly note that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Their main analysis assumes &lt;strong&gt;weak epistasis&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Exceptions exist, but they are not the dominant force early on van_Dijk_2017_Phys._Biol._14_05…
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Big picture conclusions&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What this paper shows&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Evolution can be predictable&lt;/strong&gt;, even on rugged fitness landscapes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Predictability depends strongly on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutation supply
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Selection strength
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Large-effect mutations dominate early adaptation&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sign epistasis limits evolutionary paths&lt;/strong&gt;, but does not necessarily reduce short-term predictability
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Stochastic events early in selection can permanently alter evolutionary trajectories
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
