        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_158"/>
        <dc:title>Phenotypic Plasticity: From Theory and Genetics to Current and Future Challenges</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Phenotypic plasticity is deﬁned as the property of organisms to produce distinct phenotypes in response to environmental variation. While for more than a century, biologists have proposed this organismal feature to play an important role in evolution and the origin of novelty, the idea has remained contentious. Plasticity is found in all domains of life, but only recently has there been an increase in empirical studies. This contribution is intended as a fresh view and will discuss current and future challenges of plasticity research, and the need to identify associated molecular mechanisms. After a brief summary of conceptual, theoretical, and historical aspects, some of which were responsible for confusion and contention, I will formulate three major research directions and predictions for the role of plasticity as a facilitator of novelty. These predictions result in a four-step model that, when properly ﬁlled with molecular mechanisms, will reveal plasticity as a major factor of evolution. Such mechanistic insight must be complemented with comparative investigations to show that plasticity has indeed created novelty and innovation. Together, such studies will help develop a true developmental evolutionary biology.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2020-05-01</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Phenotypic Plasticity</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/genetics/article/215/1/1/5930418</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-23 03:39:53</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>https://academic.oup.com/journals/pages/open_access/funder_policies/chorus/standard_publication_model</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>1-13</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_160">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Abstract (Overview)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main message:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Phenotypic plasticity — the ability of one genotype to produce different phenotypes depending on environment — is widespread but historically controversial in evolutionary biology.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key points:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Plasticity occurs across all life forms
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It may play a major role in evolutionary innovation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Understanding requires identifying &lt;strong&gt;molecular mechanisms&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The author proposes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Three major research directions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A four-step model of how plasticity drives evolution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;1️⃣ Introduction — What Is Phenotypic Plasticity?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Definition&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Phenotypic plasticity = environmentally induced variation in traits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It can affect:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Morphology
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Physiology
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Behavior
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Life history
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Development
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Important idea&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plasticity is universal — even microbes and viruses show it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Phage λ switching between lytic and lysogenic cycles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Historical controversy&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Neo-Darwinian thinking often downplayed plasticity, arguing:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Lack of empirical evidence
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Plasticity might hinder evolution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Unknown molecular mechanisms
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Modern research challenges these assumptions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;2️⃣ Three Conceptual Features of Plasticity&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sommer clarifies confusion by identifying three independent dimensions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;A. Continuous vs Discrete Plasticity&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Continuous:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Gradual variation across environments&lt;br&gt;→ Hard to distinguish from genetic differences&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Discrete (polyphenism):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Distinct alternative forms&lt;br&gt;→ Easier to study experimentally&lt;br&gt;→ Key to theory development&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Examples: seasonal forms, caste systems, defensive morphs&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;B. Adaptive vs Nonadaptive Plasticity&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Adaptive:&lt;/strong&gt; improves fitness in new environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nonadaptive:&lt;/strong&gt; initially harmful or neutral but may enable future adaptation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Important emerging idea:&lt;br&gt;Nonadaptive responses can still fuel evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;C. Conditional vs Stochastic Regulation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conditional:&lt;/strong&gt; environment triggers specific response&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stochastic:&lt;/strong&gt; random switching independent of environment&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Microbial examples:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Persister cells
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Spore formation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Both mechanisms can coexist.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;3️⃣ Historical Development of the Concept&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Explains why the field became confusing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Baldwin Effect (1896)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Learned behaviors influence evolution by altering fitness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Reaction Norm — Woltereck (1909)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Describes phenotype across environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Later clarified by Johannsen:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Distinction between genotype and phenotype
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genotype × environment interaction
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Genetic Assimilation — Schmalhausen &amp;amp; Waddington&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Environmentally induced traits can become genetically fixed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Waddington showed:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Developmental perturbations can produce heritable traits
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Introduced epigenetic thinking before molecular genetics
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Polymorphism vs Polyphenism — Mayr (1963)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Polymorphism:&lt;/strong&gt; genetic variation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Polyphenism:&lt;/strong&gt; environmental variation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Critical conceptual distinction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Bradshaw (1965)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Major breakthrough:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Plasticity itself is genetically controlled.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different species can have different degrees of plasticity for the same trait.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;4️⃣ West-Eberhard’s Contributions&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mary Jane West-Eberhard’s work is presented as a turning point.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Her four major contributions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Demonstrated Plasticity Is Ubiquitous&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Collected extensive examples of alternative phenotypes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Alternative Phenotypes Can Be Independent Targets of Selection&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different forms can evolve separately.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Critique of Traditional Evolutionary Theory&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Neo-Darwinism focuses too heavily on genes, ignoring development and environment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4. Plasticity as a Facilitator of Novelty&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;New traits often evolve from plastic responses.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key idea:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Plasticity allows new traits to evolve without losing old ones.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;5️⃣ Three Predictions About Plasticity in Evolution&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To support the “facilitator hypothesis,” three conditions must hold.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Prediction 1 — Novelty Often Starts With Plasticity&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(“Plasticity-first evolution”)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Environmental change induces variation → selection refines it → trait may become fixed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Example: Spadefoot Toad Tadpoles&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ancestral omnivore form
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Plastic species produce carnivorous morph when food changes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Some species evolved to be permanently carnivorous
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This supports plasticity preceding evolutionary change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Prediction 2 — Plasticity Requires Developmental Switch Genes&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plastic responses are controlled by genes that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sense environmental signals
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reprogram development
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Example: Nematode Mouth Forms&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In &lt;em&gt;Pristionchus pacificus&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Two morphs: bacterial feeder vs predator
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Controlled by gene &lt;strong&gt;eud-1&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Gene dosage determines morph outcome
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Demonstrates a true molecular “switch”
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key insight:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Plastic traits are not vague responses — they are genetically regulated.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Prediction 3 — Plasticity Becomes Genetically Fixed Over Time&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Environmental dependence may disappear.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Processes involved:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Canalization
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genetic accommodation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genetic assimilation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plastic trait → selection → stable inherited trait&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;6️⃣ Case Studies Supporting the Model&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper uses examples from:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Vertebrates (toads)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Nematodes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Insects
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These illustrate all stages of plasticity-driven evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;7️⃣ Four-Step Model of Plasticity-Driven Evolution&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sommer synthesizes evidence into a general mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Step 1 — Environmental Change Induces Plastic Response&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Organism produces new phenotype without genetic mutation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Step 2 — Genetic Variation in Response&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different individuals respond differently.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Selection can act on these differences.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Step 3 — Refinement by Natural Selection&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Adaptive forms increase in frequency.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Step 4 — Genetic Fixation (Canalization)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Trait becomes stable even without environmental trigger.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;8️⃣ Future Challenges Identified&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The author argues the field must focus on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;🔬 Molecular Mechanisms&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Identify genes, pathways, regulatory networks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;🌳 Comparative Studies&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use phylogenies to reconstruct evolutionary history.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;🧬 Developmental Biology Integration&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Understand how development mediates evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;🌍 Environmental Relevance&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Important for understanding responses to climate change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🎯 Final Take-Home Messages&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper argues strongly that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;✔ Phenotypic plasticity is central to evolution&lt;br&gt;✔ New traits often arise from environmental responses&lt;br&gt;✔ Plasticity is genetically controlled&lt;br&gt;✔ Molecular understanding is the next frontier&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In short:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Evolution is not only mutation → selection&lt;br&gt;👉 It can also be environment → plastic response → genetic fixation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
