        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_161"/>
        <dc:title>Phenotypic plasticity made simple, but not too simple</dc:title>
        <dc:date>10/2022</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://bsapubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ajb2.16068</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-23 07:15:11</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>1519-1524</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0002-9122,%201537-2197">
        <prism:volume>109</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>American Journal of Botany</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1002/ajb2.16068</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>10</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>American J of Botany</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0002-9122, 1537-2197</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_163">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Overall Message (Big Idea)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper argues that research on phenotypic plasticity has become &lt;strong&gt;too simplified&lt;/strong&gt; and often misleading.&lt;br&gt;The authors propose a &lt;strong&gt;clear but comprehensive framework&lt;/strong&gt; for studying plasticity realistically in nature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Their key claim:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To truly understand phenotypic plasticity, we must consider four essential components together — not in isolation. Gomulkiewicz and Stinchcombe - …&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;⭐ The Four Core Components (Central Contribution)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the heart of the paper.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1) Patterns of Plasticity — Reaction Norms&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key idea:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Plasticity should be described using &lt;strong&gt;reaction norms&lt;/strong&gt;, not simple metrics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A reaction norm =&lt;br&gt;➡️ the set of phenotypes a genotype expresses across environments&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Why this matters:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Plasticity cannot be summarized by a single number
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Different metrics can rank genotypes differently
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Real environments are complex and often nonlinear
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Important insights:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reaction norms may be:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
linear or nonlinear
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
discrete or continuous
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
influenced by internal or ancestral environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Comparing “how plastic” organisms are using one value is often meaningless
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Major recommendation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Treat plasticity as a category, not a quantity. Gomulkiewicz and Stinchcombe - …&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2) Environmental Encounters&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plasticity only matters if organisms actually experience multiple environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key points:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;distribution of environments encountered in nature&lt;/strong&gt; strongly affects outcomes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Many studies unrealistically assume environments occur equally often
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Lab designs often misrepresent real-world exposure
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Examples of environmental structure:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fine-grained variation (e.g., daily temperature changes)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Coarse-grained variation (e.g., seasonal habitats)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Spatial vs temporal variability
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Active choice of environments (habitat selection, niche construction)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Critical insight:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two populations with identical reaction norms can show different phenotypes if they encounter environments differently.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3) Fitness Consequences&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plastic traits matter only if they affect survival or reproduction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key ideas:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fitness effects can vary across environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plasticity can be:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
adaptive
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
neutral
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
maladaptive
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Plasticity does NOT automatically evolve adaptively
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Important clarifications:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plasticity can evolve non-adaptively via:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
genetic drift
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
correlated selection
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
costs of plasticity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A phenotype produced in one environment may affect fitness in another
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Selection landscapes are often nonlinear
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 The authors caution strongly against assuming plasticity is beneficial.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4) Inheritance&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plasticity must be heritable to evolve.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key points:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Genetic basis can involve:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
many genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
single major genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
epigenetic inheritance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
non-Mendelian mechanisms
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Gene expression itself can be environment-dependent
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Plasticity at the phenotype level emerges from gene-level reaction norms
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Important nuance:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genotype-by-environment interaction (G×E) is necessary but not sufficient for evolution of plasticity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Absence of G×E does not mean absence of plasticity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🚫 Major Critiques of Current Research&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper argues that many studies are misleading because they:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use only two environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Assume linear responses
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use oversimplified plasticity indices
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ignore real environmental distributions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Automatically assume adaptive plasticity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors compare this to taking many out-of-focus photos — more data doesn’t fix bad framing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🧩 Key Conceptual Insights&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Plasticity is inherently complex&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because it depends on interactions between:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
genotype
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
environment
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
development
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ecology
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
evolution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Plasticity research must integrate disciplines&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The framework connects:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
genetics
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ecology
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
evolution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
development
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
behavior
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Plasticity can be neutral&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Not all plastic traits affect fitness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This suggests a need for a &lt;strong&gt;neutral theory of plasticity evolution&lt;/strong&gt;, similar to neutral theory in molecular evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
