        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_168"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_166"/>
        <dc:title>Cost of resistance: an unreasonably expensive concept</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The concept of “cost of resistance” has been very important for decades, for fundamental reasons (theory of adaptation), with a wide range of applications for the genetics and genomics of resistance: resistance to antibiotics, insecticide, herbicide, fungicides; resistance to chemotherapy in cancer research; coevolution between all kinds of parasites and their hosts. This paper reviews this history, including latest developments, shows the interest of the idea but also challenges the usefulness and limits of this widely used concept, based on the most recent development of adaptation theory.  It explains how the concept can be flawed and how this can impede research efforts in the field of resistance at large, including all applied aspects. In particular, it would be clearer to simply measure the fitness effects of mutations across environments and to better distinguish those effects from ‘pleiotropic effects’ of those mutations. Overall, we show how to correct the concept, and how this correction helps to better understand the wealth of data that has accumulated in recent years. The main points are:
            1. The concept of «cost of resistance» needs to be carefully used, to avoid misconceptions, false paradox and flawed applications. The recent developments in adaptation theory are useful to clarify this.
            2. “Cost of resistance” and pleiotropy have to be distinguished. More than one trait is required to discuss pleiotropy. Resistance evolution must at least involve the modification of one trait. If there is an irreducible trade-off on that trait between environments with and without drug, it creates a fitness effect that is not due to pleiotropy. Pleiotropic effects can, but need not, occur in addition.
            3. “Cost of resistance” must depend on the pair of environments considered with and without drug. Hence, there are as many measures of cost as there are environments without drug. If the focal genotype is not well adapted to one focal environment, it is relatively easy to observe “negative” costs of resistance. There is nothing surprising about this, and it does not indicate an absence of trade-off.
            4. Environments with drug can differ according to the dose. It may be more informative to measure the possible trade-offs among all doses than to focus exclusively on the fitness contrast between the presence and the absence of drug.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2018-12-31</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Cost of resistance</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://rethinkingecology.pensoft.net/article/31992/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-24 06:07:50</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>51-70</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:2534-9260">
        <prism:volume>3</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Rethinking Ecology</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.3897/rethinkingecology.3.31992</dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:alternative>ReEco</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 2534-9260</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_168">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Key Concepts from Lenormand et al. (2018)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fitness Effect&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the broadest term. It simply describes how a mutation changes an organism's reproductive success in a given environment — and crucially, the paper emphasises there is no single fitness effect for any mutation. A resistance mutation might be highly beneficial in a treated environment and harmful in an untreated one. Fitness is always relational: it depends on the environment, the genetic background, and what you're comparing against. The paper argues the field should just measure fitness effects directly across environments rather than collapsing them into the blunter concept of &quot;cost.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cost of Resistance&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a specific application of fitness effect — the selection coefficient of a resistance mutation measured in the &lt;em&gt;absence&lt;/em&gt; of drug, relative to the wild type. The problem the paper identifies is that this framing implies a single, fixed property of the mutation, when in reality there are as many &quot;costs&quot; as there are non-treated environments you choose to measure in. Worse, the cost can come out as zero or even negative (the resistant mutant outperforms the wild type in the drug-free environment too), not because there's no trade-off, but because the wild type wasn't well adapted to the test environment in the first place. The word &quot;cost&quot; smuggles in assumptions that mislead researchers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pleiotropy&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is where the paper makes its sharpest conceptual move. Pleiotropy means a single mutation affects multiple traits simultaneously. In the geometric model, if the O→A axis represents the direction of &quot;resistance&quot; (the phenotypic change needed to go from the drug-free optimum to the drug-adapted optimum), then pleiotropic effects are everything &lt;em&gt;perpendicular&lt;/em&gt; to that axis — the sideways collateral changes to traits that have nothing to do with resistance itself. These can be compensated over time through ameliorating mutations that push the phenotype back onto the resistance axis. The paper's key point is that &lt;strong&gt;pleiotropy and cost of resistance are geometrically and biologically distinct things&lt;/strong&gt; — the field has been conflating them, assuming that the cost of resistance &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; the pleiotropic damage. But even if you fully compensate all pleiotropic effects, the organism is still displaced from its original optimum O, and so still pays a cost. The trade-off between environments is irreducible even with zero pleiotropy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GxE (Genotype by Environment Interaction)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This underlies the entire paper, even if not always named explicitly. GxE means the fitness effect of a genotype is not fixed but changes depending on the environment. A resistance mutation is beneficial in the treated environment and deleterious in the untreated one — that contrast &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; a GxE interaction. The paper argues the field underappreciates just how profound this is: there is no inherent property of a mutation being beneficial, deleterious, or neutral. Different drug doses create different fitness landscapes with different optima, meaning the same mutation can be a &quot;resistance mutation&quot; at one dose and irrelevant or harmful at another. The cost of resistance concept partially acknowledged GxE but then froze it into a two-environment binary (drug vs. no drug) that is far too simplistic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GxG (Genotype by Genotype Interaction, i.e. Epistasis)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper touches on this more briefly. It comes up in the context of genetic background — the fitness effect of a resistance mutation depends not just on the environment but on what other alleles are present. Compensatory mutations interact epistatically with the original resistance mutation: their benefit depends entirely on the presence of that resistance mutation. The paper notes that thinking in terms of average fitness across genetic backgrounds (as is common in sexual populations) can be very misleading when GxG interactions are strong, because the mutation doesn't experience all backgrounds equally.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
