        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_378"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_365"/>
        <dc:title>The &lt;i&gt;rpoB&lt;/i&gt; mutants destabilizing initiation complexes at stringently controlled promoters behave like “stringent” RNA polymerases in &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>In Escherichia coli, stringently controlled genes are highly transcribed during rapid growth, but ‘‘turned off’’ under nutrient limiting conditions, a process called the stringent response. To understand how transcriptional initiation at these promoters is coordinately regulated, we analyzed the interactions between RNA polymerase (RNAP) (both wild type and mutants) and four stringently controlled promoters. Our results show that the interactions between RNAP and stringently controlled promoters are intrinsically unstable and can alternate between relatively stable and metastable states. The mutant RNAPs appear to specifically further weaken interactions with these promoters in vitro and behave like ‘‘stringent’’ RNAPs in the absence of the stringent response in vivo, constituting a novel class of mutant RNAPs. Consistently, these mutant RNAPs also activate the expression of other genes that normally require the response. We propose that the stability of initiation complexes is coupled to the transcription of stringently controlled promoters, and this unique feature coordinates the expression of genes positively and negatively regulated by the stringent response.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>1998-03-17</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.95.6.2908</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-03-16 03:56:43</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>2908-2913</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_378">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Background and Context&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, a subset of genes — called &lt;strong&gt;stringently controlled genes&lt;/strong&gt; — are transcribed at very high rates during rapid growth but are sharply downregulated when nutrients become scarce (e.g., amino acid starvation). This shutdown is mediated by the &lt;strong&gt;stringent response&lt;/strong&gt;, which involves rapid accumulation of the signaling molecule &lt;strong&gt;ppGpp&lt;/strong&gt;. Stringently controlled genes mostly encode components of the translational machinery (rRNA, tRNA) but also some mRNA-encoding operons like &lt;em&gt;pyrBI&lt;/em&gt; (pyrimidine biosynthesis). Despite decades of study, the molecular mechanism coordinating this response had remained unclear.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors focused on four &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations in the β subunit of RNA polymerase (RNAP): &lt;strong&gt;rpoB114(S531F)&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;rpoB3449(D532A)&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;rpoB3443(L533P)&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;rpoB3370(T563P)&lt;/strong&gt;. These had previously been characterized as rifampicin-resistance alleles, but were known to reduce transcription from stringently controlled promoters. Crucially, these mutant RNAPs also mimicked the &lt;em&gt;in vivo&lt;/em&gt; effects of the stringent response even in the &lt;em&gt;absence&lt;/em&gt; of ppGpp — hence the paper's framing of them as behaving like &lt;strong&gt;&quot;stringent&quot; RNAPs&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Central Questions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper set out to answer two specific questions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Are these RNAP mutants broadly defective across &lt;em&gt;multiple&lt;/em&gt; stringent promoters, suggesting they are responding to a shared feature of this promoter class?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Is there a &lt;em&gt;common mechanistic defect&lt;/em&gt; underlying how these mutant RNAPs interact with stringent promoters?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Findings&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;1. The mutant RNAPs reduce transcription from multiple stringent promoters &lt;em&gt;in vivo&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using &lt;em&gt;lacZ&lt;/em&gt; reporter fusions, the authors showed that all four &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants reduced transcription from the &lt;em&gt;rrnB&lt;/em&gt; P1 promoter (a canonical stringent promoter) by 2–4-fold compared to wild type, regardless of whether the Fis protein — a transcriptional activator of &lt;em&gt;rrnB&lt;/em&gt; — was present. Importantly, the same mutants actually &lt;em&gt;increased&lt;/em&gt; transcription 2–3-fold from the non-stringent control promoter &lt;em&gt;lacUV5&lt;/em&gt;, demonstrating that the defect is &lt;strong&gt;promoter-class specific&lt;/strong&gt;, not a general reduction in RNAP activity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;2. Mutant RNAPs form abnormally unstable initiation complexes at stringent promoters &lt;em&gt;in vitro&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the paper's most mechanistically important finding. Using &lt;em&gt;in vitro&lt;/em&gt; transcription assays with purified RNAPs and four stringent promoters (&lt;em&gt;rrnB&lt;/em&gt; P1, &lt;em&gt;pyrBI&lt;/em&gt;, and P1/P2 of the &lt;em&gt;rpoD&lt;/em&gt; operon), the authors characterized the &lt;strong&gt;stability of RNAP–promoter open complexes&lt;/strong&gt; using competitors like heparin, poly[d(A·T)], and challenging plasmid DNA.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Wild-type RNAP formed stable complexes at all four stringent promoters on supercoiled DNA, with half-lives of roughly 15–40 minutes.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The mutant RNAPs formed &lt;strong&gt;dramatically less stable complexes&lt;/strong&gt; at all four stringent promoters. For example, with RpoB3449 (the least-defective mutant) at &lt;em&gt;rrnB&lt;/em&gt; P1, the half-life dropped from ~15 min (wild type) to only ~4–5 min. At &lt;em&gt;pyrBI&lt;/em&gt;, the mutant complex half-life was under 30 seconds.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Crucially, the mutant RNAPs performed &lt;strong&gt;normally or even better&lt;/strong&gt; at non-stringent control promoters (RNAI, &lt;em&gt;tac&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;lacUV5&lt;/em&gt;, promoter P upstream of &lt;em&gt;pyrBI&lt;/em&gt;), confirming the specificity of the defect.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;3. Stringent promoters share a unique intrinsic property: extreme sensitivity to DNA superhelicity&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A critical discovery was that &lt;strong&gt;all four stringent promoters&lt;/strong&gt; examined share the same unusual behavior: their RNAP initiation complexes are &lt;strong&gt;intrinsically extremely unstable on linear DNA&lt;/strong&gt; (half-lives of mere seconds) but become &lt;strong&gt;substantially stabilized on supercoiled DNA&lt;/strong&gt; (half-lives of tens of minutes). This supercoiling-dependent stabilization had previously been described only for &lt;em&gt;rrnB&lt;/em&gt; P1; this paper generalized it to &lt;em&gt;pyrBI&lt;/em&gt; and the &lt;em&gt;rpoD&lt;/em&gt; P1/P2 promoters for the first time. The authors proposed this is likely a &lt;strong&gt;hallmark feature of all stringent promoters&lt;/strong&gt;, linked to a GC-rich sequence motif called the &lt;strong&gt;discriminator&lt;/strong&gt; (between the −10 element and the +1 transcription start site), which raises the energy barrier for open complex formation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;4. The mutant RNAPs amplify the intrinsic instability of stringent promoter complexes&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The mutant RNAPs essentially make interactions with stringent promoters on supercoiled DNA resemble those of wild-type RNAP on linear DNA — profoundly unstable. The four &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations all cluster in the &lt;strong&gt;rif-region&lt;/strong&gt; of the β subunit, implying this structural region of RNAP is critical for stable interactions specifically with this class of promoters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;5. The mutants activate genes &lt;em&gt;positively&lt;/em&gt; controlled by the stringent response&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Consistent with behaving like &quot;stringent&quot; RNAPs, the mutants were also shown to upregulate &lt;em&gt;himA&lt;/em&gt;, which encodes the α subunit of IHF (Integration Host Factor) — a gene normally requiring the stringent response for full induction. The &lt;em&gt;rpoB114&lt;/em&gt; mutant showed roughly 2-fold higher IHF levels than wild type in the absence of any stringent signal.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Proposed Model&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose a &lt;strong&gt;feedback coupling model&lt;/strong&gt; (Fig. 7 in the paper):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
During &lt;strong&gt;rapid growth&lt;/strong&gt;, high transcription rates at stringent promoters generate negative superhelicity upstream of elongating RNAPs. This increased local supercoiling stabilizes RNAP–promoter initiation complexes, sustaining high transcription in a positive feedback loop.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
During &lt;strong&gt;amino acid starvation&lt;/strong&gt; (the stringent response), transcriptional activity at these promoters falls. This reduces local superhelicity, which destabilizes initiation complexes further, reinforcing the shutdown.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;discriminator sequence&lt;/strong&gt; imposes an intrinsically high energy barrier for open complex formation, making stringent promoters uniquely sensitive to the superhelical state of DNA compared to non-stringent promoters, where the isomerization step is essentially irreversible and stable.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ppGpp likely contributes by directly or indirectly destabilizing initiation complexes, or by reducing overall transcription activity, which feeds back through supercoiling.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The model also accounts for the &lt;strong&gt;dual nature&lt;/strong&gt; of the stringent response (some genes go up, others go down): during rapid growth, most RNAP is sequestered at highly active stringent promoters, limiting free RNAP availability for other genes. When the stringent response fires (or is mimicked by the &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants), RNAP dissociates from stringent promoters, increasing the free RNAP pool and boosting transcription of genes &lt;em&gt;positively&lt;/em&gt; regulated by the stringent response.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Significance&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper made several important contributions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It established that &lt;strong&gt;intrinsic initiation complex instability coupled to DNA superhelicity&lt;/strong&gt; is a general feature of stringent promoters, not just &lt;em&gt;rrnB&lt;/em&gt; P1.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It identified a novel class of &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants that constitutively mimic the stringent response, providing a valuable genetic tool for dissecting the pathway.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It proposed a mechanistic framework in which the &lt;strong&gt;rate-limiting step&lt;/strong&gt; for stringent gene transcription is the stability of the RNAP–promoter complex, and that this stability is dynamically coupled to the transcriptional activity itself via supercoiling — an elegant self-regulatory mechanism.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It offered a unified explanation for how both the negative and positive arms of the stringent response could be coordinated through RNAP redistribution rather than requiring separate dedicated regulatory mechanisms for each gene class.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
