    <bib:Article rdf:about="https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.1919672117">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf>
            <bib:Journal>
                <prism:volume>117</prism:volume>
                <dc:title>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</dc:title>
                <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1073/pnas.1919672117</dc:identifier>
                <prism:number>32</prism:number>
                <dcterms:alternative>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A.</dcterms:alternative>
                <dc:identifier>ISSN 0027-8424, 1091-6490</dc:identifier>
            </bib:Journal>
        </dcterms:isPartOf>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Alexander</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Helen K.</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>MacLean</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>R. Craig</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_513"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_383"/>
        <dc:title>Stochastic bacterial population dynamics restrict the establishment of antibiotic resistance from single cells</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Significance
            The emergence of antibiotic resistance poses a critical threat to the efficacy of antibiotic treatments. A resistant bacterial population must originally arise from a single cell that mutates or acquires a resistance gene. This single cell may, by chance, fail to successfully reproduce before it dies, leading to loss of the nascent resistant lineage. Here, we show that antibiotic concentrations that selectively favor resistance are nonetheless sufficient to reduce the chance of outgrowth from a single cell to a very low probability. Our findings suggest that lower antibiotic concentrations than those required to clear a large resistant population may be sufficient to prevent, with high probability, outgrowth of initially rare resistant mutants.
          , 
            
              A better understanding of how antibiotic exposure impacts the evolution of resistance in bacterial populations is crucial for designing more sustainable treatment strategies. The conventional approach to this question is to measure the range of concentrations over which resistant strain(s) are selectively favored over a sensitive strain. Here, we instead investigate how antibiotic concentration impacts the initial establishment of resistance from single cells, mimicking the clonal expansion of a resistant lineage following mutation or horizontal gene transfer. Using two
              Pseudomonas aeruginosa
              strains carrying resistance plasmids, we show that single resistant cells have &lt;5% probability of detectable outgrowth at antibiotic concentrations as low as one-eighth of the resistant strain’s minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC). This low probability of establishment is due to detrimental effects of antibiotics on resistant cells, coupled with the inherently stochastic nature of cell division and death on the single-cell level, which leads to loss of many nascent resistant lineages. Our findings suggest that moderate doses of antibiotics, well below the MIC of resistant strains, may effectively restrict de novo emergence of resistance even though they cannot clear already-large resistant populations.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2020-08-11</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.1919672117</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-03-24 05:39:32</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>19455-19464</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_513">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Summary: Alexander &amp;amp; MacLean (2020), &lt;em&gt;PNAS&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Background and Motivation&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The conventional framework for thinking about antibiotic resistance evolution centers on the &lt;strong&gt;Mutant Selection Window (MSW)&lt;/strong&gt; — the idea that resistant strains are selectively enriched at antibiotic concentrations between the MIC of the sensitive strain (MIC_S) and the MIC of the resistant strain (MIC_R). This framework is built on population-level competition experiments using large numbers of cells, and it focuses on &lt;em&gt;relative fitness&lt;/em&gt; (which strain grows faster relative to the other).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What this framework ignores is the stochastic, single-cell reality of how resistance actually &lt;em&gt;originates&lt;/em&gt;. Before a resistant population can be selected for, it must first arise from a single mutant cell and successfully expand. That initial expansion is inherently probabilistic — any individual cell can die before dividing, even if it has a growth advantage on average. Alexander &amp;amp; MacLean set out to quantify this &lt;strong&gt;establishment probability&lt;/strong&gt; experimentally.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Experimental System&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors worked with &lt;em&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa&lt;/em&gt; PA01, an important opportunistic pathogen known for rapid resistance evolution. They used two resistant strains:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PA01:Rms149&lt;/strong&gt; — carries a nonconjugative plasmid conferring resistance to &lt;strong&gt;streptomycin&lt;/strong&gt; via the &lt;em&gt;aadA5&lt;/em&gt; gene (adenylation of streptomycin). MIC_R = 2,048 μg/mL.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PA01:PAMBL2&lt;/strong&gt; — carries a multidrug resistance plasmid conferring resistance to &lt;strong&gt;meropenem&lt;/strong&gt; (a clinically critical carbapenem antibiotic) via the &lt;em&gt;bla&lt;/em&gt;_VIM-1 carbapenemase gene. MIC_R = 512 μg/mL.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Core Experimental Design: The &quot;Seeding Experiment&quot;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To isolate the establishment phase, the authors inoculated hundreds of replicate 96-well plate cultures with extremely dilute cultures of the resistant strain, yielding on average &lt;strong&gt;~1–3 cells per well&lt;/strong&gt;. Because of the Poisson distribution of cell counts at such dilutions, many wells receive zero cells, and very few receive more than one or two. After incubation for up to 3 days, wells were scored for detectable growth (OD &amp;gt; 0.1). By fitting a mathematical model accounting for random inoculum sizes and demographic stochasticity, they extracted the &lt;strong&gt;per-cell probability of establishment&lt;/strong&gt; (p̃_c) at each antibiotic concentration.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Major Finding 1: Establishment Probability Collapses Well Below MIC_R&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For both strains, the probability that a single resistant cell successfully establishes a detectable population drops dramatically at antibiotic concentrations far below MIC_R:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At &lt;strong&gt;1/32 × MIC_R&lt;/strong&gt;: no detectable effect on establishment.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At &lt;strong&gt;1/16 × MIC_R&lt;/strong&gt;: p̃_c falls significantly, to ~55–73% of the antibiotic-free value.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At &lt;strong&gt;1/8 × MIC_R&lt;/strong&gt;: p̃_c drops to just &lt;strong&gt;3–5%&lt;/strong&gt; — meaning fewer than 1 in 20 resistant cells successfully establishes a lineage.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At &lt;strong&gt;1/4 × MIC_R&lt;/strong&gt;: essentially zero establishment was observed in the meropenem system.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is striking because 1/8 × MIC_R is a concentration at which the resistant strain still has a clear selective advantage over the sensitive strain — it is well within the Mutant Selection Window. Selection alone is therefore not sufficient to guarantee establishment of a resistant lineage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Major Finding 2: MIC Is Inoculum-Size Dependent — A Purely Stochastic Phenomenon&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors noticed an apparent paradox: how can a strain fail to grow at concentrations far below its MIC? Their explanation was the &lt;strong&gt;inoculum effect&lt;/strong&gt; — MIC values measured at standard inoculation densities (~5 × 10⁵ CFU/mL, corresponding to ~10⁵ cells per 200 μL well) do not reflect what happens with single cells.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They confirmed this by conducting MIC assays across a range of inoculum sizes spanning three orders of magnitude (~10² to 10⁵ CFU/well). MIC increased strongly with inoculum size. Critically, by varying both inoculation &lt;em&gt;density&lt;/em&gt; and culture &lt;em&gt;volume&lt;/em&gt; independently, they showed that &lt;strong&gt;absolute cell number&lt;/strong&gt; (CFU), not cell density (CFU/mL), is the key driver. This rules out density-dependent mechanisms like antibiotic enzymatic inactivation as the explanation here, pointing squarely to &lt;strong&gt;demographic stochasticity&lt;/strong&gt; — with more cells, there are simply more chances for at least one to survive and establish.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors emphasize that MIC should be understood as an &lt;strong&gt;emergent population-level property&lt;/strong&gt;, not an intrinsic single-cell property.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Major Finding 3: Individual Cells Establish Independently&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To test whether cells cooperate or compete during establishment (which would violate the independence assumption), the authors fit a &lt;strong&gt;null model&lt;/strong&gt; in which each cell independently establishes a lineage with a fixed probability p_c, regardless of inoculum size. The probability of culture growth is then:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;p_w(N, x) = 1 − e^(−N·p_c(x))&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This null model provided an excellent fit to their data across all tested inoculum sizes and streptomycin concentrations. This means cells are not &quot;more susceptible&quot; at smaller population sizes — they simply fail to outgrow because fewer independent establishment attempts are available. There is no evidence for cooperative antibiotic breakdown or competitive suppression among resistant cells in this system.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Major Finding 4: Sub-MIC_R Antibiotics Cause Real Toxicity to Resistant Cells&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To understand &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; resistant cells fail to establish, the authors examined cell-level dynamics:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Live-dead staining (flow cytometry)&lt;/strong&gt;: The fraction of membrane-compromised (dead) cells after 7 hours of treatment rose from ~3–4% in antibiotic-free conditions to &lt;strong&gt;&amp;gt;20% at 1/8 × MIC_R&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Viable cell tracking over time&lt;/strong&gt;: At 1/8 and 1/4 × MIC_R, cultures inoculated with ~100 cells showed an &lt;strong&gt;extended lag phase of at least 7–8 hours&lt;/strong&gt;, during which viable cell numbers initially &lt;em&gt;declined&lt;/em&gt;. After 3 days, 25% of cultures at 1/8 × MIC_R eventually grew, while 75% failed to reach detectable density. At 1/4 × MIC_R, almost none (1/60) grew.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This demonstrates that sub-MIC_R concentrations genuinely impair the absolute fitness of resistant cells, increasing death rates and reducing division rates. Combined with the randomness of single-cell birth and death events (demographic stochasticity), this creates a high probability that a nascent resistant lineage is stochastically lost before it can expand.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Major Finding 5: The Sensitive Population Has Complex Effects on Resistance Establishment&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In realistic clinical scenarios, resistance arises within a large, predominantly sensitive bacterial population. The authors therefore ran seeding experiments where a few resistant cells were introduced into either a low-density (~5 × 10⁵ CFU/mL) or high-density (~5 × 10⁷ CFU/mL) sensitive population.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key results:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No antibiotic&lt;/strong&gt;: the sensitive population &lt;em&gt;abolished&lt;/em&gt; establishment of resistant cells at either density, due to competitive suppression (as expected, since resistance carries a fitness cost without antibiotic pressure). The minimum selective concentration (MSC) was between 1/32 and 1/16 × MIC_S.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Above MSC, low-density sensitive population&lt;/strong&gt;: negligible effect on establishment probability of resistant cells.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Above MSC, high-density sensitive population&lt;/strong&gt;: at most concentrations, still little effect — but at &lt;strong&gt;1/8 × MIC_R&lt;/strong&gt;, the high-density sensitive population &lt;em&gt;increased&lt;/em&gt; establishment probability from near zero to ~65%. The authors hypothesize this is because sensitive cells absorb antibiotic from the media (&quot;inoculum protection&quot;), reducing the effective concentration experienced by resistant cells.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This protective effect disappeared at concentrations of 1/2 × MIC_R and above.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The practical implication is that a large sensitive population can somewhat extend the range of concentrations at which resistance can emerge, but does not eliminate the fundamental stochastic bottleneck.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Conceptual and Clinical Implications&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Selection ≠ Establishment&lt;/strong&gt;: A positive selection coefficient (relative fitness advantage) is not sufficient to guarantee that a resistant lineage arising from a single cell will survive. Demographic stochasticity is an independent and important barrier.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sub-MIC_R dosing may be clinically relevant&lt;/strong&gt;: If MIC_R lies above the maximum safely achievable dose, concentrations well below MIC_R may still prevent de novo emergence of resistance with high probability, even though they cannot clear an already-large resistant population.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rethinking the MSW&lt;/strong&gt;: The MSW framework, which defines resistance emergence risk by relative fitness thresholds, needs to be supplemented with stochastic establishment probability, which is governed by absolute fitness at the single-cell level.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mutation rate alone is insufficient to assess resistance risk&lt;/strong&gt;: The probability of resistance emergence depends not just on mutation rates but on the probability that resistant mutants escape extinction when rare — and this is strongly shaped by antibiotic concentration.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Broader applications&lt;/strong&gt;: The statistical framework developed (relating inoculum size to population growth probability via a simple exponential model) has applications beyond antibiotic resistance — to pathogen transmission, food safety microbiology, invasive bacterial infections, and cancer metastasis.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Limitations and Open Questions&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors acknowledge several unresolved issues: why some cells establish while others don't (possible roles of phenotypic heterogeneity, metabolic state, or resistance gene expression levels); the effect of time-varying antibiotic concentrations that reflect real pharmacokinetics; and a fuller characterization of how sensitive population density, timing of treatment, and antibiotic dose interact over time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Bottom Line&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper makes the important and experimentally well-supported argument that &lt;strong&gt;antibiotic concentrations well below the MIC of a resistant strain can effectively prevent de novo emergence of resistance from single cells&lt;/strong&gt;, because of the combined effect of antibiotic toxicity on resistant cells and the inherent randomness of single-cell birth-death processes. The establishment probability of a resistant cell can fall to below 5% at 1/8 × MIC_R — a concentration at which the resistant strain is still selectively favored. This reframes how we should think about dosing strategies, suggesting that lower doses than conventionally thought may be sufficient to suppress the very earliest stages of resistance evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
