        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_404"/>
        <dc:title>Structural basis for rifamycin resistance of bacterial RNA polymerase by the three most clinically important RpoB mutations found in &lt;i&gt;Mycobacterium tuberculosis&lt;/i&gt;</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Since 1967, Rifamaycin (RIF) has been used as a first line antibiotic treatment for tuberculosis (TB), and it remains the cornerstone of current short-term TB treatment. Increased occurrence of RIF-resistant (RIFR) TB, ~41% of which results from the RpoB S531L mutation in RNA polymerase (RNAP), has become a growing problem worldwide. In this study, we determined the X-ray crystal structures of the EscherichiacoliRNAPs containing the most clinically important S531L mutation and two other frequently observed RIFR mutants, RpoB D516V and RpoB H526Y. The structures reveal that the S531L mutation imparts subtle if any structural or functional impact on RNAP in the absence of RIF. However, upon Rifampin (RMP) binding, the S531L mutant exhibits a disordering of the RIF binding interface, which effectively reduces the RIF affinity. In contrast, the H526Y mutation reshapes the RIF binding pocket, generating significant steric conflicts that essentially prevent RIF binding. While the D516V mutant does not exhibit any such gross structural changes, certainly the electrostatic surface of the RIF binding pocket is dramatically changed, likely resulting in the decreased affinity for RIF. Analysis of interactions of RMP with three common RIFR mutant RNAPs suggests that modifications to RMP may recover its efficacy against RIFR TB.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>03/2017</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/mmi.13606</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-03-27 04:35:01</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/termsAndConditions#vor</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>1034-1045</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_424">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Summary: Structural Basis for Rifamycin Resistance in &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; RNA Polymerase&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Background and Clinical Significance&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Tuberculosis remains one of the deadliest infectious diseases globally, with an estimated 10.4 million new cases and 1.8 million deaths reported in 2015 alone. Since the 1960s, Rifamycin (RIF) and its semisynthetic derivative Rifampin (RMP) have been the cornerstone of TB treatment, prized for their efficacy even against latent TB (&quot;sterilizing activity&quot;). RIF works by binding to the β subunit (RpoB) of bacterial RNA polymerase (RNAP), physically blocking RNA chain extension beyond 2–3 nucleotides.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The growing problem, however, is RIF-resistant (RIF^R) TB. Resistance arises from spontaneous mutations (at a frequency of ~10⁻⁸) within four conserved regions of RpoB called the &lt;strong&gt;RIF Resistance-Determining Regions (RRDRs)&lt;/strong&gt;. While 90 different resistance-conferring mutations have been identified across 33 codons, just three account for roughly &lt;strong&gt;85% of all clinical cases&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;S531L&lt;/strong&gt; (~41% of cases)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;H526Y&lt;/strong&gt; (~36% of cases)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;D516V&lt;/strong&gt; (~9% of cases)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite years of biochemical study, the precise molecular mechanisms by which each mutation confers resistance were poorly understood prior to this work. Previous models relied on &lt;em&gt;in silico&lt;/em&gt; substitutions into wild-type (WT) structures rather than direct experimental determination of mutant structures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Goals of the Study&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors set out to:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Crystallize &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; RNAPs carrying each of the three clinically important mutations (S531L, H526Y, D516V)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Solve X-ray crystal structures of these mutant RNAPs both alone and in complex with RMP
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Determine the precise structural and electrostatic changes responsible for resistance in each case
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use the structural data to guide future drug design
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; RNAP was used as a model system because the MTB RNAP has not been amenable to crystallization, but the two systems share highly conserved RRDR sequences and produce similar resistance phenotypes — validated by parallel IC₅₀ measurements in both organisms.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Experimental Validation of the Model System&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before structural work, the team confirmed &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; RNAP was a valid proxy for MTB RNAP by comparing IC₅₀ values for RMP across both systems (Table 1):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; IC₅₀&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;MTB IC₅₀&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;WT&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;lt;0.005 µM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;lt;0.005 µM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;D516V/D435V&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;398 µM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;880 µM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;S531L/S450L&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;263 µM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;789 µM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;H526Y/H445Y&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;≥2 mM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;No detectable inhibition&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Critically, both organisms showed the same &lt;strong&gt;two-tier pattern&lt;/strong&gt;: D516V and S531L mutations reduce but don't eliminate RIF sensitivity, while H526Y renders the enzyme essentially insensitive. This parallel behavior validated the &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; system for mechanistic studies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A notable technical point: the S531L mutation is rare in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; clinical isolates because it requires &lt;em&gt;two&lt;/em&gt; nucleotide changes in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; rpoB (TCT→TTG/TTA), whereas in MTB it requires only &lt;em&gt;one&lt;/em&gt; (TCG→TTG). The authors introduced it artificially to study the clinically prevalent MTB mutation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Structural Findings&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. S531L Mutation — A Bipartite Resistance Mechanism&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the WT RNAP, serine at position 531 sits deep in the RIF binding pocket and forms a &lt;strong&gt;direct hydrogen bond&lt;/strong&gt; with the phenol on C8 (O2 atom) of RMP's naphthalene ring. The top of the naphthalene ring is also covered by &lt;strong&gt;fork loop 2&lt;/strong&gt; (residues 534–541), which contributes significant van der Waals contacts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The S531L structure revealed a surprising, two-part resistance mechanism:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;In the absence of RMP&lt;/strong&gt;, the S531L mutation causes no major structural change — fork loop 2 remains ordered and the binding pocket is largely intact. This explains why the enzyme retains normal transcriptional activity.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Upon RMP binding&lt;/strong&gt;, the bulkier leucine side chain at position 531 creates a steric clash with RMP that physically &lt;strong&gt;pushes fork loop 2 away&lt;/strong&gt;, causing it to become disordered. This disordering exposes approximately half of RMP's naphthalene ring to solvent, reducing the RNAP–RMP contact area by ~40% (from 386 Å² in WT to 244 Å² in the mutant).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The combined effect is a large loss of van der Waals interactions and the elimination of the key hydrogen bond at C8, together dramatically reducing RMP affinity. This &quot;induced disordering&quot; mechanism was entirely unpredicted by prior modeling studies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. H526Y Mutation — Steric Blockade of the Binding Pocket&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the WT enzyme, histidine-526 forms part of the back wall of the RIF binding cavity and may hydrogen bond with oxygen atoms of the ansa bridge and the Q513 side chain.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The H526Y crystal structure revealed &lt;strong&gt;large-scale rearrangements&lt;/strong&gt; of the main chain in two regions of the RRDR (residues 512–520 and 756–766). These rearrangements fundamentally &lt;strong&gt;reshape the RIF binding pocket&lt;/strong&gt; itself.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When RMP from the WT complex was modeled into the H526Y pocket, it showed severe steric clashes with the rigid hydrophobic ansa bridge of RMP, particularly at the C19 position of the ansa bridge. The binding pocket is physically incompatible with RMP's structure, making the enzyme &lt;strong&gt;essentially insensitive&lt;/strong&gt; to RIF (IC₅₀ ≥2 mM). Confirming this, soaking H526Y crystals with even 2 mM RMP produced no electron density for RMP in the binding pocket, indicating no specific binding occurred even at this extreme concentration.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. D516V Mutation — Electrostatic Disruption&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the WT enzyme, aspartate-516 contributes to the sidewall of the RIF binding pocket. It helps position RMP to form two critical hydrogen bonds between the F514 backbone and the RMP ansa bridge (at O9/C23 and O8/C35). It also contributes van der Waals contacts with the ansa bridge around C20 and, importantly, provides charge neutralization of basic residues R529 and R687 in the binding site — which is necessary to facilitate binding of the relatively nonpolar RMP molecule.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Structurally, D516V shows &lt;strong&gt;no significant change&lt;/strong&gt; in the overall shape of the RRDR or the binding pocket, distinguishing it from both other mutations. The D516V–RMP complex structure is also globally similar to the WT complex.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The key difference is purely &lt;strong&gt;electrostatic&lt;/strong&gt;: replacing the negatively charged aspartate with a neutral valine makes the RIF binding pocket more electropositive overall (due to uncompensated R529 and R687). This creates a less favorable electrostatic environment for binding the apolar RMP molecule, reducing affinity without physically blocking access or disrupting binding pocket geometry.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Implications for Drug Design&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The three mutations represent three fundamentally different resistance mechanisms, and the authors propose distinct strategies to overcome each:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Against S531L&lt;/strong&gt;: Since the overall binding pocket structure is largely preserved (except for fork loop 2 disordering), adding extra contact groups to the C3/C4 tail of the RIF scaffold (e.g., as in benzoxazinorifamycins) could compensate for the lost contacts — particularly interactions with the sigma finger that are independent of fork loop 2. Alternatively, reducing steric bulk around the C7 methyl of the naphthalene ring might lessen the clash with L531.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Against H526Y&lt;/strong&gt;: Because the ansa bridge binding region is fundamentally reshaped, major modifications to the RIF ansa bridge backbone itself would be required. This is chemically challenging given the semi-synthetic nature of RMP, but recent advances in biosynthetic engineering of the RIF polyketide backbone offer a potential route.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Against D516V&lt;/strong&gt;: Modifications that improve the electrostatic complementarity of RIF with a more electropositive binding pocket, or that strengthen contacts independent of D516, could restore efficacy.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors also note that acquiring secondary resistance mutations in the RRDR to defeat modified RIF derivatives would likely impose additional &lt;strong&gt;fitness costs&lt;/strong&gt; on the bacterium (reduced transcriptional fidelity), potentially rendering those further-mutated strains non-viable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Broader Significance&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper is the &lt;strong&gt;first direct structural characterization&lt;/strong&gt; of the three most clinically prevalent RIF-resistant RNAP mutants. It overturns several predictions from prior modeling work (particularly for S531L and H526Y), demonstrating that structure determination of the actual mutant enzymes is essential — modeling from WT structures is insufficient.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Beyond TB, the authors note that RIF derivatives are now used clinically against &lt;em&gt;M. leprae&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;M. avium-intracellulare&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Chlamydophila pneumoniae&lt;/em&gt;, and even &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;-associated traveler's diarrhea (Rifaximin), making this structural framework broadly applicable to emerging resistance across multiple indications.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Does the Paper Explain Why These Mutations Are Most Common?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The short answer is &lt;strong&gt;only partially&lt;/strong&gt;, and somewhat indirectly. The paper's primary focus is the structural basis of resistance rather than the evolutionary or epidemiological question of mutation prevalence. Here's what it does and doesn't address:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What the Paper Does Address&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Codon usage as a driver of mutation frequency (for S531L specifically)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the clearest mechanistic explanation offered. The paper notes that S531L is the dominant resistance mutation in MTB but is essentially &lt;em&gt;absent&lt;/em&gt; in naturally occurring &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; resistance. The reason is purely genetic:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In MTB, serine at position 531 is encoded by &lt;strong&gt;TCG&lt;/strong&gt; — so a single nucleotide change (TCG→TTG) produces the leucine substitution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, serine is encoded by &lt;strong&gt;TCT&lt;/strong&gt; — requiring &lt;em&gt;two&lt;/em&gt; nucleotide changes to reach leucine (TCT→TTG or TTA)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, the serine→&lt;strong&gt;phenylalanine&lt;/strong&gt; substitution is instead most common because it only requires one change (TCT→TTT)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a compelling explanation for why S531L specifically dominates in MTB — it is simply the most &lt;em&gt;mutationally accessible&lt;/em&gt; resistance-conferring substitution given MTB's codon usage. However, the paper only makes this argument for S531L, not for H526Y or D516V.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fitness costs as an indirect filter&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the Discussion, the paper briefly touches on why not just &lt;em&gt;any&lt;/em&gt; RNAP mutation becomes clinically prevalent. It cites evidence that additional mutations in the RRDR — beyond the established three — tend to compromise transcriptional integrity and impose &lt;strong&gt;fitness costs&lt;/strong&gt; on the bacterium, potentially rendering further-mutated strains nonviable. The implication is that the three common mutations represent a kind of &quot;sweet spot&quot;: they confer strong resistance while preserving sufficient RNAP function for bacterial survival. However, the paper doesn't systematically compare the fitness costs of these three mutations against the many rarer ones to make this argument rigorously.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
