        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_425"/>
        <dc:title>Rifampin Resistance Mutations Are Associated with Broad Chemical Remodeling of Mycobacterium tuberculosis</dc:title>
        <dc:date>07/2016</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0021925820367685</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-03-30 05:35:26</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>14248-14256</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:00219258">
        <prism:volume>291</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Journal of Biological Chemistry</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1074/jbc.M116.716704</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>27</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Journal of Biological Chemistry</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 00219258</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_436">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Summary: &quot;Rifampin Resistance Mutations Are Associated with Broad Chemical Remodeling of &lt;em&gt;Mycobacterium tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;&quot; (Lahiri et al., 2016)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Background &amp;amp; Question&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While it is well established that &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations confer rifampin resistance by altering the β subunit of RNA polymerase, the downstream biochemical consequences of having a permanently altered transcriptional apparatus are poorly understood. Since RNAP controls gene expression broadly, &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations could in principle affect the production of almost any cellular component. This paper asks: what happens to the cell wall lipid composition of &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; after acquisition of rifampin resistance? The authors used an unbiased, organism-wide lipidomic profiling approach to answer this question systematically.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Approach&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors used normal-phase HPLC coupled to high-accuracy mass spectrometry (quadrupole time-of-flight MS) to profile approximately 10,000 cell wall lipid species simultaneously in a panel of isogenic &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants. Three clinically relevant resistance mutations — S531L, H526Y, and Q513E — were introduced into two genetically distinct &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; strain backgrounds (CDC1551 and W-Beijing), producing six mutant-parent pairs. This design allowed the authors to distinguish lipid changes that are characteristic of rifampin resistance in general from those specific to a particular mutation or genetic background.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main Findings&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. rpoB mutations trigger broad, organism-wide lipid remodeling.&lt;/strong&gt; Each resistance mutant showed quantitative alterations (more than 2-fold, p &amp;lt; 0.05) in 2.7–6.2% of all detected lipid species — hundreds of individual lipid events per strain. This scale of change is comparable to the total lipid differences observed between two separately evolved &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; lineages (H37Rv and W-Beijing), suggesting that mutation of a single gene encoding RNAP causes a degree of biochemical remodeling typically associated with lineage-level evolutionary divergence. Background variance from normal bacterial culture conditions was only ~0.7%, confirming the changes were biologically meaningful rather than technical noise.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. The lipid changes are stereotyped and reproducible across mutation sites and strain backgrounds.&lt;/strong&gt; A meta-analysis identified lipid events consistently changed across all three mutation sites in both genetic backgrounds. Despite different resistance-conferring amino acid substitutions at positions 513, 526, and 531, the pattern of lipid change was qualitatively similar in all six mutants, pointing to a shared physiological response to RNAP dysfunction rather than mutation-specific idiosyncrasies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Mycobactin and carboxymycobactin siderophores are markedly reduced in all rifampin-resistant strains.&lt;/strong&gt; Mycobactins and carboxymycobactins are iron-chelating siderophores that are essential for &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; growth within macrophages. Both were consistently and substantially reduced in all six resistance mutants, detected as near-complete loss of the relevant mass spectrometry signals. This is striking because the bacteria were grown in iron-replete medium, where siderophore production is normally already suppressed via the IdeR iron-sensing regulator. The authors suggest that &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations somehow interfere with iron sensing or interact with the IdeR regulatory pathway in a previously unrecognised way, causing inappropriate downregulation of the iron uptake system.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Acylated sulfoglycolipids (Ac2SGL) are reduced across rifampin-resistant mutants.&lt;/strong&gt; Diacylated sulfoglycolipids are abundant cell surface polyketides with known roles in virulence and in stimulating host immune responses via CD1-restricted T cells. Sulfoglycolipid signals were reduced in all six mutants, reaching statistical significance in five of six cases. This is notable because sulfoglycolipids localise to the outer cell wall and influence both host–pathogen interactions and cell wall permeability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. PDIM changes are less consistent and not a core feature of the rifampin resistance response.&lt;/strong&gt; Phthiocerol dimycocerosate (PDIM) signals were increased in three W-Beijing mutants, consistent with a previous proteomic report, but this pattern was not observed consistently in CDC1551 mutants. The authors conclude that PDIM changes are not as broadly penetrant as the siderophore and sulfoglycolipid changes identified here.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Broader Implications&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The discovery that rifampin resistance is accompanied by reproducible, characteristic changes in specific lipid classes — regardless of which &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; residue is mutated or which strain background is used — has several important implications. First, reduced mycobactin levels could impair iron acquisition in iron-limited host environments (such as within macrophages), potentially contributing to the fitness costs of resistance that are well documented &lt;em&gt;in vivo&lt;/em&gt;. This also provides a novel mechanistic link between &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutation and iron metabolism that had not previously been identified. Second, reduced sulfoglycolipid levels could alter host immune recognition and cell wall permeability in rifampin-resistant strains, with potential implications for pathogenesis and cross-resistance to other drugs. Third, the characteristic and reproducible nature of these lipid changes opens the possibility of using them as biochemical biomarkers of the drug-resistant state — potentially allowing detection of rifampin resistance through lipid profiling rather than genetic sequencing or culture-based methods. Finally, the study validates organism-wide lipidomic analysis as a powerful discovery tool for understanding the physiological consequences of antibiotic resistance mutations in mycobacteria.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;rpoB mutations likely affects all downstream cellular effects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper mostly looked at RIF’s effect on TB lipid&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
