        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_429"/>
        <dc:title>Fitness costs of rifampicin resistance in &lt;span style=&quot;font-variant:small-caps;&quot;&gt; &lt;i&gt;M&lt;/i&gt; &lt;/span&gt; &lt;i&gt;ycobacterium tuberculosis&lt;/i&gt; are amplified under conditions of nutrient starvation and compensated by mutation in the β′ subunit of &lt;span style=&quot;font-variant:small-caps;&quot;&gt;RNA&lt;/span&gt; polymerase</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Rifampicin resistance, a defining attribute of multidrug resistant tuberculosis, is conferred by mutations in the β subunit of RNA polymerase. Sequencing of rifampicin resistant (RIF-R) clinical isolates of Mycobacterium tuberculosis revealed, in addition to RIF-R mutations, enrichment of potential compensatory mutations around the double-psi β-barrel domain of the β’ subunit comprising the catalytic site and the exit tunnel for newly synthesized RNA. Sequential introduction of the resistance allele followed by the compensatory allele in isogenic M. smegmatis showed that these mutations respectively caused and compensated a starvation enhanced growth defect by altering RNA polymerase activity. While specific combinations of resistance and compensatory alleles converged in divergent lineages, other combinations recurred among related isolates suggesting transmission of compensated RIF-R strains. These findings suggest nutrient poor growth conditions impose larger selective pressure on RIF-R organisms that results in the selection of compensatory mutations in a domain involved in catalysis and starvation control of RNA polymerase transcription.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>03/2014</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Fitness costs of rifampicin resistance in &lt;span style=&quot;font-variant</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/mmi.12520</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-03-30 05:38:41</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/termsAndConditions#vor</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>1106-1119</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_503">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Summary: &quot;Mutation Bias Shapes the Spectrum of Adaptive Substitutions&quot; (Cano et al., 2022)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Background &amp;amp; Question&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A longstanding assumption in evolutionary biology holds that selection alone drives adaptation. This paper asks a more nuanced question: how strongly do mutational biases — the tendency for some types of DNA changes to occur more frequently than others — shape which mutations actually fix during adaptation?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Approach&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors developed a codon-based statistical model using negative binomial regression, applied to three large datasets of adaptive amino acid changes in &lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;. The key statistic is β (the coefficient of mutational influence), where β = 0 means mutation has no influence and β = 1 means a fully proportional influence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main Findings&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Mutation bias has a proportional influence on adaptation across all three species.&lt;/strong&gt; In every case, β was close to 1 and statistically significant, meaning more mutable types of changes are proportionally more likely to contribute to adaptive evolution — even though all fixed mutations were beneficial and subject to selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Empirical mutation spectra outperform random ones.&lt;/strong&gt; When tested against a million randomly generated mutation spectra, the real experimentally-determined spectra explained the observed adaptive substitutions better in the vast majority of cases (randomized spectra outperformed empirical ones only 0.2–4.2% of the time depending on species).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. The mutation spectrum can be reverse-engineered from adaptive data.&lt;/strong&gt; By treating mutation rates as free parameters and fitting them to the adaptive substitution data, the authors accurately recovered the known species-specific mutation spectra (Pearson correlations of 0.84–0.96), confirming the deep connection between mutational and adaptive spectra.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Predictive power varies substantially between species.&lt;/strong&gt; The correlation between predicted and observed adaptive substitutions dropped from 0.68 (&lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt;) → 0.41 (&lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;) → 0.16 (&lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;). This variation is explained by two key factors explored through population simulations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mutational supply (Nμ):&lt;/strong&gt; As population size × mutation rate increases, clonal interference intensifies, and selection — not mutation — becomes the dominant determinant of which beneficial allele fixes. This pushes β toward zero.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Diversity of beneficial mutations (mutational target size):&lt;/strong&gt; When more diverse types of changes can be beneficial, mutation bias has broader leverage and predictive power improves. &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; antibiotic resistance is constrained to very few target sites (e.g., &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; for rifampicin resistance), explaining its low predictability.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Results are robust to data contamination.&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; datasets likely contain some non-adaptive &quot;hitchhiker&quot; mutations (~24% and ~13% respectively). Simulations showed contamination levels of ~65% and ~44% would be needed to eliminate significance — far above estimated levels.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Broader Implications&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The findings challenge the classical view (from Fisher, Haldane) that mutation is too weak a force to direct adaptation. Instead, the paper supports a theoretical framework in which mutation acts not just as a frequency-shifter but as a source of genetic novelty, meaning biases in which variants are &lt;em&gt;introduced&lt;/em&gt; systematically influence which ones fix. The authors draw parallels to cancer biology, where driver mutations reflect underlying mutational processes (e.g., UV exposure, APOBEC activity), and argue their results provide a population-genetic foundation for long-standing claims in evo-devo about how &quot;internal&quot; variational biases constrain or direct evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;You said: summarise this paper and present me with the main findings&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;/api/76734bc1-4836-43de-b4e8-0e2ebcb6bc66/files/ffb42fc2-68ea-482b-b048-890f1b84db8e/thumbnail&quot; alt=&quot;Song et al. - 2014 - Fitness costs of rifampicin resistance in  M  .pdf&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;pdf&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;summarise this paper and present me with the main findings&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;11:34 am&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Claude responded: Summary: &quot;Fitness costs of rifampicin-resistance in M.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Identified paper request and prepared comprehensive summary synthesis&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Identified paper request and prepared comprehensive summary synthesis&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Summary: &quot;Fitness costs of rifampicin-resistance in &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; are amplified under conditions of nutrient starvation and compensated by mutation in the β' subunit of RNA polymerase&quot; (Song et al., 2014)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Background &amp;amp; Question&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rifampicin (RIF) is the cornerstone of TB chemotherapy, and resistance to it — conferred by mutations in &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; (encoding the β subunit of RNA polymerase, RNAP) — is a defining feature of MDR-TB. Resistance mutations often impose a fitness cost on the bacterium, but compensatory mutations can arise to restore fitness without reverting the resistance allele, potentially locking in resistance and enabling transmission. This paper asks: what are the true fitness costs of RIF resistance, where do compensatory mutations arise, and what are their functional consequences?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main Findings&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. RpoC is preferentially mutated in drug-resistant clinical isolates.&lt;/strong&gt; Whole-genome sequencing of 33 South Korean clinical isolates (15 drug-sensitive, 18 drug-resistant) revealed that, beyond known resistance genes (e.g., &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;gyrA&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;katG&lt;/em&gt;), the β' subunit of RNAP encoded by &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; was significantly enriched for mutations in drug-resistant strains. This made it a strong candidate for harboring compensatory mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Secondary rpoC mutations are strongly associated with specific rpoB resistance alleles.&lt;/strong&gt; Across a combined dataset of 76 isolates, 11 of 31 RIF-resistant strains (carrying &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations) also carried &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; mutations, compared to only 5 of 36 drug-sensitive strains (p = 0.048). The association was especially pronounced for the &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; S531L allele — the most clinically common RIF-resistance mutation — where 54% of strains carrying S531L also had an &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; mutation, versus only 10% of strains with other resistance alleles. Sequencing of a further 170 RIF-resistant isolates confirmed that secondary mutations in &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; (outside the resistance-determining region), or &lt;em&gt;rpoA&lt;/em&gt; were present in nearly half (49.4%) of all strains.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Both convergent evolution and transmission drive the spread of compensatory mutations.&lt;/strong&gt; Using MIRU-VNTR phylogenetic typing, the authors showed that the same compensatory allele combination (&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; S531L + &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; F452L) had spread through 13 nearly clonally identical strains, consistent with nosocomial transmission from a single source. In contrast, &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; V483G appeared independently in four phylogenetically unrelated strains carrying S531L, indicating convergent evolution of the same compensatory solution. This dual mechanism — transmission of already-compensated strains &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; independent convergent acquisition — helps explain the high frequency of &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; mutations in circulating RIF-resistant populations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Fitness costs of rpoB S531L are strongly amplified under nutrient starvation.&lt;/strong&gt; Using isogenic &lt;em&gt;M. smegmatis&lt;/em&gt; strains engineered to express only &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis rpoB&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; alleles, the authors found that in rich media, the S531L mutation caused only a modest ~13% reduction in growth rate — consistent with prior literature suggesting small fitness costs. However, under nutrient-limited conditions (reduced carbon source, iron, and biotin starvation), the growth defect grew to nearly twofold. This is highly relevant to the &lt;em&gt;in vivo&lt;/em&gt; situation, where TB bacilli in human hosts frequently encounter nutrient-poor, hypoxic microenvironments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Compensatory rpoC mutations restore both growth and RNA polymerase transcriptional activity.&lt;/strong&gt; Both &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; F452L (the transmitted allele) and &lt;em&gt;rpoC&lt;/em&gt; V483G (the convergent allele) partially or fully rescued the growth defect under all nutrient conditions tested, including the most stringent. Transcription efficiency assays confirmed the mechanistic basis: S531L reduced RNAP transcription efficiency to ~50% of wild type, while F452L restored it to ~65% and V483G to ~83%.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6. The structural mechanism involves the RNA exit tunnel.&lt;/strong&gt; The compensatory mutations cluster around a three-α-helix loop in the double-psi β-barrel (DPBB) domain of the β' subunit, which forms the RNA exit tunnel at the β/β' interface. The S531L substitution (a slightly bulkier residue) is proposed to impede RNA movement through this tunnel, reducing transcription efficiency. Compensatory mutations in the β' subunit on the opposite side of the tunnel may slightly widen it, restoring RNA flow. Notably, the DPBB domain is also the site of ppGpp binding in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; — a key signal for the stringent response under starvation — providing a plausible mechanistic link between nutrient limitation and the magnitude of the fitness defect.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Broader Implications&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The finding that fitness costs are dramatically amplified under starvation conditions reframes the threat posed by RIF-resistant TB. Standard &lt;em&gt;in vitro&lt;/em&gt; assays in rich media substantially underestimate the selective disadvantage faced by resistance mutants in the host, and by extension also underestimate the selective pressure driving compensatory evolution. The rapid fixation of compensatory alleles in the clinical population is now explained: conditions inside the human host are far more stringent than laboratory conditions. The paper also shows that transmission of fully fitness-compensated, RIF-resistant strains — rather than independent acquisition — is a significant driver of MDR-TB spread, with important implications for infection control and treatment design.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
