        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_433"/>
        <dc:title>Stochastic establishment of β-lactam-resistant &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt; mutants reveals conditions for collective resistance</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>For antibiotic resistance to arise, new resistant mutants must establish in a bacterial population before they can spread via natural selection. Comprehending the stochastic factors that influence mutant establishment is crucial for a quantitative understanding of antibiotic resistance emergence. Here, we quantify the single-cell establishment probability of four
              Escherichia coli
              strains expressing β-lactamase alleles with different activity against the antibiotic cefotaxime, as a function of antibiotic concentration in both unstructured (liquid) and structured (agar) environments. We show that concentrations well below the minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) can substantially hamper establishment, particularly for highly resistant mutants. While the pattern of establishment suppression is comparable in both tested environments, we find greater variability in establishment probability on agar. Using a simple branching model, we investigate possible sources of this stochasticity, including environment-dependent lineage variability, but cannot reject other possible causes. Lastly, we use the single-cell establishment probability to predict each strain's MIC in the absence of social interactions. We observe substantially higher measured than predicted MIC values, particularly for highly resistant strains, which indicates cooperative effects among resistant cells at large cell numbers, such as in standard MIC assays.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-05-11</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2021.2486</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-01 05:57:55</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>20212486</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_439">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Summary: Saebelfeld et al. (2022)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Background &amp;amp; Question&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When a new antibiotic-resistant mutant arises in a bacterial population, it starts as a single cell and can easily be lost by chance before natural selection can act on it. This paper asks: how likely is a single resistant &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; cell to successfully establish a population under antibiotic pressure, and what factors influence this?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Experimental Setup&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors used four &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; strains carrying different TEM β-lactamase alleles — an ancestor (low resistance) and single, double, and triple mutants with progressively higher resistance to the antibiotic cefotaxime (CTX). They measured single-cell establishment probability across a range of CTX concentrations in both liquid (unstructured) and agar (structured) environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main Findings&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Establishment fails well below the MIC.&lt;/strong&gt; A single cell's probability of successfully establishing a population drops to near zero at concentrations substantially below the strain's minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC). This effect is &lt;em&gt;more pronounced&lt;/em&gt; for highly resistant strains, which is somewhat counterintuitive.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cells don't interact at low densities in liquid.&lt;/strong&gt; Testing two different inoculum sizes (~1 vs ~3 cells) produced nearly identical establishment probability estimates, confirming that cooperative β-lactamase activity (antibiotic breakdown) plays no meaningful role when cell numbers are very low.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Agar shows higher stochasticity.&lt;/strong&gt; While the overall pattern of establishment was similar on agar and in liquid, the decline in establishment probability with increasing antibiotic concentration was shallower on agar, meaning outcomes were more unpredictable. The authors quantified this using Shannon entropy. The cause could not be definitively resolved, but variability in division probability among cell lineages is one candidate explanation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Standard MIC assays overestimate true single-cell resistance.&lt;/strong&gt; Because MIC assays use large inocula (~10⁵ cells/ml), cells cooperatively break down the antibiotic, artificially boosting the apparent MIC — especially for the more resistant strains. The authors call this &lt;strong&gt;collective resistance&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A New Measure of Resistance&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To address this, the authors propose &lt;strong&gt;MIC_q(N)&lt;/strong&gt; — a resistance metric that accounts for inoculum size and stochasticity. Using a null model assuming independent cell fates, they show that the measured MICs of the mutant strains far exceed what would be expected without social interactions, confirming strong cooperative effects at high cell densities.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Broader Significance&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The study highlights that standard MIC assays can be misleading because they conflate individual-cell resistance with collective resistance arising from enzyme-mediated antibiotic degradation. This has implications for how we understand resistance emergence and how antibiotic dosing should be determined clinically.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
