        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0099-2240, 1098-5336</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_445">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-citation-items=&quot;%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FU7I5Q55L%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FU7I5Q55L%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22article-journal%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22RNA%20polymerase%20(RNAP)%20is%20a%20highly%20conserved%20macromolecular%20machine%20that%20contributes%20to%20the%20%EF%AC%82ow%20of%20genetic%20information%20from%20genotype%20to%20phenotype.%20In%20Bacillus%20subtilis%2C%20mutations%20in%20the%20rpoB%20gene%20encoding%20the%20b-subunit%20of%20RNAP%20have%20been%20shown%20to%20alter%20a%20number%20of%20global%20phenotypes%2C%20including%20growth%2C%20utilization%20of%20unusual%20nutrient%20sources%2C%20sporulation%2C%20germination%2C%20and%20production%20of%20secondary%20metabolites.%20In%20addition%2C%20the%20spectrum%20of%20mutations%20in%20rpoB%20leading%20to%20rifampin%20resistance%20(Rifr)%20can%20change%20dramatically%20depending%20upon%20the%20environment%20to%20which%20B.%20subtilis%20cells%20or%20spores%20are%20exposed.%20Rifr%20rpoB%20mutations%20have%20historically%20been%20associated%20with%20slower%20growth%20and%20reduced%20%EF%AC%81tness%3B%20however%2C%20these%20assessments%20of%20%EF%AC%81tness%20were%20conducted%20on%20limited%20collections%20of%20mutants%20in%20rich%20laboratory%20media%20that%20poorly%20re%EF%AC%82ect%20natural%20environments%20typically%20inhabited%20by%20B.%20subtilis.%20Using%20a%20novel%20deep-sequencing%20approach%20in%20addition%20to%20traditional%20measurements%20of%20growth%20rate%2C%20lag%20time%2C%20and%20pairwise%20competitions%2C%20we%20demonstrated%20that%20the%20competitive%20advantages%20of%20speci%EF%AC%81c%20rpoB%20alleles%20differ%20depending%20on%20the%20growth%20environment%20in%20which%20they%20are%20determined.%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22Applied%20and%20Environmental%20Microbiology%22%2C%22DOI%22%3A%2210.1128%2Faem.02422-21%22%2C%22ISSN%22%3A%220099-2240%2C%201098-5336%22%2C%22issue%22%3A%225%22%2C%22journalAbbreviation%22%3A%22Appl%20Environ%20Microbiol%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en%22%2C%22page%22%3A%22e02422-21%22%2C%22source%22%3A%22DOI.org%20(Crossref)%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Environmental%20Dependence%20of%20Competitive%20Fitness%20in%20Rifampin-Resistant%20%3Ci%3ErpoB%3C%2Fi%3E%20Mutants%20of%20Bacillus%20subtilis%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fjournals.asm.org%2Fdoi%2F10.1128%2Faem.02422-21%22%2C%22volume%22%3A%2288%22%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Leehan%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Joss%20D.%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Nicholson%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Wayne%20L.%22%7D%5D%2C%22editor%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Kivisaar%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Maia%22%7D%5D%2C%22accessed%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222026%22%2C4%2C7%5D%5D%7D%2C%22issued%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222022%22%2C3%2C8%5D%5D%7D%7D%7D%5D&quot; data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Not all sites in cluster I of rpoB are prone to mutation at equal frequency; some bases mutate at higher frequency (“hot spots”) or lower frequency (“cold spots”). Such patterns of mutation in a gene are referred to as its mutational spectrum” &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FU7I5Q55L%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%222%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Leehan and Nicholson, 2022, p. 2&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Summary: Leehan &amp;amp; Nicholson (2022)&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Background &amp;amp; Motivation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;RNA polymerase (RNAP) is central to bacterial gene expression, and mutations in its β-subunit gene (&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;) confer resistance to the antibiotic rifampin (Rif). Historically, these mutations were assumed to carry a fitness cost — meaning resistant bacteria grow more slowly and compete poorly against wild-type strains. However, prior assessments were largely conducted in rich laboratory media that don't reflect the natural environments &lt;em&gt;Bacillus subtilis&lt;/em&gt; actually inhabits (soils, plant roots). This study set out to rigorously test whether fitness costs are truly universal, or whether they depend on the growth environment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Key Questions&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Do different &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutant alleles differ in competitive fitness?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Does the growth environment (rich vs. minimal medium) change which alleles are most fit?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Can growth rate alone predict relative fitness?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Strains &amp;amp; Experimental Design&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors assembled a collection of &lt;strong&gt;18 congenic &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt; strains&lt;/strong&gt; — a wild-type and 17 strains each carrying a distinct Rif-resistance (&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;) mutation (e.g. H482Y, H482N, S487L, A478D, D472Y, etc.). Two growth environments were compared:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LB&lt;/strong&gt; — nutrient-rich Miller LB medium (standard lab conditions)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SMMAsn&lt;/strong&gt; — Spizizen minimal medium with L-asparagine as the &lt;em&gt;sole&lt;/em&gt; carbon source (more representative of natural soil/rhizosphere conditions)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three main experimental approaches were used:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;IC₅₀ measurements&lt;/strong&gt; — to confirm all mutants were genuinely rifampin-resistant
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Growth rate &amp;amp; lag time measurements&lt;/strong&gt; — monoculture experiments tracking exponential growth
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Multi-allele competition experiments&lt;/strong&gt; — all 18 strains grown together simultaneously, with allele frequencies tracked every ~7 generations (for 49 generations total) using &lt;strong&gt;next-generation sequencing (NGS)&lt;/strong&gt; of &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; amplicons
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pairwise competition experiments&lt;/strong&gt; — individual mutants competed head-to-head against wild type, quantified using selection coefficients (&lt;em&gt;S&lt;/em&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Results&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Rifampin Resistance Levels&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All 17 mutant strains were confirmed as genuinely resistant. Strains selected on high Rif (50 µg/mL) had IC₅₀ values &amp;gt;100 µg/mL, while those selected on low Rif (5 µg/mL) had lower but still substantial IC₅₀ values (4–84 µg/mL) — all at least 67-fold higher than wild type.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Growth Rates in LB (Monoculture)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
10 of 17 mutants grew &lt;strong&gt;significantly slower&lt;/strong&gt; than wild type in LB
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
8 of 17 grew at a rate &lt;strong&gt;statistically indistinguishable&lt;/strong&gt; from wild type
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This already challenged the blanket assumption that Rifr = slower growth
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Multi-Allele Competition in LB&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Starting at ~5–6% each, by generation 35 the &lt;strong&gt;wild-type allele came to dominate&lt;/strong&gt; all three replicate cultures
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most Rifr mutant alleles dropped below the detection limit (~0.1%) by generation 35–49
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Two mutants persisted longer: &lt;strong&gt;H482N&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;A478D&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In one of the three replicate cultures, the A478D mutant dramatically &lt;em&gt;increased&lt;/em&gt; in frequency after generation 35, eventually comprising &amp;gt;80% of that population — traced to the spontaneous acquisition of a &lt;strong&gt;second-site compensatory mutation, I528V&lt;/strong&gt;, in &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Pairwise competitions confirmed: A478D alone was very unfit (&lt;em&gt;S&lt;/em&gt; = −1.26), but the evolved A478D+I528V strain was highly fit (&lt;em&gt;S&lt;/em&gt; = +0.84). Importantly, simply reconstructing A478D+I528V in a clean background only modestly improved fitness (&lt;em&gt;S&lt;/em&gt; = −0.47), suggesting &lt;strong&gt;additional genomic mutations&lt;/strong&gt; beyond I528V were responsible for the dramatic fitness increase in the evolved strain
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Multi-Allele Competition in SMMAsn (Minimal Medium)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The results were &lt;strong&gt;strikingly different&lt;/strong&gt; from LB:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;H482N mutant rose to dominate&lt;/strong&gt;, reaching ~70% of the population by generation 49
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The D472Y mutant came second (~20%), and wild type fell to only ~10%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;S487L mutant&lt;/strong&gt; — which had been &lt;em&gt;predicted&lt;/em&gt; to perform well based on prior metabolic profiling showing enhanced L-asparagine utilization — instead &lt;strong&gt;dropped out of the population by generation 14&lt;/strong&gt;, performing no better than the least fit alleles
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
All three replicate SMMAsn cultures behaved consistently, suggesting no second-site mutations arose (unlike the LB experiment)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Growth Rates &amp;amp; Lag Times in SMMAsn&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
All three tested mutants (D472Y, H482N, S487L) grew &lt;strong&gt;significantly faster&lt;/strong&gt; than wild type in SMMAsn monoculture
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Yet only &lt;strong&gt;H482N&lt;/strong&gt; proved more fit in competition — D472Y and S487L did not dominate despite faster growth rates
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Both H482N and S487L had &lt;strong&gt;longer lag times&lt;/strong&gt; than wild type, while D472Y did not
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Together, these results demonstrate that &lt;strong&gt;neither growth rate nor lag time alone can predict relative fitness&lt;/strong&gt; in competition
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Pairwise Competitions (LB vs. SMMAsn)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The most striking single finding: the &lt;strong&gt;H482N mutant flipped fitness sign&lt;/strong&gt; depending on the environment:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In LB: &lt;em&gt;S&lt;/em&gt; = −0.047 (slightly &lt;em&gt;less&lt;/em&gt; fit than wild type)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In SMMAsn: &lt;em&gt;S&lt;/em&gt; = +0.403 (significantly &lt;em&gt;more&lt;/em&gt; fit than wild type)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;S487L was less fit than wild type in both media, though significantly less penalized in SMMAsn than LB. D472Y showed a similar trend but the difference between media was just above the significance threshold.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Key Conclusions&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fitness of Rifr &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; alleles is strongly environment-dependent&lt;/strong&gt; — the same mutation can be disadvantageous in one environment and advantageous in another
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Growth rate is a poor proxy for fitness&lt;/strong&gt; — faster monoculture growth does not predict competitive success
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Metabolic assay results (e.g. OmniLog) also fail to predict fitness&lt;/strong&gt; — S487L's enhanced asparagine utilization in vitro did not translate to competitive advantage in asparagine minimal medium, possibly because the S487L allele autoinduces production of the antibiotic NTD, placing a metabolic burden on the cell
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compensatory mutations in &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g. I528V rescuing A478D) can restore or even exceed wild-type fitness, consistent with clinical observations in &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Broader implication&lt;/strong&gt;: rifampin resistance need not come with a fitness cost — under the right environmental conditions, resistance can be selectively &lt;em&gt;advantageous&lt;/em&gt; even in the &lt;em&gt;complete absence&lt;/em&gt; of the antibiotic. This has significant implications for understanding how antibiotic resistance persists and spreads in natural environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Methods Highlights&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
NGS-based multi-allele competition assay using Illumina iSeq100 sequencing of ~500 bp &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; amplicons — a novel approach enabling simultaneous tracking of 18 alleles
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Detection limit of ~0.1% allele frequency
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Statistical analyses: ANOVA with Tukey's HSD and Dunnett's tests; normality confirmed via Shapiro-Wilk
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
