        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_458"/>
        <dc:title>Environmental and genetic influence on the rate and spectrum of spontaneous mutations in Escherichia coli</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Spontaneous mutations are the ultimate source of novel genetic variation on which evolution operates. Although mutation rate is often discussed as a single parameter in evolution, it comprises multiple distinct types of changes at the level of DNA. Moreover, the rates of these distinct changes can be independently influenced by genomic background and environmental conditions. Using fluctuation tests, we characterized the spectrum of spontaneous mutations in Escherichia coli grown in low and high glucose environments. These conditions are known to affect the rate of spontaneous mutation in wild-type MG1655, but not in a ΔluxS deletant strain – a gene with roles in both quorum sensing and the recycling of methylation products used in E. coli’s DNA repair process. We find an increase in AT&gt;GC transitions in the low glucose environment, suggesting that processes relating to the production or repair of this mutation could drive the response of overall mutation rate to glucose concentration. Interestingly, this increase in AT&gt;GC transitions is maintained by the glucose nonresponsive ΔluxS deletant. Instead, an elevated rate of GC&gt;TA transversions, more common in a high glucose environment, leads to a net non-responsiveness of overall mutation rate for this strain. Our results show how relatively subtle changes, such as the concentration of a carbon substrate or loss of a regulatory gene, can substantially influence the amount and nature of genetic variation available to selection.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-04-30</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.001452</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-09 01:34:03</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_460">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Summary: Gifford et al. (2024)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Background &amp;amp; Motivation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Spontaneous mutations are the raw material of evolution, but mutation rate is not a single, uniform parameter — it encompasses multiple distinct types of DNA changes (transitions, transversions, indels) that can each respond independently to genetic and environmental factors. The authors build on their prior finding that &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; grown in low glucose mutates at a higher overall rate than in high glucose, but that this effect disappears in a ΔluxS deletion strain. The goal of this study was to understand &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; — i.e., which specific mutation types drive these patterns.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The luxS Gene and Why It Matters&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; plays a dual role: it participates in AI-2-mediated quorum sensing and in the activated methyl cycle (AMC), which produces S-adenosyl-l-methionine (SAM). SAM is required for DNA methylation, which in turn supports methyl-directed mismatch repair (MMR) in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;. The authors therefore hypothesized that ΔluxS might disrupt MMR efficiency, producing a characteristic shift in transition mutations (AT&amp;gt;GC and GC&amp;gt;AT).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Experimental Design&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strains:&lt;/strong&gt; Wild-type &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; K-12 MG1655 and an isogenic ΔluxS deletant
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Conditions:&lt;/strong&gt; Two glucose concentrations — low (80 mg/l) and high (250 mg/l) in Davis minimal medium
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Method:&lt;/strong&gt; High-throughput fluctuation tests (182 independent cultures per condition) using rifampicin resistance as a selective marker, since resistance maps to &amp;gt;80 known SNVs in the &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; gene, making it well-suited for mutational spectrum analysis
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sequencing:&lt;/strong&gt; Sanger sequencing of the rifampicin resistance determining region (RRDR) of &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; in resistant isolates, with mutations classified into six SNV types plus insertions and deletions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Results&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;1. Overall Mutation Rates&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Replicating prior findings, wild-type MG1655 showed a significantly higher mutation rate at low glucose (low-to-high ratio = 1.60), while the ΔluxS strain showed essentially no response to glucose (ratio = 1.06). The difference between strains was statistically significant (z-test, P = 0.004).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;2. AT&amp;gt;GC Transitions — the glucose-responsive mutation&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Both strains showed a significant increase in AT&amp;gt;GC transitions under low glucose conditions (P = 0.020), with no significant effect of strain or interaction. This is consistent with an independent prior study using a different selective marker and experimental setup (Maharjan &amp;amp; Ferenci), strengthening confidence in the finding. This suggests that whatever molecular process drives elevated AT&amp;gt;GC transitions under glucose limitation operates independently of &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mechanistically, elevated AT&amp;gt;GC transitions are associated with deficient mismatch repair — consistent with the authors' prior observation that deletion of MMR genes (&lt;em&gt;mutS&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;mutH&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;mutL&lt;/em&gt;) diminishes the glucose-mutation rate relationship. An alternative explanation involves thymine starvation, which can also selectively elevate AT&amp;gt;GC transitions, pointing toward a possible role for cellular pyrimidine availability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;3. GC&amp;gt;TA Transversions — the ΔluxS-specific effect&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The ΔluxS strain showed a significantly elevated frequency of GC&amp;gt;TA transversions across both glucose conditions (P = 0.022), with a slight (non-significant) increase at high glucose. This was the critical finding: in the ΔluxS strain under high glucose, the rise in GC&amp;gt;TA transversions roughly offsets the fall in AT&amp;gt;GC transitions, producing a net flat overall mutation rate — making the strain &lt;em&gt;appear&lt;/em&gt; non-responsive to glucose, when in reality two opposing changes in mutation types cancel each other out.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GC&amp;gt;TA transversions are typically caused by oxidative DNA damage and corrected by the GO repair system (MutM and MutY). This suggests &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; may have an uncharacterized role in regulating oxidative stress response or the GO system — a hypothesis supported by evidence from &lt;em&gt;Streptococcus mutans&lt;/em&gt;, where &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; regulates base excision repair genes and oxidative stress response genes, and by prior transcriptomic data showing that &lt;em&gt;E. coli luxS&lt;/em&gt; deletion affects expression of oxidative damage response genes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;4. Indels&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In-frame insertions and deletions were rare (n=10 total) and significantly more common in the wild-type background than in ΔluxS (P = 0.019), but were unaffected by glucose concentration. No frameshift indels were detected, as expected since &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; is essential.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Interpretation and Conclusions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The apparent &quot;non-responsiveness&quot; of ΔluxS to glucose is not because &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; abolishes the AT&amp;gt;GC transition response to glucose — that response is intact. Rather, an additional and independent elevation of GC&amp;gt;TA transversions in ΔluxS under high glucose masks it. The authors conclude that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutation rate plasticity in response to glucose is driven primarily by changes in &lt;strong&gt;AT&amp;gt;GC transitions&lt;/strong&gt;, not a global shift in all mutation types
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; gene likely influences &lt;strong&gt;oxidative damage repair&lt;/strong&gt; (GO system) rather than MMR, contrary to their original hypothesis about SAM/methylation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The link between nutrient availability and overall mutation rate is probably &lt;strong&gt;not mediated by quorum sensing&lt;/strong&gt; but instead by mutagenesis or repair deficiencies tied to resource availability
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Seemingly minor genetic or environmental changes can have profound, &lt;strong&gt;mechanistically distinct&lt;/strong&gt; effects on the mutational landscape available to natural selection
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
