    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:1178-6973">
        <prism:volume>Volume 14</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Infection and Drug Resistance</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.2147/IDR.S333433</dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:alternative>IDR</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1178-6973</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_471">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Summary: Li et al. (2021) — &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; Mutations and Rifampin Resistance in &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This study sequenced the entire &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; gene across 175 clinical TB isolates from China, measured minimum inhibitory concentrations (MICs) for rifampin (RIF), and used crystal structure modelling to understand how specific mutations physically disrupt the RpoB–RIF interaction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Core Finding: Three Hotspot Codons Dominate&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The vast majority of mutations clustered at just three codons — &lt;strong&gt;450, 445, and 435&lt;/strong&gt; — with frequencies of 47.5%, 28.0%, and 16.1% respectively among mutated isolates. Everything else combined accounted for only ~27% of mutations, and most of those co-occurred alongside a hotspot mutation anyway.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why Are These Mutations So Much More Common?&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper doesn't state this explicitly in one place, but the answer emerges from combining the structural and phenotypic data:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Location in the RIF-binding pocket&lt;/strong&gt; Residues H445 and S450 form direct &lt;strong&gt;intermolecular hydrogen bonds&lt;/strong&gt; with RIF — they are the structural anchors of drug binding. D435 participates in &lt;strong&gt;van der Waals interactions&lt;/strong&gt; in the binding pocket. Mutations here directly destabilize the drug-protein interface, making them highly effective at conferring resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. High-level resistance payoff&lt;/strong&gt; S450L, H445D, H445Y, and H445R all drove MICs to ≥128 µg/mL — the maximum tested — and were statistically significant in multivariate analysis. This extreme resistance phenotype means bacteria carrying these mutations survive even at high drug concentrations, giving them a strong selective advantage during treatment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Structural versatility at H445&lt;/strong&gt; Eight different amino acid substitutions were observed at position 445 (R, L, Y, C, D, P, N, Q), and every single one disrupted the hydrogen bond with RIF. This means the bacterial genome has many mutational &quot;paths&quot; to resistance through this one codon, making it a highly accessible target for selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Mutations elsewhere carry fitness costs without sufficient reward&lt;/strong&gt; Many non-hotspot mutations (e.g., those at P45, T400, G759, Q1056) had &lt;strong&gt;no direct interaction with RIF at all&lt;/strong&gt; — they simply don't contribute to resistance on their own. Others like Q429H, S431G, and D435G left RpoB–RIF interactions completely unchanged in structural modelling. These appear to function as &lt;strong&gt;compensatory mutations&lt;/strong&gt; that offset the fitness costs imposed by the major resistance mutations, rather than driving resistance themselves.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Some resistance mechanisms are structurally &quot;weaker&quot;&lt;/strong&gt; Mutations at hydrophobic residues like L430, L452, and I491 cause resistance by disrupting hydrophobic interactions, but the resistance levels achieved are generally lower and more variable. L452P, for instance, showed a wide MIC range (2–128 µg/mL). Lower resistance levels mean less selective advantage, so these mutations are less likely to sweep through a bacterial population under drug pressure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Bigger Picture&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The dominance of S450L in particular (~46% of all mutated isolates with a single substitution) reflects the convergence of several advantages: it disrupts a critical hydrogen bond, introduces steric hindrance that physically blocks RIF binding, and confers maximum-level resistance. Natural selection under rifampin treatment essentially funnels &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; toward the handful of mutations that are both structurally effective and accessible — and S450L, H445X, and D435V sit at that intersection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
