        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_468"/>
        <dc:title>Study of the Rifampin Monoresistance Mechanism in Mycobacterium tuberculosis</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>ABSTRACT
            
              Rifampin (RIF) susceptibility is a key factor in determining the treatment effectiveness of the standardized treatment regimens. In
              Mycobacterium tuberculosis
              , both target gene mutation and the efflux pump play major roles in the resistance to antituberculosis drugs. By eliminating RIF-resistant strains with
              rpoB
              mutation, the choice of RIF-monoresistant strains may allow us to identify the RIF-specific efflux pump genes. This study explored the RIF monoresistance mechanism in
              M. tuberculosis
              . Data from DNA sequencing and MIC measurements revealed that specific mutations, including Ser531Leu and His526Asp in RpoB, show high-level drug resistance. Three-dimensional structure modeling provided further evidence that the affinity between RIF and RpoB mutants was in accordance with the drug resistance level of the corresponding isolates. Furthermore, transcription-level analysis among the nonmutated isolates indicated that three efflux pumps (Rv0783, Rv2936, and Rv0933) might be involved in exporting RIF from the cell. Compared to 8 μg/ml for wild-type
              Escherichia coli
              , the MICs for the transgenic
              E. coli
              strains with either Rv0783 or Rv2936 were 32 and 16 μg/ml, respectively. In conclusion, our study indicated that several RpoB mutant types, including Ser531Leu and His526Asp, show high-level RIF resistance attributed to low affinity between RpoB mutant proteins and RIF. In addition, this work demonstrates that Rv2936 and Rv0783 may be responsible for low-level resistance to RIF by exporting RIF from cells. The predicted structure of RpoB and the newly identified efflux pumps in this study will provide a novel approach to design new drugs and develop novel diagnosis technologies.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>02/2013</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://journals.asm.org/doi/10.1128/AAC.01024-12</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-16 01:59:58</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>893-900</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_472">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Summary: Pang et al. (2013) — Rifampin Monoresistance Mechanism in &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This study took a two-pronged approach to understanding RIF resistance: examining the structural basis of &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations in 24 RIF-monoresistant clinical isolates, and then separately investigating efflux pumps in the subset of isolates that had &lt;em&gt;no&lt;/em&gt; detectable &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutation at all.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Findings on &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; Mutations&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Among the 16 isolates with RRDR mutations, codon 531 was mutated most frequently (37.5%), followed by codon 526 (25%). This mirrors global patterns consistently reported across populations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;MIC outcomes varied dramatically by mutation type:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Avg. MIC (µg/mL)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mechanism&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ser531Leu&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;192&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lost H-bond + steric clash&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;His526Asp&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;256&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lost H-bond + charge repulsion&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;His526Gly&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;0.75&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lost H-bond only&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;His526Leu&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;4&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lost H-bond only&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;His526Arg&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Steric bump, but H-bond retained&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Leu533Pro&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;0.5&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;No meaningful interaction change&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why Are Ser531Leu and His526Asp So Much More Common?&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper's structural modelling provides a compelling mechanistic answer — and it comes down to the &lt;em&gt;compounding&lt;/em&gt; of multiple disruptive effects:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ser531Leu dominates because it hits the RIF interaction in two ways simultaneously.&lt;/strong&gt; Wild-type Ser531 forms a direct hydrogen bond with RIF. The leucine substitution not only destroys that bond but also introduces &lt;strong&gt;steric clashing&lt;/strong&gt; — the bulkier leucine side chain physically collides with the RIF molecule, further reducing binding affinity. This double disruption drives MICs to 128–256 µg/mL, providing overwhelming selective advantage under drug pressure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;His526Asp is similarly devastating through a different double mechanism.&lt;/strong&gt; Histidine at position 526 carries a positive charge that is attracted to the negatively charged surface of RIF, and it also forms a direct hydrogen bond. Substitution with aspartate flips the charge from positive to negative, introducing &lt;strong&gt;electrostatic repulsion&lt;/strong&gt; against RIF in addition to destroying the hydrogen bond. Again, two independent mechanisms combine to essentially eliminate drug binding.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Contrast this with the &quot;weaker&quot; mutations at the same codons.&lt;/strong&gt; His526Gly and His526Leu only break the hydrogen bond — they are smaller or neutral residues that don't introduce new repulsive forces. His526Arg is an interesting case: the arginine residue is large enough to cause steric bumping, but because it retains a positive charge similar to histidine, it partially maintains hydrogen bonding and charge affinity. These partial disruptions translate directly into low-to-moderate resistance levels (MIC 0.75–4 µg/mL), meaning the bacteria survive drug exposure but with less competitive advantage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The key principle the paper articulates explicitly: high binding affinity depends on &lt;em&gt;both&lt;/em&gt; structural complementarity (no steric clashes) &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; strong intermolecular interactions (hydrogen bonds, electrostatic attraction). &lt;strong&gt;Only mutations that disrupt both simultaneously — Ser531Leu and His526Asp — produce the high-level resistance that confers the strongest selective sweep under treatment.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Efflux Pump Contribution&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Notably, over 30% of isolates in this study had &lt;em&gt;no&lt;/em&gt; RRDR mutation at all, yet were phenotypically resistant. Transcriptional profiling identified three upregulated efflux pump genes (Rv2936, Rv0783, Rv0933) in these strains. Functional validation in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; confirmed that Rv2936 and Rv0783 independently increased RIF MIC 4-fold and 2-fold respectively. However, efflux-mediated resistance produced only low-to-moderate MICs — it is a secondary mechanism that can't match the resistance conferred by the dominant hotspot mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Synthesis&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Together with the Li et al. paper, Pang et al. reinforces a consistent picture: &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations are not created equal. The mutations that become epidemiologically dominant are precisely those that disrupt RIF binding through the most mechanistically destructive combinations — breaking the critical hydrogen bond &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; adding steric or electrostatic repulsion. Mutations that only partially impair binding generate insufficient resistance to dominate under strong selection pressure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
