        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_481"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_477"/>
        <dc:title>High-resolution landscape of an antibiotic binding site</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Abstract
            
              Antibiotic binding sites are located in important domains of essential enzymes and have been extensively studied in the context of resistance mutations; however, their study is limited by positive selection. Using multiplex genome engineering
              1
              to overcome this constraint, we generate and characterize a collection of 760 single-residue mutants encompassing the entire rifampicin binding site of
              Escherichia
              coli
              RNA polymerase (RNAP). By genetically mapping drug–enzyme interactions, we identify an alpha helix where mutations considerably enhance or disrupt rifampicin binding. We find mutations in this region that prolong antibiotic binding, converting rifampicin from a bacteriostatic to bactericidal drug by inducing lethal DNA breaks. The latter are replication dependent, indicating that rifampicin kills by causing detrimental transcription–replication conflicts at promoters. We also identify additional binding site mutations that greatly increase the speed of RNAP.Fast RNAP depletes the cell of nucleotides, alters cell sensitivity to different antibiotics and provides a cold growth advantage. Finally, by mapping natural
              rpoB
              sequence diversity, we discover that functional rifampicin binding site mutations that alter RNAP properties or confer drug resistance occur frequently in nature.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-10-05</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06495-6</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-17 01:16:19</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>180-187</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0028-0836,%201476-4687">
        <prism:volume>622</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Nature</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1038/s41586-023-06495-6</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>7981</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Nature</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0028-0836, 1476-4687</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_481">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Yang et al. (2023) — &quot;High-resolution landscape of an antibiotic binding site&quot; (&lt;em&gt;Nature&lt;/em&gt; 622)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Background and Motivation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prior work on antibiotic resistance mutations is inherently biased: you can only study mutations that survive positive selection (i.e., ones that confer resistance without killing the cell). This means the vast majority of the mutational space at antibiotic binding sites had never been characterized. The rifampicin (Rif) binding site on the β-subunit of RNA polymerase (RNAP), encoded by &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;, is well-defined structurally, but known resistance mutants cover less than 5% of the possible single-residue substitutions in the region.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Approach: Saturated Mutagenesis via MAGE-seq&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors used &lt;strong&gt;Multiplex Automated Genome Engineering (MAGE)&lt;/strong&gt; in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; to systematically generate all possible single amino acid substitutions across the entire Rif binding site — 20 amino acid substitutions × 38 positions (rpoB 510–537 and 563–572) = &lt;strong&gt;760 mutants&lt;/strong&gt;. This was combined with deep sequencing to track mutant frequencies before and after selection, bypassing the constraint of positive selection entirely. Critically, they could detect both resistant &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; hypersensitive mutants, which traditional approaches would miss.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They screened the mutant pool under four conditions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Normal growth (LB) — to measure &lt;strong&gt;fitness&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Rifampicin (Rif) — drug sensitivity/resistance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Bicyclomycin (BCM) — inhibitor of Rho transcription termination
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
5-fluorouracil (5FU) — a nucleotide analogue
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Finding 1: Position 531 is &quot;Featureless&quot; in the Screen — and Why That's Interesting&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is directly relevant to your earlier question. The paper notes that mutations at position 531 are conspicuously hard to detect as resistant in the screening format — not because they don't confer resistance, but because &lt;strong&gt;they are generally slow-growing&lt;/strong&gt;. The fitness screen reveals these mutants carry a significant growth cost, which depresses their abundance even when they are highly Rif-resistant. This is consistent with the clinical literature showing S531L has a fitness cost that is compensated by secondary mutations over time, and helps explain why this paper is relevant: even the most clinically dominant rpoB mutation (531) carries measurable fitness baggage in the absence of compensatory evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Finding 2: A Critical Alpha Helix (residues 521–526)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper identifies a single alpha helix spanning rpoB positions &lt;strong&gt;521–526&lt;/strong&gt; as a major determinant of Rif binding. Mutations within this helix have polarized effects:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutations at &lt;strong&gt;S522 and H526&lt;/strong&gt; tend to produce &lt;strong&gt;high-level Rif resistance&lt;/strong&gt; (disrupting drug binding)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutations at &lt;strong&gt;L521 and T525&lt;/strong&gt; tend to cause &lt;strong&gt;Rif hypersensitivity&lt;/strong&gt; (tightening drug binding)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This establishes a precise genetic map of drug–enzyme interaction at single-residue resolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Finding 3: Rif Hypersensitive (RifHS) Mutants — Converting a Bacteriostatic Drug to Bactericidal&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two mutants stood out most strongly: &lt;strong&gt;L521Y&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;T525D&lt;/strong&gt;. These have no fitness defect on their own, but when exposed to Rif, cells take over 8 hours to recover (vs. 2 hours for wild type), have lower MICs, and show tighter Rif binding in vitro.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Strikingly, while Rif is normally &lt;strong&gt;bacteriostatic&lt;/strong&gt; in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, it becomes &lt;strong&gt;bactericidal&lt;/strong&gt; in both RifHS mutants, reducing viability by at least one order of magnitude. This effect is Rif-specific — other bacteriostatic drugs remain bacteriostatic in these strains.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mechanism of killing:&lt;/strong&gt; The bactericidal effect involves DNA damage:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
TUNEL staining shows elevated DNA fragmentation (~35%) in RifHS mutants treated with Rif, comparable to known bactericidal drugs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Knockouts of double-strand break (DSB) repair enzymes (&lt;em&gt;recA&lt;/em&gt; or &lt;em&gt;recBCD&lt;/em&gt;) dramatically potentiate Rif killing in RifHS mutants
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Even wild-type cells lacking &lt;em&gt;recA&lt;/em&gt; become Rif-sensitive, indicating Rif causes low-level DNA damage even normally
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ChIP-seq shows that RifHS RNAP persists at promoters longer after Rif washout compared to wild type
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SLR-qPCR shows DSBs are concentrated specifically at &lt;strong&gt;promoters&lt;/strong&gt;, not gene bodies
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Critically, blocking replication (using a temperature-sensitive &lt;em&gt;dnaC&lt;/em&gt; allele or dCas9 targeting oriC) &lt;strong&gt;abolishes bactericidal killing&lt;/strong&gt;, demonstrating the damage is replication-dependent
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The proposed mechanism is that RNAP trapped at promoters by Rif creates &lt;strong&gt;transcription–replication conflicts&lt;/strong&gt; — the replisome collides with the stalled RNAP initiation complex, generating lethal DSBs. This is a mechanistically distinct form of bacterial killing, not involving reactive oxygen species (ROS), unlike most other bactericidal antibiotics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Finding 4: Fast RNAP Mutants and Nucleotide Depletion&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutations at positions 515, 523, 526, and 527 produce RNAP that transcribes faster than wild type — measured directly by a multiprobe qPCR assay along the &lt;em&gt;lac&lt;/em&gt; operon. These fast mutants:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Have higher elongation rates (~51–54 nt/s vs. ~41 nt/s for WT)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Pause less frequently (measured by NETseq)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Are highly sensitive to &lt;strong&gt;5FU&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;BCM&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Are sensitive to &lt;strong&gt;trimethoprim&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The connection to 5FU sensitivity is mechanistic: fast RNAP &lt;strong&gt;depletes intracellular pyrimidine nucleotides&lt;/strong&gt; (uridine and cytidine triphosphate fall to the level of a &lt;em&gt;upp&lt;/em&gt; salvage-pathway mutant), which sensitizes cells to 5FU's inhibition of &lt;em&gt;thyA&lt;/em&gt; (thymidylate synthase). Thymidine supplementation rescues 5FU sensitivity, confirming the mechanism. The authors also show fast mutants have a shorter &quot;resistance phase&quot; in thymineless death assays, consistent with faster depletion of dTMP reserves.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Notably, fast RNAP provides a &lt;strong&gt;growth advantage at lower temperatures (25°C)&lt;/strong&gt; due to poor termination efficiency — poor Rho-dependent termination means more readthrough, which appears beneficial in the cold. This is corroborated by the fact that subinhibitory BCM (a Rho inhibitor) similarly accelerates wild-type growth at 25°C.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Finding 5: Natural rpoB Diversity&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors aligned ~4,000 &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; orthologues from across bacterial phyla (from KEGG) and mapped natural variation onto their functional screen. The Rif binding site is highly evolutionarily conserved overall, but some variants exist at appreciable frequency:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;524L&lt;/strong&gt; — found in &amp;gt;10% of species surveyed, has fast-RNAP characteristics, common in Actinobacteria and Firmicutes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;526N&lt;/strong&gt; — found in ~1% of species, combines fast-RNAP phenotype with Rif resistance; present in Tenericutes including &lt;em&gt;Mycoplasma&lt;/em&gt; (which are known to be Rif-resistant)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;567Q&lt;/strong&gt; — found in ~2% of species, confers resistance to both BCM and 5FU; associated with Epsilonproteobacteria including &lt;em&gt;Helicobacter&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;Campylobacter&lt;/em&gt;, which live in harsh environments like digestive tracts
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An interesting observation is that clinical resistance mutations (which develop quickly) carry larger fitness costs than the functionally similar mutations that arose over millions of years of evolution in these organisms.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Broader Significance&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Drug design:&lt;/strong&gt; Identifying mutations that tighten Rif binding (converting it bactericidal) suggests a rational route to improving Rif analogues — increased binding affinity could shorten treatment duration and broaden antimicrobial spectrum.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Transcription–replication conflicts:&lt;/strong&gt; The finding that replication is required for Rif killing implicates promoter-trapped RNAP as a general source of DNA damage during normal bacterial physiology.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Transcription speed and physiology:&lt;/strong&gt; The demonstration that RNAP speed directly controls intracellular nucleotide pools is a novel physiological insight — marginal increases in transcription rate have significant downstream consequences for antibiotic sensitivity and growth temperature optima.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Methodology:&lt;/strong&gt; The MAGE-seq approach provides a template for comprehensively characterizing any antibiotic binding site, free from selection bias — a significant advance over classical resistance mutation screens.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
