        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_489"/>
        <dc:title>Mutation bias and adaptation in bacteria</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Genetic mutation, which provides the raw material for evolutionary adaptation, is largely a stochastic force. However, there is ample evidence showing that mutations can also exhibit strong biases, with some mutation types and certain genomic positions mutating more often than others. It is becoming increasingly clear that mutational bias can play a role in determining adaptive outcomes in bacteria in both the laboratory and the clinic. As such, understanding the causes and consequences of mutation bias can help microbiologists to anticipate and predict adaptive outcomes. In this review, we provide an overview of the mechanisms and features of the bacterial genome that cause mutational biases to occur. We then describe the environmental triggers that drive these mechanisms to be more potent and outline the adaptive scenarios where mutation bias can synergize with natural selection to define evolutionary outcomes. We conclude by describing how understanding mutagenic genomic features can help microbiologists predict areas sensitive to mutational bias, and finish by outlining future work that will help us achieve more accurate evolutionary forecasts.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-11-09</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.001404</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-22 01:43:49</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_501">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Overview&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This review argues that mutation — while fundamentally stochastic — is not random in any uniform sense. Certain genomic positions and certain mutation types arise far more frequently than others, and these biases can profoundly shape the course of bacterial adaptation. The authors synthesize the mechanistic causes of mutation bias, the environmental conditions that modulate it, the evolutionary contexts where it most strongly influences outcomes, and the tools available to predict it — ultimately building toward a vision of evolutionary forecasting in bacteria.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Definitions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper opens by carefully distinguishing three related but distinct concepts that are often conflated:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutation rate&lt;/strong&gt; refers to the absolute frequency of mutation per nucleotide per generation — a genome-wide average. &lt;strong&gt;Mutation bias&lt;/strong&gt; describes &lt;em&gt;relative&lt;/em&gt; differences in mutation rates between positions or mutation types — for example, transitions being more common than transversions despite transversions being theoretically more numerous. &lt;strong&gt;Mutational hotspots&lt;/strong&gt; are specific positions or short regions that mutate at rates substantially higher than the genome average, while &lt;strong&gt;coldspots&lt;/strong&gt; mutate below average. These distinctions matter because a change in overall mutation rate (say, from a hypermutator allele) does not uniformly amplify all mutations — it shifts the spectrum, introducing new biases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A crucial insight in the review is that mutation bias and natural selection can operate in synergy &lt;em&gt;or&lt;/em&gt; in opposition. When the mutations favored by bias overlap with those favored by selection, evolution becomes highly repeatable and predictable. When they conflict — as in the universal bacterial GC→AT bias that persists in GC-rich genomes — selection overrides bias. Determining which regime applies in any given situation is central to the paper's goals.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Section 1: Causes of Mutation Bias — Local and Genome-Wide Mechanisms&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors organize the mechanistic causes of bias across multiple spatial scales, from genome-wide patterns down to individual nucleotides. Four major processes drive these biases: &lt;strong&gt;replication&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;transcription&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;replication-transcription conflicts&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;DNA repair&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Replication-associated biases&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bacteria have a single origin of replication (OriC) and replicate bidirectionally. This architecture has measurable consequences for mutation rates. Replication fidelity is demonstrably higher near OriC in multiple species including &lt;em&gt;P. fluorescens&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;S. enterica&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;V. fischeri&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;V. cholerae&lt;/em&gt; — meaning mutation rates increase at greater chromosomal distances from the origin, producing a broad &quot;wave-like&quot; pattern across the genome.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Additionally, the leading and lagging strands are replicated by different mechanisms. The leading strand is copied continuously, while the lagging strand is copied in segments (Okazaki fragments), leaving it exposed as single-stranded DNA for longer periods. This makes the lagging strand more vulnerable to &lt;strong&gt;cytosine deamination&lt;/strong&gt;, a spontaneous chemical reaction that converts cytosine to uracil, resulting in C→T transitions. However, lagging strand synthesis is paradoxically associated with &lt;em&gt;higher fidelity&lt;/em&gt; in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; overall, reflecting the complex interplay of multiple competing effects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Local nucleotide context&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the finest scale — the level of individual bases — the immediate nucleotide neighborhood profoundly affects mutation rates. Several specific sequence features are discussed:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mononucleotide repeats&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., CCCCC) cause the DNA polymerase to slip, generating insertions or deletions (indels). &lt;strong&gt;Inverse repeat sequences&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., ATCGAC-n-GTCGAT) can form secondary structures — hairpins or cruciform structures — that stall the polymerase and generate polymorphisms or double-strand breaks. &lt;strong&gt;Homopolymeric guanine tracts&lt;/strong&gt; on the leading strand cause substitution mutations with elevated frequency. The &lt;strong&gt;nucleotide triplet context&lt;/strong&gt; — the identity of the two flanking bases surrounding a focal nucleotide — is identified as arguably the single most important determinant of local substitution bias in bacteria; mismatch repair efficiency in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; can vary by ~43-fold depending on this triplet.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Transcription-associated biases&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During transcription, only one strand — the transcribed strand — is bound by RNA polymerase. The &lt;strong&gt;non-transcribed strand&lt;/strong&gt; is exposed as free single-stranded DNA and becomes more vulnerable to cytosine deamination. This generates a transcription-coupled strand asymmetry in mutation rates. Furthermore, &lt;strong&gt;transcription-coupled nucleotide excision repair&lt;/strong&gt; operates alongside RNA polymerase and preferentially repairs the transcribed strand, reinforcing this asymmetry.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Replication-transcription conflicts&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When replication and transcription occur simultaneously, the two polymerase complexes can collide. &lt;strong&gt;Co-directional collisions&lt;/strong&gt; (same direction) cause some genomic instability but are generally less damaging. &lt;strong&gt;Head-on collisions&lt;/strong&gt; (opposing directions) are more disruptive and were initially reported to elevate mutation rates in &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt;. However, subsequent studies complicated this picture considerably. Both Foster et al. and Schroeder et al. found that the apparent elevation in mutation at head-on genes in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt; could be explained instead by local nucleotide compositional features rather than collision events per se. The authors conclude that while head-on genes can cause instability and may elevate indel rates in promoter regions, their role as a potent general mutagen remains unresolved.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;DNA repair as a source of bias&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mismatch repair (MMR) is the genome's primary safeguard against replication errors, and defects in MMR produce characteristic, strongly biased mutational spectra. Loss of &lt;strong&gt;MutS&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;MutL&lt;/strong&gt; (the core MMR proteins) causes sharp increases in transitions and small indels. In contrast, defects in &lt;strong&gt;MutT&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;MutY&lt;/strong&gt; — enzymes that remove the oxidized guanine derivative 8-oxo-G — shift the spectrum toward specific transversions: A:T→C:G and C:G→A:T respectively. Importantly, many pathogens evolve functional MMR deficiency under strong selection, generating &lt;strong&gt;mutator phenotypes&lt;/strong&gt; with biased spectra that may facilitate rapid adaptation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Section 2: Environmental Drivers of Mutation Bias&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutation bias is not static — it changes depending on the bacterium's growth phase and exposure to environmental stressors. This section makes the critical point that genomic features should be understood as having &lt;strong&gt;mutagenic potential&lt;/strong&gt; that is realized differently under different conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Growth phase effects&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;exponential (log) phase&lt;/strong&gt; is when replication is most active, so replication-associated biases — including those from polymerase slippage, strand asymmetries, and proximity to OriC — are most strongly expressed during this phase.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;stationary phase&lt;/strong&gt; represents nutrient limitation and activates the &lt;strong&gt;general stress response&lt;/strong&gt;, characterized by upregulation of the sigma factor &lt;strong&gt;RpoS&lt;/strong&gt;. This increases overall mutation rate but does so unevenly — for example, G:C→T:A rates increase while G:C→C:G rates do not — thereby driving mutation bias. An important counterbalance is the phenomenon of &lt;strong&gt;density-associated mutation rate plasticity (DAMP)&lt;/strong&gt;: at high cell densities, the mutagenic compound 8-oxo-G is more effectively scavenged by MutT, so denser populations paradoxically exhibit &lt;em&gt;lower&lt;/em&gt; mutation rates from this source, creating a trade-off between growth-associated and stress-induced mutagenesis.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nucleoid-associated proteins&lt;/strong&gt; that compact and organize the chromosome protect DNA from environmental damage during stationary phase but can interfere with replication machinery when DNA is bound in superhelical conformations, increasing mutagenesis during exponential growth.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Biofilm formation&lt;/strong&gt; creates local microenvironments with nutrient depletion and toxin accumulation that trigger the &lt;strong&gt;SOS response&lt;/strong&gt; — a non-general stress response that upregulates error-prone DNA polymerases (Pol II, Pol IV, and Pol V). These polymerases perform &lt;strong&gt;translesion synthesis&lt;/strong&gt; past DNA lesions but introduce mutations with biases that depend on both the type of lesion and the specific polymerase involved.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Antibiotic exposure&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Antibiotics are highlighted as particularly relevant environmental mutagens because of their clinical importance. Beyond simply raising overall mutation rates, certain antibiotics alter the mutational &lt;em&gt;spectrum&lt;/em&gt;. For instance, norfloxacin (a fluoroquinolone) specifically increases G:C→T:A transversions in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; in a dose-dependent manner. This means that the &lt;em&gt;treatment itself&lt;/em&gt; reshapes the mutation landscape, potentially influencing which adaptive mutations are most likely to arise during therapy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Section 3: When Mutation Bias Guides Adaptation — The Hard vs. Soft Sweep Framework&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This section develops the core theoretical framework for understanding when mutation bias will actually determine adaptive outcomes, as opposed to merely influencing background variation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Hard vs. soft selective sweeps&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;hard selective sweep&lt;/strong&gt; occurs when a single &lt;em&gt;de novo&lt;/em&gt; adaptive mutation arises in a population with little or no standing genetic variation and sweeps to fixation before rival adaptive mutations appear. In this &quot;origin-fixation&quot; model, the first mutation to occur is the one that wins — so the relative rates at which different mutations arise directly determines which adaptation occurs. Mutation bias is therefore &lt;strong&gt;maximally influential&lt;/strong&gt; under hard sweep conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;soft selective sweep&lt;/strong&gt; occurs when multiple adaptive genotypes already exist (from standing genetic variation) or when multiple adaptive mutations arise and compete simultaneously. Here, which mutation fixes is determined more by its fitness benefit than by which one arose first. Mutation bias is &lt;strong&gt;less influential&lt;/strong&gt; under soft sweep conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The key variables that determine which regime applies are: (1) initial genetic diversity — whether the population starts with adaptive variants or not; (2) mutation supply (μ) — how quickly new mutations arrive relative to the rate of fixation; (3) the selective benefit (s) of adaptive mutations — stronger selection accelerates fixation, allowing less time for rivals to appear; (4) population size — larger populations have higher mutation supply, increasing the likelihood of soft sweeps; and (5) spatial structure — isolated sub-populations (niches) can shelter competing genotypes, promoting soft sweeps.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Clinical relevance&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Infection represents a context strongly conducive to hard sweeps and therefore to mutation bias having a defining role. During host colonization, bacterial populations pass through severe &lt;strong&gt;bottlenecks&lt;/strong&gt; — at mucosal barriers, organ filters, and during transmission — that dramatically reduce genetic diversity. Non-mutator obligate pathogens facing limited bottlenecks and strong novel selection can readily satisfy hard sweep conditions. Indeed, mutation bias has been documented in the adaptive evolution of &lt;em&gt;Mycobacterium tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; and the influenza virus, and antibiotic-imposed mutational biases have been detected in clinical evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Laboratory relevance&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Many standard evolution experiment designs also favor hard sweeps. Experiments initiated with &lt;strong&gt;clonal populations&lt;/strong&gt; begin with no standing variation. &lt;strong&gt;Short-duration experiments&lt;/strong&gt; isolating the first adaptive mutant (as in resistance screens) artificially enforce fixation of the first successful variant. Smaller population sizes reduce mutation supply. All of these design choices increase the prominence of mutation bias in determining outcomes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Work by Schenk et al. illustrates this clearly: structural variants (SVs) arose at higher rates than single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, allowing SVs to arrive earlier in both large and small populations despite conferring lower average fitness than SNPs. But SVs were more likely to reach fixation specifically in &lt;strong&gt;smaller populations&lt;/strong&gt;, where their early arrival gave them a decisive advantage before a fitter SNP mutant could appear.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Section 4: Using Genomic Signatures to Predict Bias&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A major applied thrust of the review is the development of a systematic, multi-scale approach to predicting which genomic regions will exhibit elevated mutation rates — enabling &lt;strong&gt;evolutionary forecasting&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Multi-scale prediction framework&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the &lt;strong&gt;megabase scale&lt;/strong&gt;, the wave-like pattern of replication fidelity (lower near OriC and terminus, higher at intermediate distances) provides a broad genomic map of relative mutability. Absence of mismatch repair genes causes a transition bias across the entire genome, detectable through alignment searches.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the &lt;strong&gt;kilobase scale&lt;/strong&gt;, gene orientation (leading vs. lagging strand during both replication and transcription) determines strand-associated biases across a locus. Proximity to insertion sequences (IS elements) and other direct repeats flagged potential recombination-mediated deletion hotspots. Proximity to nucleoid-associated protein binding sites determines growth-phase sensitivity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the &lt;strong&gt;single-nucleotide scale&lt;/strong&gt;, specific sequence motifs drive highly localized bias: CCWGG sequences (dcm methylation targets) prone to cytosine deamination; quasi-palindromic sequences enabling hairpin formation and template-switching; homopolymeric tracts causing slippage; nucleotide triplets affecting mismatch repair efficiency.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose that these features can be incorporated into bioinformatic pipelines to identify mutation-sensitive sequences in any well-annotated bacterial genome, enabling prospective identification of likely hotspot positions within genes of clinical or evolutionary interest.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Section 5: Future Directions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Understanding interacting mechanisms&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A key challenge is that mutational mechanisms do not operate independently — they interact, sometimes synergistically and sometimes antagonistically. Methylated cytosines on the non-transcribed strand are doubly vulnerable to deamination. A hotspot's apparent dominance can be masked when placed in a mutator background where the overall spectrum shifts toward transitions. Pipelines for evolutionary forecasting must account for these interactions rather than treating each mechanism in isolation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Detecting bias from experimental data&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When evolution experiments are performed, what is observed is a subset of the complete pool of possible adaptive mutations — filtered both by which mutations actually arose (shaped by bias) and by which were advantageous (shaped by selection). Disentangling these is non-trivial. The authors recommend two complementary strategies: (1) increasing experimental replicates and knocking out frequently-observed hotspot targets to reveal rarer adaptive mutations; and (2) making functionally-silent genomic modifications to known bias-causing sequence features to directly test their causal role without perturbing gene function.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Does selection shape mutation bias itself?&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The review concludes with a genuinely open and contested question: does natural selection act to &lt;em&gt;regulate&lt;/em&gt; mutation bias, not just respond to its outputs? Several lines of evidence are discussed. Lynch's &lt;strong&gt;drift-barrier hypothesis&lt;/strong&gt; predicts that purifying selection reduces mutation rates across the genome, but is counteracted by genetic drift — meaning essential genes under strong purifying selection will evolve lower mutation rates, while less constrained regions will be more mutable. Evidence for this from &lt;em&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/em&gt; (where essential gene bodies show lower mutation rates) is intriguing but disputed. &lt;strong&gt;Second-order selection&lt;/strong&gt; — where mutagenic mechanisms hitchhike alongside selected traits — explains the indirect evolution of mutators and may also explain why stress-induced mutagenesis evolves as a by-product of stress-protective mechanisms. &lt;strong&gt;Contingency loci&lt;/strong&gt; — genes with high-rate reversible mutations in promoters or reading frames that generate rapid phenotypic switching — represent possible examples of positively selected elevated mutation at specific positions, particularly in pathogens adapting to fluctuating host environments. However, direct evidence for &lt;em&gt;selection&lt;/em&gt; specifically maintaining any given bias remains limited and contested.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Conclusion&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Horton and Taylor make a compelling case that mutation bias is not a background noise to be subtracted from evolutionary analyses, but an active force that — especially in small populations, during infection, and in short-term evolution experiments — can be the decisive factor determining which adaptive outcomes occur. The field is moving toward practical evolutionary forecasting by combining mechanistic understanding of bias-generating features with bioinformatic analysis of genomic sequence. The immediate challenge is integrating knowledge of how these mechanisms interact, and high-throughput, open-access mutation data will be essential for making genomic predictions of mutability generalizable across bacterial species and contexts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
