        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_496"/>
        <dc:title>Reflections on the Predictability of Evolution: Toward a Conceptual Framework</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Evolution is generally considered to be unpredictable because genetic variations are known to occur randomly. However, remarkable patterns of repeated convergent evolution are observed, for instance, loss of pigments by organisms living in caves. Analogous phenotypes appear in similar environments, sometimes in response to similar constraints. Alongside randomness, a certain evolutionary determinism also exists, for instance, the selection of particular phenotypes subjected to particular environmental constraints in the ‘‘evolutionary funnel.’’ We pursue the idea that eco-evolutionary specialization is in some way determinist. The conceptual framework of phenotypic changes entailing specialization presented in this essay explains how evolution can be predicted. We also discuss how the predictability of evolution could be tested using the case of metabolic specialization through gene losses. We also put forward that microorganisms could be key models to test and possibly make headway evolutionary predictions and knowledge about evolution.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>11/2020</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Reflections on the Predictability of Evolution</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2589004220309330</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-23 05:58:37</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>101736</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_528">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Summary: Mas et al. (2020) — Reflections on the Predictability of Evolution&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Core Question&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Is evolution predictable, or is it fundamentally random?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Tension&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Beliefs have constantly shifted between a deterministic Lamarckist view and the all-random insights of the neutral theory. The authors argue neither extreme is fully right.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Why Evolution Seems Unpredictable&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Predicting evolution is intricate as it invokes every biological level — from DNA modification to changes in phenotypes and even population and community structures — and each level has its own complexity and feedbacks on the others. On top of this, the environment is also modified by organisms themselves as they evolve, creating eco-evolutionary feedbacks that require continual refinement of new adaptations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;But Convergent Evolution Suggests Otherwise&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Remarkable patterns of repeated evolution have been observed at the phenotypic level, where analogous phenotypes appear in similar environments — sometimes in response to similar constraints. This extends to the genetic level too, with independent lineages showing similar genetic modifications, such as the same mutations in pigmentation genes causing cave-adapted animals to lose pigmentation, or convergent hemoglobin adaptations to altitude.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The &quot;Evolutionary Funnel&quot; Framework&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors' central contribution is the concept of an &lt;strong&gt;evolutionary funnel&lt;/strong&gt;: starting from the virtual set of all potential modifications at the individual level, variations are &quot;sieved&quot; by constraints at different biological levels — genetic, metabolic, phenotypic, and environmental — reducing the number of achievable optimal evolutionary solutions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;When Can We Predict Evolution?&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prediction is most feasible in the context of &lt;strong&gt;specialization&lt;/strong&gt;. In a temporally stable and spatially homogeneous environment, metabolic optimization is expected to be progressively integrated into the genome, and it is in this context of evolution toward specialization that it is probably feasible to prune the space of evolutionary possibilities and predict evolutionary trajectories. Conversely, in a switching environment, organisms may have to develop entirely new features in a highly complex space of possibilities, which seems very difficult to predict.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Microorganisms as a Test Case&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A metabolic modeling approach is expected to improve characterisation of the constraints that shape evolutionary trajectories, and tools like flux balance analysis can model the molecular physiology of an organism to predict which metabolic pathways will be activated or inactivated under specific environmental conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Bottom Line&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Evolution is neither fully random nor fully deterministic. Predictability is highest when organisms face stable, constrained environments that push them toward specialization — particularly loss of unused functions — and microorganisms are ideal models to experimentally test these predictions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
