        <dc:title>Mycobacterium tuberculosis mutation rate estimates from different lineages predict substantial differences in the emergence of drug-resistant tuberculosis</dc:title>
        <dc:date>7/2013</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>https://www.nature.com/articles/ng.2656</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-30 04:54:30</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://www.springer.com/tdm</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>784-790</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_506">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Summary: Ford et al. (2013), &lt;em&gt;Nature Genetics&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Core Question:&lt;/strong&gt; Why are some &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; strains more prone to developing multidrug resistance (MDR)?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Finding:&lt;/strong&gt; Lineage 2 strains (East Asian/Beijing) have a higher baseline mutation rate than Lineage 4 strains (Euro-American), which directly explains their greater tendency to acquire drug resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main Experiments &amp;amp; Results&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using Luria-Delbrück fluctuation analysis on clinical and lab strains, the authors showed that every Lineage 2 strain acquired rifampicin resistance at a significantly higher rate than every Lineage 4 strain — roughly a 10-fold difference between group means.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They then systematically ruled out alternative explanations: the difference wasn't due to post-antibiotic exposure mutation, differences in drug tolerance, or fitness costs of resistance mutations. While Lineage 2 strains did have a slightly larger mutational target size in the &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; gene, this alone couldn't explain the gap. The elevated resistance rate extended to isoniazid and ethambutol too, pointing to a genuinely higher basal mutation rate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Connecting Lab to Clinical Reality&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;By analysing whole-genome sequences from a real TB outbreak in British Columbia, the authors showed that the in vitro mutation rate closely matched the rate at which bacteria mutated within human patients — supporting a molecular clock of roughly 0.3–0.5 mutations per genome per year.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mathematical Modelling&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A stochastic simulation model incorporating these mutation rates predicted that patients infected with a Lineage 2 strain are approximately &lt;strong&gt;22 times more likely&lt;/strong&gt; to harbour MDR bacteria at the time of diagnosis, even before treatment begins. Bacterial burden at diagnosis was also identified as a critical variable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Implications&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors argue that bacterial mutation rate is an underappreciated risk factor for MDR-TB, alongside the traditionally cited causes like patient non-compliance and inappropriate dosing. They suggest that &lt;strong&gt;early, sensitive diagnosis&lt;/strong&gt; (reducing bacterial burden at detection) and awareness of infecting strain lineage should be incorporated into TB control strategies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
