        <dc:title>Escherichia coli rpoB Mutants Have Increased Evolvability in Proportion to Their Fitness Defects</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Evolvability is the capacity of an organism or population for generating descendants with increased ﬁtness. Simulations and comparative studies have shown that evolvability can vary among individuals and identiﬁed characteristics of genetic architectures that can promote evolvability. However, little is known about how the evolvability of biological organisms typically varies along a lineage at each mutational step in its history. Evolvability might increase upon sustaining a deleterious mutation because there are many compensatory paths in the ﬁtness landscape to reascend the same ﬁtness peak or because shifts to new peaks become possible. We use genetic marker divergence trajectories to parameterize and compare the evolvability—deﬁned as the ﬁtness increase realized by an evolving population initiated from a test genotype—of a series of Escherichia coli mutants on multiple timescales. Each mutant differs from a common progenitor strain by a mutation in the rpoB gene, which encodes the b subunit of RNA polymerase. Strains with larger ﬁtness defects are proportionally more evolvable in terms of both the beneﬁcial mutations accessible in their immediate mutational neighborhoods and integrated over evolutionary paths that traverse multiple beneﬁcial mutations. Our results establish quantitative expectations for how a mutation with a given deleterious ﬁtness effect should inﬂuence evolvability, and they will thus inform future studies of how deleterious, neutral, and beneﬁcial mutations targeting other cellular processes impact the evolutionary potential of microorganisms.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2010-06-01</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/mbe/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/molbev/msq024</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-30 05:58:27</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>1338-1347</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_509">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Background &amp;amp; Approach&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors used eight &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; strains, each carrying a different single mutation in the &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; gene (encoding the β subunit of RNA polymerase). These rifampicin-resistance mutations cause varying degrees of fitness cost in the absence of the antibiotic, providing a clean series of strains with defined, graded fitness defects. Each strain was then evolved for 640 generations across 12 replicate populations, allowing the authors to measure how readily each could improve through natural selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Evolvability was tracked using a clever &lt;strong&gt;marker divergence&lt;/strong&gt; approach: two variants of each strain, distinguishable by a neutral genetic label, were mixed together at the start. As beneficial mutations arose and spread in one marker background, the ratio of the two markers diverged, allowing the authors to infer both the &lt;strong&gt;rate&lt;/strong&gt; at which beneficial mutations arose (μ) and their &lt;strong&gt;selective advantage&lt;/strong&gt; (s) without directly observing individual mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main Findings&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. More damaged strains are more evolvable — proportionally&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The central finding is strikingly clean: strains with larger fitness defects consistently accessed beneficial mutations of larger selective advantage. Across the full range of fitness costs tested, a strain's initial fitness defect was highly predictive of the magnitude of its first successful beneficial mutations (r² = 0.67). Strains with the lowest fitness could access compensatory mutations conferring at least twice the selective advantage available to nearly-neutral mutants.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. The rate of beneficial mutation is unchanged&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Importantly, the &lt;em&gt;rate&lt;/em&gt; at which beneficial mutations arose (μ) did not differ across strains — it was approximately constant at around 6.6 × 10⁻⁸ per generation regardless of fitness defect. This means more-defective strains are not generating more mutations; rather, a larger fraction of the existing mutational landscape is &quot;uphill&quot; for them, and those uphill steps are bigger.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. The relationship holds across multiple timescales&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Looking at fitness gains accumulated over the full 640 generations (through multiple sequential beneficial mutations), the same pattern held even more strongly (r² = 0.86). More defective strains recovered more fitness overall, though in both the short and long term the recovery was incomplete — the slope of the relationship was significantly less than one, meaning increased evolvability never fully compensates for the initial cost.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. A quantitative null model for evolvability&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The precision of the relationship between fitness defect and evolvability was notable. On average, the fitness gap between an evolved mutant and an evolved wild-type strain narrowed to about 66% of the ancestral gap after one beneficial mutation, and to 43% after 640 generations. The authors propose this as a useful &lt;strong&gt;null model&lt;/strong&gt; — a quantitative baseline expectation for how any deleterious mutation of a given size should affect evolvability, against which future studies of other mutations and gene targets can be benchmarked.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Broader Significance&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The results are consistent with classical fitness landscape theory: strains further from a fitness peak simply have more and larger uphill paths available. What is new here is the systematic, quantitative demonstration of this principle across a defined series of mutations in a biological system. The work raises important follow-up questions — for instance, whether mutations in certain genes make organisms disproportionately more or less evolvable than their fitness costs would predict, which has direct relevance to understanding how pathogens like &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; or &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; evolve drug resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
