        <dc:title>Mutation rate plasticity in rifampicin resistance depends on Escherichia coli cell–cell interactions</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Abstract
            
              Variation of mutation rate at a particular site in a particular genotype, in other words mutation rate plasticity (MRP), can be caused by stress or ageing. However, mutation rate control by other factors is less well characterized. Here we show that in wild-type
              Escherichia coli
              (K-12 and B strains), the mutation rate to rifampicin resistance is plastic and inversely related to population density: lowering density can increase mutation rates at least threefold. This MRP is genetically switchable, dependent on the quorum-sensing gene
              luxS
              —specifically its role in the activated methyl cycle—and is socially mediated via cell–cell interactions. Although we identify an inverse association of mutation rate with fitness under some circumstances, we find no functional link with stress-induced mutagenesis. Our experimental manipulation of mutation rates via the social environment raises the possibility that such manipulation occurs in nature and could be exploited medically.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2014-04-29</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms4742</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-05 03:21:13</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>3742</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:2041-1723">
        <prism:volume>5</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Nature Communications</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1038/ncomms4742</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>1</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Nat Commun</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 2041-1723</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_512">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Summary: Krašovec et al. (2014) — Mutation Rate Plasticity in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Background &amp;amp; Central Question&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutation rates have long been treated as fixed properties of a genotype. This paper challenges that assumption by asking: can the rate of mutation at a specific site vary within a single genotype, and if so, what controls it? The authors call this phenomenon &lt;strong&gt;mutation rate plasticity (MRP)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Findings&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;1. Mutation Rate Is Not Fixed — It Varies With Population Density&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using classic Luria-Delbrück fluctuation assays (which count cells that spontaneously become resistant to the antibiotic rifampicin, or Rif^R), the authors demonstrated that mutation rate in wild-type &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; (both K-12 and B strains) is &lt;strong&gt;inversely related to population density&lt;/strong&gt;: as cell density decreases, mutation rate increases. The magnitude of this effect is substantial — &lt;strong&gt;reducing density by ~77% was sufficient to double the mutation rate&lt;/strong&gt;, and overall variation spanned at least threefold.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;2. Density Is the Key Variable — Not Nutrients, Fitness, or Division Number&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To rule out alternative explanations, the authors systematically tested four competing hypotheses. They manipulated glucose concentration, culture volume, growth period, and strain pairings in coculture experiments. After statistical modelling, &lt;strong&gt;only final population density (D)&lt;/strong&gt; emerged as a significant predictor of mutation rate. Glucose concentration, number of cell divisions, and relative competitive fitness were all non-significant — pointing clearly to density itself as the causal factor, likely operating through a signalling mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;3. MRP Is Genetically Controlled by &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The gene &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; encodes an enzyme involved in quorum sensing — the bacterial system by which cells detect how many neighbours they have. A &lt;em&gt;ΔluxS&lt;/em&gt; deletion mutant grew to the same density as wild-type but &lt;strong&gt;completely lost the density-dependent mutation rate response&lt;/strong&gt;; its mutation rate slope against density was not significantly different from zero. Wild-type cells, by contrast, showed the expected inverse relationship. This established MRP as a &lt;strong&gt;genetically switchable&lt;/strong&gt; phenomenon.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;4. The Effect Is Through the Activated Methyl Cycle — Not AI-2 Quorum Sensing&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; has two known roles: (a) producing the quorum-sensing signal &lt;strong&gt;AI-2&lt;/strong&gt; (via DPD), and (b) participating in the &lt;strong&gt;activated methyl cycle&lt;/strong&gt;, which regulates methyl group availability in the cell. The authors performed complementation experiments to distinguish between these:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Adding &lt;strong&gt;synthetic DPD&lt;/strong&gt; (the precursor to AI-2) to low-density cultures did &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; restore normal MRP — in fact, mutation rate slightly &lt;em&gt;increased&lt;/em&gt; with added DPD, the opposite of what an AI-2-mediated mechanism would predict.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Adding &lt;strong&gt;aspartate&lt;/strong&gt; (which complements the metabolic/methyl cycle defects of &lt;em&gt;ΔluxS&lt;/em&gt;) &lt;strong&gt;fully restored&lt;/strong&gt; the density-dependent mutation rate response in the mutant.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This conclusively identified the &lt;strong&gt;metabolic role of LuxS in the activated methyl cycle&lt;/strong&gt; — not its role in AI-2 quorum sensing — as the key driver of MRP.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;5. MRP Is Mediated Through Cell–Cell Interactions&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a striking experiment, wild-type K-12 cells (marked with chloramphenicol resistance) were cocultured with either wild-type or &lt;em&gt;ΔluxS&lt;/em&gt; neighbours at the &lt;em&gt;same overall population density&lt;/em&gt;. The mutation rate of the marked wild-type cells was significantly higher when cocultured with &lt;em&gt;ΔluxS&lt;/em&gt; mutants than with wild-type cells — &lt;strong&gt;over a third higher on average&lt;/strong&gt;. This effect was again reversed by aspartate supplementation. This proves that MRP is not just an internal cellular response to density, but is transmitted &lt;strong&gt;through the social/chemical environment between cells&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;6. MRP Is Not Stress-Induced Mutagenesis&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A key alternative explanation — that raised mutation rates at low density reflect stress-induced mutagenesis via error-prone DNA polymerases PolIV and PolV (encoded by &lt;em&gt;dinB&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;umuC&lt;/em&gt;) — was examined and largely ruled out:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Transcription of &lt;em&gt;dinB&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;umuC&lt;/em&gt; was similar in wild-type and &lt;em&gt;ΔluxS&lt;/em&gt; strains at high density (where mutation rates differ most between the two).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Analysis of published expression data showed that the general stress response (RpoS regulon) and error-prone polymerase genes are &lt;em&gt;positively&lt;/em&gt; correlated with density-dependent expression — the opposite of what stress-induced mutagenesis would predict.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Existing literature confirms that in glucose minimal media, RpoS expression is not functionally coupled to population density.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Proposed Mechanistic Hypothesis&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose that variation in &lt;strong&gt;Dam methylase activity&lt;/strong&gt; is the most plausible mechanism. The activated methyl cycle (regulated by LuxS) controls the availability of methyl groups used in DNA methylation. The &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; gene — where mutations confer Rif^R — contains two key positions that are adenines within &lt;strong&gt;GATC sites&lt;/strong&gt;, which are specifically methylated by Dam methylase and are known mutational hot spots. At high density, the activated methyl cycle functions normally, Dam methylation is maintained, and mutation rates are low. At low density (or when &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt; is absent), this pathway is disrupted, methylation may decrease, and mutation rates rise.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Broader Implications&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper concludes with two major implications:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Evolutionary:&lt;/strong&gt; The direction of MRP (higher mutation rates at lower density/fitness) is consistent with theoretical models suggesting elevated mutation rates are advantageous when populations are displaced from adaptive peaks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Medical:&lt;/strong&gt; When &lt;em&gt;luxS&lt;/em&gt;-dependent quorum sensing is disrupted in a mixed bacterial population (as quorum-sensing inhibitors used as anti-virulence drugs would do), the emergence of &lt;em&gt;de novo&lt;/em&gt; antibiotic resistance in bystander cells is &lt;strong&gt;boosted&lt;/strong&gt;. This is a potentially serious unintended consequence of quorum-sensing-based therapies. Conversely, increasing cell–cell interactions (higher density of interacting cells) could potentially be exploited to &lt;em&gt;slow&lt;/em&gt; the emergence of antibiotic resistance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
