        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_516"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_514"/>
        <dc:title>Quantifying stochastic establishment of mutants in microbial adaptation</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Studies of microbial evolution, especially in applied contexts, have focused on the role of selection in shaping predictable, adaptive responses to the environment. However, chance events – the appearance of novel genetic variants and their establishment, i.e. outgrowth from a single cell to a sizeable population – also play critical initiating roles in adaptation. Stochasticity in establishment has received little attention in microbiology, potentially due to lack of awareness as well as practical challenges in quantification. However, methods for high-replicate culturing, mutant labelling and detection, and statistical inference now make it feasible to experimentally quantify the establishment probability of specific adaptive genotypes. I review methods that have emerged over the past decade, including experimental design and mathematical formulas to estimate establishment probability from data. Quantifying establishment in further biological settings and comparing empirical estimates to theoretical predictions represent exciting future directions. More broadly, recognition that adaptive genotypes may be stochastically lost while rare is significant both for interpreting common lab assays and for designing interventions to promote or inhibit microbial evolution.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-08-10</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.001365</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-06 01:16:58</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_516">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Summary: Alexander (2023), &lt;em&gt;Microbiology&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&quot;Quantifying stochastic establishment of mutants in microbial adaptation&quot;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a methodological review paper written by Helen K. Alexander — the same first author as the 2020 PNAS paper — published three years later. Where the 2020 paper was an experimental study, this paper steps back to synthesize the broader field, argue for the importance of stochastic establishment as a concept, and provide a practical guide to how it can be measured. It serves as both a retrospective on the 2020 work and a forward-looking manifesto for the field.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Core Argument&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper opens with a key observation: microbiology has, for good reasons, focused heavily on &lt;strong&gt;selection&lt;/strong&gt; as the driver of adaptive evolution. Large population sizes and bulk culture conditions make selection highly visible and repeatable. However, this focus has obscured an equally fundamental layer of randomness: even if a beneficial mutation arises and is selectively favoured, &lt;strong&gt;it is not guaranteed to survive&lt;/strong&gt;. Any individual cell may die or fail to divide before producing enough descendants to form a lasting lineage — a process called &lt;strong&gt;demographic stochasticity&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Alexander argues that this stochastic &lt;strong&gt;establishment&lt;/strong&gt; step (the outgrowth of a single mutant cell into a large, detectable population) has been almost entirely overlooked in applied microbiology, despite being well-studied theoretically in population genetics for nearly a century. The review's purpose is to close this gap by making establishment probability a measurable, experimentally accessible quantity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Why Establishment Matters&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper makes several arguments for why establishment deserves attention beyond theoretical interest:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Establishment shapes evolutionary trajectories.&lt;/strong&gt; When multiple adaptive mutations are possible, the first to establish can set the population on a particular evolutionary path, with all subsequent evolution contingent on this early event. Both mutation rate and establishment probability jointly determine which genotype &quot;wins&quot; the race to adapt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Establishment is critical under time pressure.&lt;/strong&gt; In declining populations — for instance, a pathogen being cleared by antibiotic treatment — adaptation must happen fast. Rapid establishment of resistant cells is the key to &quot;evolutionary rescue,&quot; preventing extinction. Slow or failed establishment means the infection is cleared.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Haldane's classical result.&lt;/strong&gt; In the 1920s, Haldane showed mathematically that the establishment probability of a weakly beneficial mutation is approximately twice its selective advantage — meaning even strongly favoured mutations fail to establish most of the time. This result is well known in population genetics but has not been translated into microbiology practice.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Growth rate assays are insufficient.&lt;/strong&gt; Critically, conventional competition assays measuring relative fitness or growth rate in large populations &lt;strong&gt;cannot predict establishment probability&lt;/strong&gt;. The same net growth rate can be achieved with very different ratios of birth and death rates, but a strain with higher mortality is less likely to establish. Density-dependent or frequency-dependent fitness further complicates extrapolation from large-population measures to single-cell outcomes. A tailored assay is needed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Why Establishment Has Been Neglected&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Alexander identifies both conceptual and methodological barriers:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Disciplinary divides&lt;/strong&gt;: Population genetics theoreticians have studied establishment for decades, but this work hasn't translated well to applied microbiologists.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Invisible in bulk culture&lt;/strong&gt;: Demographic stochasticity is irrelevant when populations are large; individual lineages are not visible.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mindset mismatch&lt;/strong&gt;: Microbiology typically equates &quot;replication&quot; with &quot;reproducibility&quot; — running a few replicates and expecting consistent outcomes. Studying stochastic events requires running &lt;em&gt;many&lt;/em&gt; replicates and &lt;em&gt;embracing&lt;/em&gt; qualitatively different outcomes (growth vs. no growth). Luria and Delbrück made this point for mutations; Alexander argues it applies equally to establishment.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Technical barriers&lt;/strong&gt;: High replication is laborious; mutant detection is not always straightforward; the statistical analysis is unfamiliar to many.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, Alexander argues these barriers have now largely been eliminated. Modern tools — 96-well plates, multichannel pipettes, lab automation, flow cytometry, fluorescence microscopy, plate readers — make high-replicate experiments feasible. Statistical methods and code-sharing platforms exist. The time is ripe to expand this work.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Three Methods for Estimating Establishment Probability&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The heart of the paper is a practical review of three experimental approaches, unified by a shared mathematical framework.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All three methods share common requirements: a mutant strain of interest, the ability to distinguish it from wild-type, high experimental replication, an estimate of inoculum size (how many lineages were initiated), and a way to detect sufficiently large populations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;Method 1: Colony Counts on Agar&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The most direct approach. Mutant cells are plated on solid agar — in a baseline (benign) environment and in test environments (e.g. containing antibiotics). Each visible colony is assumed to derive from one cell and represent one established lineage. Establishment probability is simply:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;p_e(x) = C_x / C_0&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where C_x is colony count in test environment x, and C_0 is colony count in baseline. This is sometimes called &quot;plating efficiency.&quot; It requires minimal equipment and is highly intuitive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitation&lt;/strong&gt;: Sampling error in both C_0 and C_x propagates into the estimate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;Method 2: Sector Counts in Expanding Colonies&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Used primarily to study spatial structure and genetic drift. Mutant and wild-type cells are mixed (mutant at very low frequency) and inoculated at the centre of an agar plate, which is then incubated to allow colony expansion. Mutant lineages that &quot;surf&quot; the expanding colony front form visible coloured or fluorescent &lt;strong&gt;sectors&lt;/strong&gt; radiating outward. Each sector corresponds to one established lineage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Establishment probability is estimated analogously:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;p_e(x) = N_sec(x) / N_0&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where N_sec is the number of mutant sectors and N_0 is the estimated mutant inoculum size (often restricted to the outermost cell layer, since only cells at the colony front have a meaningful chance of establishing).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This method has been used to study how physical traits, mechanical cell-cell interactions, and spatial position influence genetic drift — particularly relevant to biofilms and compact populations. The Aif et al. (2022) &lt;em&gt;Nature Communications&lt;/em&gt; and Schreck et al. (2023) &lt;em&gt;PNAS&lt;/em&gt; papers cited in the review extend this to questions of evolutionary rescue in spatial structures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitation&lt;/strong&gt;: Requires microscopy for fluorescence detection; inoculation position is critical and must be carefully controlled.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;Method 3: Inference from Outgrowth in Liquid (the &quot;Seeding Assay&quot;)&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the method used by Alexander &amp;amp; MacLean (2020). Cells are diluted to very low density and inoculated into many replicate liquid culture wells (typically 96-well plates). Growth is detected by optical density; in co-cultures, mutants are identified by fluorescence or selective plating. Because multiple lineages in a well cannot be visually distinguished, establishment must be inferred statistically.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The key mathematical framework proceeds in two steps:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Population-level establishment&lt;/strong&gt; — the fraction of wells showing growth — is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;P(x) = n_e(x) / n_tot(x)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where n_e is the number of wells with detectable growth. This is directly observable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To extract &lt;strong&gt;per-cell establishment probability&lt;/strong&gt; p_e from this population-level measure, one needs to account for variable inoculum size. Assuming inoculum sizes follow a Poisson distribution (validated empirically) and that cells establish independently, the relationship is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;P(x) = 1 − exp(−N̄_0 · p_e(x))&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Solving for p_e gives the key formula:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;p_e(x) = −ln(1 − P(x)) / N̄_0&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where N̄_0 (mean inoculum size) is estimated from growth in antibiotic-free media. Confidence intervals can be computed by joint likelihood inference or bootstrapping.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Advantages&lt;/strong&gt;: Most amenable to automation and very high replication; does not require sophisticated microscopy. &lt;strong&gt;Limitation&lt;/strong&gt;: Less intuitive; multiple lineages cannot be resolved; requires mathematical inference.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Statistical Framework and Model Validation&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The review emphasises that the independence assumption (each cell establishes independently, with probability unaffected by inoculum size) is testable. If cells cooperate (e.g. collectively inactivating antibiotics), per-cell p_e increases with inoculum size; if they compete for resources or secrete toxins, it decreases. Alexander &amp;amp; MacLean (2020) tested and validated the independence assumption across three orders of magnitude of inoculum size in their &lt;em&gt;P. aeruginosa&lt;/em&gt; system.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The review also notes that the same mathematical structure — P = 1 − exp(−N̄·p_e) — appears across many biological contexts: the &quot;P0 method&quot; for estimating mutation rate, transmission probability in infectious disease epidemiology, and evolutionary rescue models. This unifying structure makes the framework broadly applicable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Practical Implications of Stochastic Establishment&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Alexander argues that establishment probability is not just theoretically interesting — it quietly affects the interpretation of many standard lab assays:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Selective plating and conjugation rate estimates.&lt;/strong&gt; When co-cultured strains are distinguished by antibiotic resistance markers and enumerated by selective plating, if antibiotics reduce establishment of even &quot;resistant&quot; marker strains, only a fraction of cells form colonies. A cited study found that 99% of doubly resistant transconjugants failed to form colonies on selective plates, meaning conjugation rates would be heavily underestimated without correcting for this.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Fluctuation assays.&lt;/strong&gt; Fluctuation assays estimate mutation rates by exploiting the statistical distribution of mutant numbers across replicate cultures. However, if some spontaneous mutants stochastically fail to establish detectable colonies (because their establishment probability is &amp;lt;1), mutation rates will be underestimated. This is an underappreciated source of bias.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. MIC assays.&lt;/strong&gt; MIC is typically assessed from an inoculum of 5×10⁵ cells/mL. Because P(growth) increases with inoculum size, MIC is an emergent property of population size rather than a pure measure of single-cell susceptibility. Using establishment probability directly (e.g. plating efficiency) — for instance, defining IC99 as the concentration reducing colony counts by 99% — would give a more fundamental characterisation of antibiotic susceptibility.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Future Directions&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The review concludes with an expansive discussion of open directions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Expanding biological systems&lt;/strong&gt;: Most existing work has been done in a handful of bacterial and fungal species. The framework could be applied to any experimentally tractable microbe.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Chromosome vs. plasmid&lt;/strong&gt;: A recent study compared establishment of resistance alleles on chromosomes versus multicopy plasmids — a directly relevant extension given the role of plasmids in horizontal gene transfer.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Temporally and spatially varying environments&lt;/strong&gt;: Rare mutants are especially vulnerable to stochastic extinction during transient adverse conditions. Time-varying antibiotic concentrations (reflecting realistic pharmacokinetics) represent a key unresolved extension flagged by Alexander &amp;amp; MacLean (2020).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unpacking the &quot;black box&quot;&lt;/strong&gt;: The seeding assay gives an endpoint measure without revealing what happened inside each well. Time-lapse microscopy in microfluidic droplets or on agar pads could directly observe cell-level dynamics, allowing empirical estimates of establishment probability to be compared with theoretical birth-death models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Machine learning to identify predictive cell traits&lt;/strong&gt;: Single-cell phenotypic heterogeneity — differences in metabolic state, gene expression, membrane properties — may explain which cells establish and which don't. Machine learning applied to single-cell data could link observable traits to establishment outcomes.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Antibiotic dosing design&lt;/strong&gt;: Conventional dosing strategy focuses on selecting concentration ranges outside the Mutant Selection Window. Establishment probability suggests an orthogonal strategy: concentrations that select &lt;em&gt;for&lt;/em&gt; resistance may nonetheless drive rare resistant cells extinct by keeping per-cell establishment probability very low. Dosing frequency also matters — modelling suggests that frequent small doses achieve greater average population decline, while infrequent large doses may be more effective at eradicating small partially resistant populations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Bottom Line&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This review is Alexander's attempt to formally establish &quot;stochastic establishment&quot; as a distinct, measurable, and important component of microbial evolutionary biology — one that complements the well-studied concepts of mutation rate and selection. It synthesises a decade of scattered experimental work, provides a unified mathematical treatment with simple formulas accessible to non-specialists, and makes the practical argument that establishment probability belongs in the toolkit of anyone studying antibiotic resistance, experimental evolution, or microbial adaptation more broadly. It reads as both a retrospective synthesis of the 2020 PNAS paper and a call to action for the field.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
