        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1742-5689, 1742-5662</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_527">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Synthesized mutation fixation probability research findings&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Synthesized mutation fixation probability research findings&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Summary: Patwa &amp;amp; Wahl (2008) — &quot;The Fixation Probability of Beneficial Mutations&quot;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Purpose and Scope&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a review paper that traces the mathematical history of fixation probability — the chance that a novel beneficial allele will ultimately reach 100% frequency in a population — from its origins in the 1920s through to contemporary work. The authors are motivated by an emerging possibility: that experimental microbial evolution has finally advanced to the point where these long-standing theoretical predictions could be empirically tested. The review is organized around relaxing the increasingly unrealistic assumptions of early models.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Core Concept: Why Fixation Requires Stochastic Treatment&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a purely deterministic model, any beneficial mutation would inevitably fix, since it increases in frequency every generation. In reality, when a mutation first arises it exists as a single copy, and random fluctuations — genetic drift — can easily eliminate it before selection has a chance to act. The fixation probability is therefore essentially equivalent to the probability of surviving genetic drift while rare. Once the mutant lineage becomes sufficiently numerous, deterministic selection takes over and extinction becomes negligible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The selective advantage s is defined such that if wild-type individuals have on average W offspring, mutants have W(1+s) offspring.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Historical Foundations (Section 2)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three mathematical frameworks have been used to compute fixation probabilities:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Markov chain approaches&lt;/strong&gt; enumerate the population state exactly, but are only computationally feasible for small populations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Branching process approaches&lt;/strong&gt; (following Haldane and Fisher) treat each mutant lineage as independent, assuming the wild-type population is large enough that mutant fates don't interact. These are the most widely used.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Diffusion approximations&lt;/strong&gt; (following Wright, Fisher, and especially Kimura) model gene frequency as a continuous variable changing gradually over time, applicable when selection is weak and populations are large.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Haldane's result&lt;/strong&gt; is the most celebrated: using a discrete-time branching process with Poisson-distributed offspring, he showed that for a weakly beneficial mutation arising as a single copy in a large, stable population, the fixation probability is approximately p ≈ 2s. This elegantly simple result implies that even favoured mutations fail to fix the vast majority of the time — a mutation with a 1% advantage fixes only ~2% of the time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A key hidden assumption in Haldane's model is that individuals cannot die independently of reproduction; death only occurs by having zero offspring. Since offspring number is Poisson-distributed, the probability of zero offspring is completely determined by the mean, leaving no room to independently specify a survival probability. This becomes important in later, more realistic models.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kimura's diffusion result&lt;/strong&gt; generalised Haldane's work to account for effective population size Ne (which captures deviations from an ideal population due to unequal sex ratios, non-random mating, etc.). His formula gives p ≈ 2sNe/N for a single-copy mutation in a large diploid population, reducing to Haldane's p ≈ 2s when Ne = N.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Populations of Changing Size (Section 3)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Growing and declining populations.&lt;/strong&gt; Fisher suggested, and later work confirmed, that fixation probability increases in growing populations and decreases in declining ones. This is intuitive: a growing population gives a mutant lineage more chances to expand before it might be lost.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cyclic population sizes.&lt;/strong&gt; Ewens showed that for populations cycling through a sequence of sizes, the fixation probability is p ≈ 2s × (harmonic mean of sizes / arithmetic mean of sizes). When sizes vary considerably, p can be much less than 2s, meaning constant population size is more favourable for mutant survival.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Population bottlenecks.&lt;/strong&gt; This section is particularly relevant to experimental evolution. Wahl &amp;amp; Gerrish (2001) modelled serial passaging — where populations grow for a fixed period and are then randomly sampled down to their initial size. They found that the fixation probability drops rapidly for mutations arising late in the growth phase, because the mutant lineage doesn't have time to grow large enough to survive the bottleneck. Heffernan &amp;amp; Wahl (2002) added the effect of genetic drift during the growth phase (not just at bottlenecks), finding a further reduction of more than 25% in fixation probability compared to the bottleneck-only model. When selection is weak and a mutation arises at the start of a growth phase, the fixation probability approximates p ≈ s(k−1), where k is the number of generations between bottlenecks — analogous to Haldane's result but scaled by the growth phase length.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dynamically changing population sizes.&lt;/strong&gt; Lambert (2006) and Parsons &amp;amp; Quince (2007a,b) introduced birth-death models where population size changes continuously according to underlying demographic events rather than being externally imposed. A key finding from Lambert is that Haldane's factor of 2 in p ≈ 2s specifically arises from the Poisson offspring assumption; more generally, p ≈ 2s/σ², where σ² is the variance of the offspring distribution. Higher reproductive variance always reduces fixation probability. Lambert also found that Haldane's and Kimura's approximations tend to underestimate fixation probability in growing populations and overestimate it in declining ones.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Parsons &amp;amp; Quince extended this without assuming weak selection, finding that for advantageous mutants, wild-type population fluctuations do not affect fixation probability, but that life-history trade-offs (e.g. between birth rate and death rate when carrying capacities are equal) do matter — mutants with higher birth rates have larger population fluctuations and therefore reduced fixation probability when the population is at carrying capacity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Subdivided Populations (Section 4)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A large body of work asks how spatial structure — populations divided into demes with migration between them — affects fixation probability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Early work by Pollak and Maruyama showed that for symmetric migration, fixation probability in a subdivided population equals that in an unstructured population of the same total size. Lande showed that the net rate of evolution in a subdivided population equals the rate in a single deme, provided fixed mutations spread by random extinction and recolonization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Slatkin found that fixation probability is bounded between the unstructured-population result and the result assuming independent fixation in each deme, and that the relationship with migration rate depends on whether the heterozygote is more or less fit than the average homozygote.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Later work by Whitlock relaxed many of these assumptions to allow arbitrary dominance, heterogeneous selection, and asymmetric migration. In a &quot;differentially productive environment,&quot; effective population size is reduced relative to census size, which enhances fixation of deleterious alleles while reducing it for beneficial ones. Crucially, Whitlock &amp;amp; Gomulkiewicz found that heterogeneous selection among demes never reduces (and sometimes substantially enhances) fixation probability compared to homogeneous selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cherry showed that local extinction and recolonization events always reduce fixation probability for beneficial alleles, and derived effective population size and effective selection coefficients so that standard unstructured-population results can be applied.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Multiple Segregating Alleles (Section 5)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Linked deleterious alleles.&lt;/strong&gt; When beneficial mutations arise on chromosomes that also carry deleterious mutations, their fixation probability is reduced. Charlesworth showed that deleterious linked loci reduce effective population size by a factor equal to the frequency of mutation-free individuals. Barton extended this to show that tightly linked deleterious mutations, substitutions, and fluctuating polymorphisms each reduce fixation probability in ways that can't simply be captured by an effective population size correction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quasi-species fixation.&lt;/strong&gt; In organisms with very high mutation rates (particularly RNA viruses), offspring are very likely to mutate before fixation occurs. In this regime, the fitness of an invading lineage depends not just on the parent mutant's fitness, but on the average fitness of its entire cloud of mutant descendants. Wilke (2003) derived a quasi-species generalisation of Haldane's result using multitype branching processes: if an invading sequence gets no support from mutational neighbours, the result reduces exactly to Haldane's p ≈ 2s; but if neighbouring sequences also contribute offspring, fixation probability increases even if some individual sequences have negative growth rates. The authors note, however, that Wilke's model retains Haldane's assumptions of discrete, non-overlapping generations and Poisson offspring — assumptions that are poorly suited to viral life histories.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Clonal interference.&lt;/strong&gt; In asexual populations, multiple beneficial mutations can arise independently in different lineages and compete with one another, potentially preventing any single mutation from fixing. Gerrish &amp;amp; Lenski (1998) provided the first formal treatment: a focal mutation only fixes if no superior mutation arises elsewhere and survives drift before the focal mutation fixes. The most striking prediction is that at high mutation rates, clonal interference imposes a &quot;speed limit&quot; on adaptation. Later work noted a complication: the focal mutation may itself arise on the genetic background of a previously segregating beneficial mutation, leading to a &quot;multiple mutation&quot; regime intermediate between clonal interference and quasi-species dynamics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Life-History Models (Section 6)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This section is arguably the most important for connecting theory to microbial experiments. All previous models effectively assume that beneficial mutations increase average offspring number (fecundity mutants). But mutations can confer advantages through other mechanisms — for instance by reducing generation time (generation time mutants) or by reducing death rate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wahl &amp;amp; DeHaan (2004) showed that for generation time mutants, p ≈ s/ln(2) in a constant population, whereas for fecundity mutants p ≈ 2s. Assuming all mutations affect fecundity thus overestimates fixation probability by a factor of roughly 1.4 for generation time mutants.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &quot;burst-death model&quot; by Hubbarde, Wild &amp;amp; Wahl (2007) and Alexander &amp;amp; Wahl (2008) — notably the same Helen Alexander who authored the first paper — introduces a continuous-time birth-death framework where each individual faces a constant death rate and a constant burst rate (producing multiple offspring simultaneously). This allows birth and death rates to vary independently, unlike Poisson models where zero-offspring probability is entirely constrained by the mean. The key finding is that mutations decreasing the death rate (increasing survival) are the most likely to fix, followed by mutations increasing the burst rate, followed by mutations increasing burst size. This demonstrates clearly that the mechanism by which a mutation is beneficial, not just its effect on mean growth rate, critically determines fixation probability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;From Theory to Experiment (Section 7)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors note that despite almost a century of theory, the fixation probability of a specific beneficial mutation had never been experimentally measured at the time of writing. They argue this is finally achievable with modern microbial evolution techniques (high-replicate 96-well plate protocols, controlled serial passaging), and that doing so would be a landmark validation of long-standing predictions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, they caution that existing models are not sufficiently tailored to real microbial life histories. Bacteria and viruses are not well modelled by discrete non-overlapping generations or Poisson offspring distributions, and recent work has demonstrated that fixation probabilities are extremely sensitive to exactly these assumptions. They call for organism-specific theoretical models — for example, bacterial fission models for bacterial experiments, or models incorporating viral attachment, eclipse times, and burst/lysis events for viral experiments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A final challenge they raise is the weak-selection assumption: most theoretical results assume s is small enough that s² terms are negligible, but experimental evolution of microbes has documented beneficial mutations with s on the order of 10 or more — far outside the regime where these approximations hold.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Overall Message&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper presents fixation probability as a problem where theory has vastly outpaced experiment. The field has progressed from Haldane's elegant but restrictive p ≈ 2s through increasingly realistic treatments of fluctuating population sizes, spatial structure, competing alleles, and organism-specific life histories. The unifying message across all these extensions is that fixation probability is far more sensitive to biological details — particularly how exactly a mutation confers its advantage and what the organism's life history looks like — than the simplicity of p ≈ 2s might suggest. Bridging remaining gaps between theory and experiment in microbial systems is presented as both urgent and, finally, technically feasible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
