        <dc:title>Population biological principles of drug-resistance evolution in infectious diseases</dc:title>
        <dc:date>03/2011</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1473309910702644</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-19 07:34:40</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>236-247</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:14733099">
        <prism:volume>11</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>The Lancet Infectious Diseases</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1016/S1473-3099(10)70264-4</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>3</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>The Lancet Infectious Diseases</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 14733099</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_531">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Summary: &quot;Population Biological Principles of Drug-Resistance Evolution in Infectious Diseases&quot; (zur Wiesch et al., 2011)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Overview&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This review, published in &lt;em&gt;The Lancet Infectious Diseases&lt;/em&gt;, synthesises population biology and evolutionary theory to explain how drug resistance emerges and spreads across pathogens like HIV, influenza, &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;S. aureus&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;P. falciparum&lt;/em&gt;. The central argument is that while underlying genetic mechanisms differ greatly between pathogens, the evolutionary dynamics of resistance share generic, transferable principles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Emergence of Resistance Within a Host&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors frame within-host resistance emergence as a multi-phase process:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phase 1 — Emergence (stochastic)&lt;/strong&gt; Before treatment begins, resistant mutants may already exist in the pathogen population at low levels, maintained by a balance of mutation rate (μ), immigration of resistant strains from outside, and the fitness cost of resistance in a drug-free environment (sA). If no resistant mutants pre-exist, their first appearance is a chance event governed purely by mutation or immigration rates and effective population size (Ne) — not by drug selection pressure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phase 2 — Establishment (stochastic)&lt;/strong&gt; Once a resistant mutant appears, it must grow beyond a threshold where random extinction becomes unlikely. The probability of survival scales with the selection coefficient in the presence of drugs (sP) and the number of copies already present. A classic population genetics result gives the fixation probability of a single advantageous mutant as approximately 2sP/Ne.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phase 3 — Increase (deterministic)&lt;/strong&gt; Once established, resistant strains increase in a largely predictable, deterministic fashion. The time to dominance is proportional to 1/sP — so even small differences in sP strongly affect how quickly resistance takes over. Mutation rate and initial frequency of resistant mutants must change by orders of magnitude to produce comparable effects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phase 4 — Equilibrium&lt;/strong&gt; The resistant strain may drive the sensitive strain to extinction, or the two may coexist. Coexistence can be maintained by back-mutation, immigration of sensitive strains, drug-free sanctuary sites, or negative frequency-dependent selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Continued Evolution: Epistasis and Compensatory Mutations&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After the first resistance mutation establishes, further mutations can arise:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Additive mutations&lt;/strong&gt; — a second resistance mutation independently adds to fitness under drug pressure and eventually sweeps through the population.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Positive epistasis&lt;/strong&gt; — two mutations confer substantial resistance only when combined; neither is effective alone.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Negative-sign epistasis&lt;/strong&gt; — each mutation is beneficial alone, but together they reduce fitness, creating complex evolutionary dynamics where the order of mutation acquisition matters greatly.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Clonal interference&lt;/strong&gt; — if two independent resistance mutations arise in wild-type backgrounds without recombination, they compete with each other and only one typically prevails.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compensatory mutations&lt;/strong&gt; — resistance mutations typically carry fitness costs. Compensatory mutations that restore fitness can arise and establish once resistance is common, making reversion to drug sensitivity increasingly unlikely, particularly if the pathway back passes through a fitness valley.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; cefotaxime example illustrates this complexity: five point mutations are needed for full resistance, but only a few mutational pathways are selectively accessible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Reversion of Resistance in the Absence of Drugs&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The decline of resistance after drug pressure is removed mirrors the rise, but tends to be considerably slower because:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The selective advantage of sensitive strains in the absence of drugs (sA) is typically much smaller than the advantage of resistant strains under drug pressure (sP).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
If the sensitive strain went extinct during treatment, it must re-emerge via back-mutation or immigration — a slow stochastic process.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compensatory mutations may have already reduced the fitness cost of resistance, making it stable even without drug pressure.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sanctuary sites (e.g., latently infected cells in HIV, bacterial persisters) can harbour resistant strains indefinitely.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Resistance genes may be genetically linked to other genes under positive selection, further stabilising them.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A notable exception is chloroquine-resistant malaria in Malawi, where resistance did decline relatively quickly after drug withdrawal — attributed to weak selection pressure and strong competition.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4. Mechanisms of Resistance Acquisition&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The review distinguishes three genetic routes to resistance:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutation&lt;/strong&gt; — universal across all pathogens discussed. Mutation rates vary by orders of magnitude (e.g., influenza ~10⁻⁵/replication vs. &lt;em&gt;P. falciparum&lt;/em&gt; ~10⁻¹¹–10⁻²⁰/replication). Importantly, intermediate mutation rates may be most dangerous — high enough to generate resistance but not so high as to cause lethal accumulation of deleterious mutations (mutational meltdown).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recombination&lt;/strong&gt; — can combine pre-existing resistance alleles but does not generate new ones. Mathematical models show recombination accelerates adaptation only under specific conditions (negative epistasis and intermediate stochasticity). For HIV, these conditions are unlikely to be met, suggesting recombination plays a minor role in resistance evolution more broadly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Horizontal gene transfer (HGT)&lt;/strong&gt; — critical in bacteria but absent in viruses and eukaryotic parasites. HGT allows resistance genes to spread rapidly across species. Plasmids often carry multiple resistance genes, so selection for one drug can inadvertently select for resistance to others. &lt;em&gt;S. aureus&lt;/em&gt; vancomycin resistance and NDM-1 spread are given as prominent examples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;5. Within-Host vs. Between-Host Dynamics&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors highlight important differences across scales:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Within-host:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Temporal heterogeneity (e.g., missed doses, pharmacokinetic variation) and spatial heterogeneity (e.g., differential tissue penetration) both facilitate resistance evolution.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Phenotypic resistance from dormant subpopulations (HIV latent reservoirs, bacterial persisters) prolongs treatment and creates protected compartments from which resistance mutations can emerge.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The immune system can substantially narrow conditions under which resistance evolves, by killing pathogens and reducing population sizes — though sanctuary sites like abscesses may counteract this.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Commensal microflora is a major reservoir of resistance mutations in bacterial diseases.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Between-host:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Population structure (e.g., community vs. hospital, treated vs. untreated hosts, human vs. animal reservoirs) strongly shapes resistance spread.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The equivalent of within-host mutation at the between-host level is a combined process of mutation and selection — partly stochastic for small within-host populations, nearly deterministic for large ones.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Nested models that simultaneously capture both within- and between-host dynamics are needed for full characterisation, though rarely used in practice.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;6. Treatment Strategies&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors organise strategies at two levels:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Within-Host Strategies (Table 2)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Strategy&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rationale&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Adequate drug dosing&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Suboptimal concentrations allow intermediate-resistance mutants to survive and evolve further&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Start treatment early&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Small pathogen populations reduce the probability of resistance mutations existing&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Treat for an appropriate duration&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Too short risks relapse and accumulation of resistance; too long imposes sustained selection pressure&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Combination therapy&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Requires pathogens to acquire multiple independent mutations simultaneously — drastically reduces probability&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Maintain adherence&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Missed doses create transient suboptimal concentrations that mimic suboptimal dosing&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Between-Host Strategies (Tables 3–4)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Strategy&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rationale&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Limit treatment to necessary cases&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reduces overall selection pressure; antibiotic consumption and resistance emergence are positively correlated&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reduce transmission&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Minimising transmission disproportionately reduces resistant infections, since incoming patients more commonly carry sensitive strains&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mixing (assigning patients to different antibiotics)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Creates fine-scale environmental heterogeneity; generally outperforms cycling&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cycling (rotating formularies)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;May be useful in epidemic contexts; neutralises early dominant mutations before most infections occur&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prophylaxis&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Effective only if transmission of resistant strains is sufficiently low; can backfire if resistant strains transmit easily&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The review cautions that &lt;strong&gt;&quot;hitting hard and early&quot;&lt;/strong&gt; — the classic clinical dictum — has solid theoretical support mainly for point-mutation-based resistance. It is less well-justified for plasmid-borne resistance, phenotypic resistance, or mechanisms such as drug-degrading enzymes and efflux pumps.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;7. Conclusions and Knowledge Gaps&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors call for:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Expanded population genetic theory&lt;/strong&gt; covering plasmid-borne, phenotypic, and non-target-alteration mechanisms of resistance — these are currently undermodelled.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nested models&lt;/strong&gt; that integrate within- and between-host dynamics for a more realistic picture of resistance spread.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cross-pathogen frameworks&lt;/strong&gt; to generalise findings across diseases rather than treating each pathogen in isolation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Collaboration between theoreticians and clinicians&lt;/strong&gt; to improve statistical power of clinical treatment studies.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Lessons from cancer biology&lt;/strong&gt; — since tumour resistance evolves entirely de novo (no transmission between hosts), comparing antimicrobial and anticancer resistance could isolate the role of transmission in resistance development.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Takeaway&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite enormous differences between pathogens in their biology, genetics, and clinical context, resistance evolution follows shared population-biological rules: it is governed by selection coefficients, effective population sizes, mutation and immigration rates, and the structure of fitness landscapes. Understanding and applying these generic principles — rather than treating each pathogen in isolation — offers the most promising path toward rational, sustainable drug use.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
