        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_532"/>
        <dc:title>MUTATIONS OF BACTERIA FROM VIRUS SENSITIVITY TO VIRUS RESISTANCE</dc:title>
        <dc:date>1943-11-20</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/genetics/article/28/6/491/6033179</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-20 01:02:36</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>https://academic.oup.com/journals/pages/open_access/funder_policies/chorus/standard_publication_model</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>491-511</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_560">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;What Luria &amp;amp; Delbrück (1943) Say About Jackpot Mutations&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the paper that &lt;strong&gt;invented&lt;/strong&gt; the concept of the jackpot distribution, so it is worth understanding exactly how they arrived at it and what they claimed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Starting Problem&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Luria and Delbrück were trying to settle a fundamental question about bacterial resistance: do bacteria mutate &lt;strong&gt;before&lt;/strong&gt; encountering a selective agent (in their case, bacteriophage virus), or does the selective agent &lt;strong&gt;cause&lt;/strong&gt; the resistance to appear? These are their two hypotheses:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutation hypothesis&lt;/strong&gt; — resistance arises spontaneously and randomly during growth, independently of any virus. The virus merely kills sensitive cells and reveals the pre-existing resistant ones.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Acquired immunity hypothesis&lt;/strong&gt; — any bacterium has a small fixed probability of surviving a virus attack, and surviving confers heritable immunity. Resistance is a response to the virus, not a pre-existing state.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The key insight was that these two hypotheses, while producing the same end result (resistant colonies on a plate), predict &lt;strong&gt;completely different statistical distributions&lt;/strong&gt; of resistant colony counts across sister cultures. This is the heart of the paper.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Mathematical Argument That Generates the Jackpot&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Under the Acquired Immunity Hypothesis&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each cell has a small fixed probability of surviving virus attack independently of all other cells. This is simply random sampling from a large population — the expected distribution is &lt;strong&gt;Poisson&lt;/strong&gt;, where variance equals the mean. If the average across sister cultures is 10 resistant colonies per plate, the variance will also be approximately 10. Results should be tightly clustered and reproducible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Under the Mutation Hypothesis&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is where the jackpot arises, and the logic is subtle and elegant.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Luria and Delbrück define the mutation rate &lt;em&gt;a&lt;/em&gt; as the probability of a sensitive cell mutating to resistance per bacterium per unit time. The number of new mutations arising during a small time interval &lt;em&gt;dt&lt;/em&gt; is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;dm=a⋅dt⋅Ntdm = a \cdot dt \cdot N_tdm=a⋅dt⋅Nt​&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Integrating over the growth period gives the total expected number of mutation &lt;strong&gt;events&lt;/strong&gt; per culture:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;m=a(Nt−N0)m = a(N_t - N_0)m=a(Nt​−N0​)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where N₀ is the starting cell number and N_t is the final cell number.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here is the critical observation. &lt;strong&gt;What you count on a plate is not the number of mutation events, but the number of resistant bacteria&lt;/strong&gt; — which equals the number of mutant cells that arose from each mutation event, multiplied by however many generations that mutant lineage had to expand before plating.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A mutation arising at generation 1 expands through almost the entire growth period and produces a large clone. A mutation arising at the last generation produces only 1–2 resistant cells. Luria and Delbrück prove that on average, across all possible times a mutation could arise, each generation contributes equally to the expected total count — early mutations are rare but enormous, late mutations are common but tiny, and these two effects exactly cancel in the mean.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But for any &lt;strong&gt;single experiment&lt;/strong&gt; with a small number of cultures, the situation is like a slot machine — you will probably get nothing from any given play, but occasionally you hit the jackpot. Specifically: most cultures will have zero mutations during early growth simply because the population is small then and the per-cell probability is low. But in those rare cultures where a mutation did arise early, the exponential amplification over many subsequent generations creates a very large clone. The result across sister cultures is a distribution with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most cultures having zero or very few resistant colonies
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A small number of cultures having enormously large counts — the jackpots
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A &lt;strong&gt;variance much greater than the mean&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They formalise this, showing the likely variance scales as:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;varr≈Ca2Nt2\text{var}_r \approx Ca^2N_t^2varr​≈Ca2Nt2​&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;which is far larger than the mean &lt;em&gt;r&lt;/em&gt; — giving a variance-to-mean ratio much greater than 1, in sharp contrast to the Poisson prediction of variance = mean.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Experimental Validation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Luria and Delbrück ran two types of experiments to test these predictions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Control experiment (Table 1):&lt;/strong&gt; They took multiple samples from the &lt;strong&gt;same culture&lt;/strong&gt; and plated them separately. If the plating method itself introduces variability, this would show up here. The result: variance ≈ mean, consistent with Poisson sampling. The method is reliable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Critical experiment (Table 2 and 3):&lt;/strong&gt; They grew many &lt;strong&gt;sister cultures&lt;/strong&gt; from small identical inocula and plated each separately. The results were dramatically different — variance was orders of magnitude greater than the mean in every single experiment. For example in Experiment 1, the mean was 26.8 resistant colonies per sample but the variance was 1217. Some cultures had 125 resistant colonies while others had 3. This is exactly the jackpot pattern.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They also confirmed that mutation rates estimated from different experiments, different culture volumes, and different media (broth vs synthetic) were all consistent at approximately 2–3 × 10⁻⁸ mutations per bacterium per division cycle, showing the mutation rate is a stable intrinsic property of the cell, not something induced by the environment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Origin of the Word &quot;Jackpot&quot;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Luria and Delbrück actually use the slot machine analogy themselves in the paper, writing that the situation is similar to the operation of a fair slot machine, where the average return from a limited number of plays is probably considerably less than the input, and improbably, when the jackpot is hit, the return is much bigger than the input. This characterises the distribution as having a long and significant tail of rare cases of high numbers of resistant bacteria, with an abnormally high variance — they coined the terminology directly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Conclusion&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper concludes that the excess variance across sister cultures — the jackpot distribution — is proof that resistance arises by spontaneous heritable mutation &lt;strong&gt;before and independently of&lt;/strong&gt; virus exposure. The virus does not induce or direct resistance. This was a foundational result for bacterial genetics, establishing that bacteria obey the same Darwinian rules as sexually reproducing organisms, and that the tools of population genetics and statistics could be applied to understand bacterial evolution — which is precisely the framework every subsequent paper you have read (Lenski et al., Fusco et al.) is built upon.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
