        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-20 05:50:33</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>e2119720119</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_538">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Summary: &quot;Mutation bias shapes the spectrum of adaptive substitutions&quot; (Cano et al., 2022)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Background and Motivation&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A longstanding assumption in evolutionary biology holds that natural selection alone determines the outcome of adaptation — that when beneficial mutations are plentiful, which ones fix is purely a matter of which confers the greatest fitness advantage. This paper challenges that view by asking a more quantitative question: how strongly does the &lt;strong&gt;mutation spectrum&lt;/strong&gt; (the relative rates of all possible nucleotide changes in a genome) shape which mutations actually get fixed during adaptation?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prior work had shown that adaptive substitutions tend to be enriched for mutationally common types — for instance, transitions being overrepresented in &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; antibiotic resistance — but these were tests for specific biases, not a general framework. This paper fills that gap.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Approach&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors develop a &lt;strong&gt;codon-based statistical model&lt;/strong&gt; applied to three large empirical datasets of adaptive amino acid substitutions in:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt; (yeast) — adaptation to high salinity, low glucose, rich media, and gene knockout stress; 713 adaptive events across 534 mutational paths
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/em&gt; — adaptation to temperature stress in laboratory evolution; 602 events across 492 paths
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Mycobacterium tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; — clinical isolates resistant to 11 antibiotics; 4,413 events across 283 paths
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The spectrum of adaptive substitutions is represented as a 354-element vector (one for each possible codon-to-amino-acid change under the standard genetic code). The key modeling step uses &lt;strong&gt;negative binomial regression&lt;/strong&gt; (appropriate for overdispersed count data) to model the expected number of adaptive events for each type of change as:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;E[n(c, a)] ∝ f(c) · μ(c, a)^β&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where f(c) is the genomic frequency of codon c, μ(c, a) is the empirically determined mutation rate from codon c to amino acid a, and &lt;strong&gt;β is the coefficient of mutational influence&lt;/strong&gt; — the central parameter of the study.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
β = 0 → mutation spectrum has no influence on adaptation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
β = 1 → mutation spectrum has a fully proportional influence (each doubling of mutation rate doubles adaptive event frequency)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
0 &amp;lt; β &amp;lt; 1 → intermediate influence
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Species-specific mutation spectra were derived from independent published data: mutation-accumulation experiments for yeast and &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, and neutral SNP patterns for &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Main Findings&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;1. Mutation bias has a strong, proportional influence on adaptive evolution in all three species&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The regression results showed statistically significant, approximately proportional effects of the mutation spectrum in all three organisms:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt;: β = 1.05 (95% CI: 0.89–1.21), p &amp;lt; 10⁻¹⁶
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;: β = 0.98 (95% CI: 0.71–1.25), p &amp;lt; 10⁻¹¹
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;: β = 0.85 (95% CI: 0.31–1.37), p &amp;lt; 10⁻³
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In every case, the 95% confidence interval includes β = 1 (proportional influence) and excludes β = 0 (no influence). This means that across all three species, differences in mutation rate produce approximately proportional differences in how often a given type of adaptive substitution occurs — a remarkably strong and consistent signal.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;2. Empirical mutation spectra outperform random ones&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors ran a randomization test, repeating the regression 10⁶ times using randomly generated mutation spectra. The empirical (real) mutation spectrum outperformed the vast majority of random ones: only 0.2%, 3.7%, and 4.2% of random spectra fit the adaptive substitution data better than the real spectrum, for yeast, &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; respectively. This confirms that the real mutation spectrum is genuinely informative about which adaptive changes occur, even outside the controlled lab conditions where mutation spectra are typically measured.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;3. The mutation spectrum can be recovered from adaptive substitution data&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;By treating the six nucleotide mutation rates as free parameters and fitting them via maximum likelihood to the adaptive substitution data, the authors were able to accurately recover the experimentally measured species-specific mutation spectra. The Pearson correlations between inferred and empirical spectra were 0.945 (yeast), 0.960 (&lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;), and 0.837 (&lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;). This is a striking result — it means that by studying which adaptive mutations accumulate, you can work backwards to learn about the underlying mutation process.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;4. Predictive power varies substantially across species&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Although β ≈ 1 in all three species, the model's ability to predict the full spectrum of adaptive substitutions differed markedly. The Pearson correlation between predicted and observed adaptive substitution spectra was:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt;: r = 0.68
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;: r = 0.41
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;: r = 0.16
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This variation motivated the simulation analyses below.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;5. Mutational supply (Nμ) and mutational target size jointly govern predictive power&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Population-genetic simulations using SLiM were used to explore why predictive power varied. The simulations manipulated population size N, mutation rate μ, and the fraction of beneficial mutations B. Key results:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Increasing Nμ decreases β toward zero.&lt;/strong&gt; At low mutational supply, the &quot;origin-fixation&quot; (or SSWM — strong selection, weak mutation) regime applies, and β ≈ 1. As supply increases, clonal interference occurs (multiple beneficial mutations compete simultaneously), and selection among segregating variants matters more than which mutation arises first, reducing mutation bias's influence.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Larger B (more beneficial mutations) improves predictive power&lt;/strong&gt; — somewhat counterintuitively. The intuition is that broader nucleotide biases can only be predictive when adaptive events are spread across many diverse codon-to-amino-acid changes. A larger B expands the set of available adaptive paths, producing more diverse, higher-entropy adaptive spectra that better align with the broad pattern of mutational biases.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Entropy of the adaptive spectrum&lt;/strong&gt; (how evenly events are spread across possible changes) is the key unifying variable. Higher entropy → better model predictions. This explains the empirical pattern: &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; has the lowest-entropy spectrum (adaptation is concentrated on a small number of resistance mutations at specific genes), while &lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt; has the highest.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The observed entropies for the three species were: &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt; (0.53) &amp;lt; &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; (0.80) &amp;lt; &lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt; (0.91), corresponding exactly to the ordering of predictive correlations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;6. Hitchhiker contamination does not explain the results&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For &lt;em&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, some fraction of substitutions may be &quot;hitchhikers&quot; — neutral mutations that reach fixation by linkage to a true driver. The authors estimated these fractions at ~24% and ~13%, respectively. Robustness analyses showed that even assuming 40% contamination, the signal of mutation bias remains strong and significant. Contamination levels of ~65% (yeast) and ~44% (&lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;) would be needed to eliminate significance — far beyond plausible estimates.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Broader Implications&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors address several wider implications of their findings:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Predictability of evolution:&lt;/strong&gt; Mutationally favored changes are more likely to contribute to adaptation, and this is predictable from prior data. At the level of the six nucleotide mutation types, the correlation with the adaptive spectrum is strong and consistent across all three species.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cancer biology parallel:&lt;/strong&gt; Driver mutations in cancer reflect the mutational signatures of underlying processes (UV light, tobacco, APOBEC, mismatch repair deficiency). The same logic applies — knowing mutation biases helps predict which adaptive changes will arise.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Challenging the classical view of mutation:&lt;/strong&gt; Classical population genetics (Haldane, Fisher) treated mutation as a weak, directionless pressure relative to selection, with strong mutational effects assumed to imply neutral evolution. This paper demonstrates that strong mutational effects on the spectrum of adaptive changes are perfectly consistent with all fixations being selective. The key theoretical insight is that mutation acts as a source of novel variants, not just a frequency-shifting force — so biases in which variants are introduced systematically bias which adaptive changes occur.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Support for dN/dS frameworks:&lt;/strong&gt; Many standard tests for positive selection (like dN/dS ratios) assume that the mutation spectrum influences adaptive and neutral substitutions proportionally. The finding that mutation spectra can be recovered from adaptive substitutions empirically validates this assumption.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Summary of Key Takeaways&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper establishes, with a rigorous and general statistical framework, that mutation bias is not a minor nuisance to be corrected for — it is a genuine and substantial driver of which adaptive substitutions accumulate. The mutation spectrum exerts approximately proportional influence on the adaptive substitution spectrum across organisms as diverse as yeast, &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;M. tuberculosis&lt;/em&gt;. This influence is strongest when mutational supply is low and the set of possible adaptive paths is broad. The results place mutation bias alongside natural selection as a quantifiable, directional cause of adaptive molecular evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
