        <dc:title>The Molecular Diversity of Adaptive Convergence</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Natural Selection Caught in the Act
            
              Understanding how new functions evolve has been of long-standing interest. However, the number of mutations needed to evolve a key innovation is rarely known, or whether other sets of mutations would also suffice, whether the intermediate steps are driven by natural selection, or how contingent the outcome is on steps along the way.
              
                Meyer
                et al.
              
              (p.
              428
              ; see the Perspective by
              
                Thompson
              
              ) answer these questions for a case in which phage lambda evolved the ability to infect its host
              Escherichia coli
              through a novel receptor. This shift required four mutations, which accumulated under natural selection in concert with coevolution of the host. However, when
              
                Tenaillon
                et al.
              
              (p.
              457
              ) exposed 115 lines of
              E. coli
              to high temperature and sequenced them, adaptation occurred through many different genetic paths, showing parallelism at the level of genes and interacting protein complexes, but only rarely at the nucleotide level. Thus, epistasis—nonadditive genetic interaction—is likely to play an important part in the process of adaptation to this environment.
            
          , 
            
              Replicate
              Escherichia coli
              lines show multiple convergent adaptations via different mutations in response to high temperature.
            
          , 
            
              To estimate the number and diversity of beneficial mutations, we experimentally evolved 115 populations of
              Escherichia coli
              to 42.2°C for 2000 generations and sequenced one genome from each population. We identified 1331 total mutations, affecting more than 600 different sites. Few mutations were shared among replicates, but a strong pattern of convergence emerged at the level of genes, operons, and functional complexes. Our experiment uncovered a set of primary functional targets of high temperature, but we estimate that many other beneficial mutations could contribute to similar adaptive outcomes. We inferred the pervasive presence of epistasis among beneficial mutations, which shaped adaptive trajectories into at least two distinct pathways involving mutations either in the RNA polymerase complex or the termination factor
              rho
              .</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2012-01-27</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1212986</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-21 04:07:08</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://www.sciencemag.org/site/feature/contribinfo/prep/license.xhtml</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>457-461</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_539">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here's a summary of the first part of the paper:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Background and motivation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors open with a fundamental question in evolutionary biology: if identical populations adapt to the same environment, do they do so through the same mutations, or through many different genetic paths? Prior work on this was limited — earlier studies had too few replicate populations and incomplete genetic data to answer the question properly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Experimental design&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To address this, the team evolved 115 separate populations of &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; at a stressful high temperature (42.2°C) for 2,000 generations, then sequenced one genome from each population. The bacterial lines all descended from a common ancestor, and selection was strong because the temperature was at the very limit of the bacteria's survival. Fitness increased substantially across all lines by the end of the experiment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutation landscape&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Across 114 lines (one was excluded due to a hypermutation defect), the researchers identified 1,258 total molecular changes — averaging 11 per clone — including point mutations, insertions/deletions, large deletions, and mobile element integrations. Roughly 80% of non-synonymous and intergenic mutations were estimated to be beneficial, based on their elevated frequency relative to neutral synonymous mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Convergence at different scales&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A key finding was that convergence depended heavily on the level of analysis. Very few specific mutations were shared between lines, but when looking at genes, operons, and functional complexes, strong patterns of parallel evolution emerged. For example, the gene &lt;em&gt;ybaL&lt;/em&gt; was mutated in 65 different lines via 65 distinct mutations. On average, two lines shared only ~2.6% of individual mutations, but ~20% of mutated genes and ~31.5% of affected functional units — a striking difference suggesting that natural selection consistently targets the same biological functions, even when the precise molecular changes differ.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Estimating the pool of beneficial mutations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using a statistical model, the authors estimated there are at least 850 — and potentially several thousand — possible beneficial mutation sites, far more than were observed in the experiment. This suggests the study only scratched the surface of possible adaptive solutions, even while nearly saturating the discovery of key functional targets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
