        <dc:title>Stochasticity in evolution</dc:title>
        <dc:date>3/2009</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534709000172</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-21 05:30:06</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>https://www.elsevier.com/tdm/userlicense/1.0/</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>157-165</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:01695347">
        <prism:volume>24</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1016/j.tree.2008.09.014</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>3</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 01695347</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_546">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Lenormand, Roze &amp;amp; Rousset (2009) — &quot;Stochasticity in Evolution&quot;: A Detailed Summary&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Core Argument&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper argues that stochasticity is far more than just &quot;noise&quot; or a source of unpredictability in evolution — it is an active, creative force that shapes evolutionary trajectories in profound ways. The authors move well beyond the classical neutralist–selectionist debate to map out a richer landscape of how chance operates.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Three Types of Stochasticity&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors open by distinguishing three distinct sources of randomness, each operating at a different biological scale.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Mutational stochasticity (gene level)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutations occur independently of their phenotypic effects — they are not directed and do not arise more frequently when advantageous. However, this doesn't mean beneficial and deleterious mutations are equally likely. Most newly arising mutations are deleterious, presumably because of the history of past adaptation. When mutation is limiting (i.e., population size × mutation rate is small), the beneficial mutation that occurs first will take a population on a particular track toward a particular adaptive solution or peak. The authors note that theory lags badly behind data here — most models either use the infinitesimal model or treat mutations as having fixed, known effects, rather than drawing from a realistic distribution of mutational effects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Life-history stochasticity (individual level)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Within the life cycle, events such as birth, reproduction, and death are best described by independent stochastic events for each individual. This is the classical source of &lt;strong&gt;genetic drift&lt;/strong&gt; — random fluctuation of allele frequencies across generations. The authors make several nuanced points about drift that challenge common intuitions. Crucially, even in an extremely large population, a particular allele carried by a given individual can be lost from one generation to the next because of stochasticity in offspring number. In other words, drift never fully disappears. They also introduce the concept of &lt;strong&gt;&quot;draft&quot;&lt;/strong&gt; — the fate of an allele could be largely determined by the random occurrence and spread of beneficial mutations at linked loci, which might be stronger than drift in larger populations where more beneficial alleles can sweep, but whose effect is important mainly when recombination is low.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Environmental stochasticity (population level)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Unlike the first two, environmental change affects all individuals simultaneously. Biotic environmental stochasticity is extremely frequent — the presence of parasites, predators, prey, and even conspecifics can be quite unpredictable for any individual. Importantly, the timescale matters: if environmental fluctuations are very rapid relative to generation time, they are averaged out, and only fluctuations at longer timescales will matter. The authors also note that environmental change is not always stochastic in an evolutionary sense — it only qualifies as stochastic when it occurs independently of the organism's adaptation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Four Situations Where Stochasticity Matters&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the conceptual heart of the paper. The authors organise the evolutionary consequences of stochasticity into four broad categories, illustrated in their Figure 1.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;(a) Maladaptation is Everywhere&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All forms of stochasticity impose a limit on adaptation. Because most random mutations and random environmental changes are likely to be deleterious given past adaptation, stochasticity is a constant source of evolutionary burden. The effects of drift, however, are nuanced and sometimes counterintuitive. For instance, the frequency of mildly deleterious alleles might be lowest at intermediate population size when their dominance coefficient is lower than 1/3, through what is known as &quot;purging by drift.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors highlight three major downstream consequences of deleterious mutation accumulation. First, deleterious mutations generate inbreeding depression and may thereby promote the evolution of inbreeding avoidance mechanisms. Second, at large enough loads, both the extinction of asexual populations by Muller's ratchet and the extinction of small sexual populations by mutational meltdown could occur when drift contributes to the accumulation of deleterious mutations. Third, at the genome level, the fixation of deleterious mutations might lead to the evolution of unnecessarily complex structures — possibly explaining the genomic complexity of multicellular eukaryotes relative to streamlined prokaryote genomes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;(b) Evolutionary Freedom&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Neutral mutations might play an important role in evolution beyond accounting for patterns of polymorphism at silent sites. The key insight is that many mutations are &lt;strong&gt;conditionally neutral&lt;/strong&gt; — only neutral in certain environments or genetic backgrounds. This might lead to the free accumulation of cryptic genetic variation, which could become an essential source of adaptive variation when the environment changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;On flat fitness landscapes (fitness ridges), chance is the main determinant of which phenotype a population will evolve. A compelling example is sexual selection: sexually selected traits in males have often no clear adaptive value with respect to environmental conditions or male–male competition, but seem only to evolve to match some arbitrary female preference. The extravagance and variety of male display traits across species is presented as a signature of evolutionary freedom — stochastic drift among many equally viable solutions. This freedom may also play a role in speciation by allowing sexually selected traits to diverge between populations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;(c) Evolutionary Revolutions&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This section addresses whether stochasticity can allow populations to escape local fitness peaks and jump to higher ones — a long-debated question in evolutionary theory. Drift has long been recognised as a possible mechanism allowing jumps from one peak to another, which might occur when population size is low, even transiently, such as through founder effects and bottlenecks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, the authors are careful to note the controversy here. Experimental work has largely failed to demonstrate that bottlenecks cause strong and long-lasting pre- or post-mating isolation, and there is little evidence that a particular adaptation occurred via the shifting balance process. The authors propose that peak shifts are more likely to involve multiple forms of stochasticity interacting — for instance, environmental fluctuations that temporarily suppress the fitness valley between peaks — rather than drift acting alone.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;(d) Stochasticity Shapes Selection Itself&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the most conceptually novel section. The authors argue that stochasticity doesn't just passively affect which variants survive — it actively generates and shapes the selection pressures organisms face.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bet hedging:&lt;/strong&gt; Environmental stochasticity generates uncertainty in future conditions, which can generate a selection pressure to minimise the potential detrimental effects of future and unpredictable conditions. Organisms can evolve &quot;bet hedging&quot; strategies — either conservative (buffering mechanisms like homeostasis) or diversified (producing variable phenotypes to spread risk, like dormant vs. non-dormant seeds).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Evolution of sex and recombination:&lt;/strong&gt; Stochasticity provides one of the most compelling explanations for why sex evolves. Sex and recombination are thought to evolve to increase the efficacy of selection by increasing variance in fitness. This is formalised through the &lt;strong&gt;Hill-Robertson effect&lt;/strong&gt;: clonal interference — an extreme form of the Hill-Robertson effect — sets an upper limit on the rate of adaptation in asexuals. Because beneficial mutations in asexual lineages compete against each other rather than combining freely, sex is favoured as a means of assembling optimal combinations of alleles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Social evolution and kin selection:&lt;/strong&gt; Local drift can shape the selection pressures that govern the evolution of sociality and life in groups. When populations are spatially structured, genetic drift creates relatedness among neighbours, and this local stochasticity is the engine that makes kin selection work. The authors stress that social traits — broadly defined — are universal: even dispersal and sex ratios qualify, so the reach of this argument is very wide.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Conclusions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper ends with a call for a more integrated treatment of stochasticity in evolutionary theory. The authors note that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Drift is the most commonly considered form of stochasticity, and the common view is that it limits adaptation — typically true, but only a partial account, since drift also plays a more creative role in evolution.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environmental stochasticity is undermodelled — many theoretical models include environmental change without treating it as genuinely stochastic.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutational stochasticity is rarely considered, perhaps because transient dynamics are often neglected in favour of equilibrium situations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Overall, combining all three forms of stochasticity in unified evolutionary models remains a major challenge for the field.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Takeaway&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper's central contribution is reframing stochasticity from a nuisance or null hypothesis into an &lt;strong&gt;active evolutionary ingredient&lt;/strong&gt;. Randomness doesn't just blur the signal of selection — it drives maladaptation, unlocks evolutionary freedom on flat landscapes, potentially enables peak shifts, and sculpts the very fitness landscapes that selection acts upon.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
