        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_547"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_544"/>
        <dc:title>Bias in the introduction of variation as an orienting factor in evolution</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>SUMMARY
              According to New Synthesis doctrine, the direction of evolution is determined by selection and not by “internal causes” that act by way of propensities of variation. This doctrine rests on the theoretical claim that because mutation rates are small in comparison to selection coefficients, mutation is powerless to overcome opposing selection. Using a simple population‐genetic model, this claim is shown to depend on assuming the prior availability of variation, so that mutation may act only as a “pressure” on the frequencies of existing alleles, and not as the evolutionary process that introduces novelty. As shown here, mutational bias in the introduction of novelty can strongly influence the course of evolution, even when mutation rates are small in comparison to selection coefficients. Recognizing this mode of causation provides a distinct mechanistic basis for an “internalist” approach to determining the contribution of mutational and developmental factors to evolutionary phenomena such as homoplasy, parallelism, and directionality.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>03/2001</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1046/j.1525-142x.2001.003002073.x</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-21 05:43:38</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/termsAndConditions#vor</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>73-83</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:1520-541X,%201525-142X">
        <prism:volume>3</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Evolution &amp; Development</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1046/j.1525-142x.2001.003002073.x</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>2</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Evolution and Development</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1520-541X, 1525-142X</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_547">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Yampolsky &amp;amp; Stoltzfus (2001): &quot;Bias in the Introduction of Variation as an Orienting Factor in Evolution&quot;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Central Problem&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper challenges a foundational doctrine of the Modern Synthesis (also called the New Synthesis) — the claim that the &lt;em&gt;direction&lt;/em&gt; of evolution is determined solely by natural selection, and that internal factors like mutation bias are too weak to orient evolutionary outcomes. Yampolsky and Stoltzfus argue this doctrine rests on a conceptual error, and they use population-genetic modelling to demonstrate an overlooked but powerful causal mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The New Synthesis Position and Its Flaw&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The orthodox view, grounded in the work of Fisher (1930) and Haldane (1932), treated mutation and selection as &lt;em&gt;opposing pressures&lt;/em&gt; acting on allele frequencies. Since mutation rates (typically ~10⁻⁵ or lower) are vastly smaller than selection coefficients (~10⁻² or 10⁻³), the conclusion seemed straightforward: mutation pressure is too weak to overcome selection and therefore cannot meaningfully orient evolutionary change. This view became canonical, repeated in textbooks and cited in authoritative reviews for decades.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors identify the critical flaw in this reasoning: it only ever modelled mutation as a &lt;strong&gt;pressure shifting the frequencies of pre-existing alleles&lt;/strong&gt;. It entirely ignored mutation's other, irreplaceable role — as the &lt;strong&gt;origin of allelic novelty&lt;/strong&gt;. In the standard &quot;shifting allele frequencies&quot; framework, all relevant variants are assumed to already exist in the population, so mutation merely nudges their frequencies up or down. Under this assumption, of course, selection dominates. But this assumption smuggles in a conclusion: by presupposing that variation is already available, the framework defines away the very step where mutation bias could matter.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Key Conceptual Distinction&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper draws a sharp line between two roles of mutation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutation as pressure&lt;/strong&gt; — nudging the frequency of an already-present allele. This effect is indeed swamped by selection when mutation rates are small.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutation as the origin of novelty&lt;/strong&gt; — introducing an allele that was previously absent (frequency = 0). Nothing else can do this. And crucially, &lt;em&gt;which&lt;/em&gt; novel allele gets introduced first is governed by mutation rates, not selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This distinction is already implicit in neutral theory. The steady-state rate of neutral evolution is simply the mutation rate (Rₙ = μₙ), because the rate of origin and the probability of fixation (1/N) multiply to give exactly the mutation rate, independent of population size. A bias in mutation rates directly becomes a bias in evolutionary outcomes — not through &quot;pressure&quot; competing with selection, but through which novelties arise at all.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Model: Combining Biased Origin with Selective Fixation&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To test whether this logic extends to &lt;em&gt;non-neutral&lt;/em&gt; evolution (i.e., when selection is active), the authors construct a haploid two-locus model. The setup, based on the Bateson-Dobzhansky-Muller framework, has:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A starting population of genotype &lt;em&gt;ab&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Two possible beneficial mutations leading to either &lt;em&gt;Ab&lt;/em&gt; (higher fitness, s₁) or &lt;em&gt;aB&lt;/em&gt; (lower fitness, s₂), with s₁ &amp;gt; s₂
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutation rates biased in the &lt;em&gt;opposite&lt;/em&gt; direction: &lt;em&gt;aB&lt;/em&gt; arises at a higher rate (μ₂ &amp;gt; μ₁)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A fitness landscape where &lt;em&gt;AB&lt;/em&gt; is deleterious, so populations evolve to one of two isolated fitness peaks
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The non-trivial question: can mutation bias push evolution predominantly toward the &lt;em&gt;less fit&lt;/em&gt; peak (&lt;em&gt;aB&lt;/em&gt;) when that peak is more mutationally accessible?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The answer is yes, strongly and robustly.&lt;/strong&gt; Through Monte Carlo simulations across a wide range of population sizes and parameter values, the bias in evolutionary outcomes closely tracks the ratio B/K = (μ₂/μ₁)/(s₁/s₂) — the mutation bias divided by the selection coefficient bias. This means:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A 10-fold mutation bias toward &lt;em&gt;aB&lt;/em&gt; can overcome a 10-fold fitness advantage of &lt;em&gt;Ab&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This effect persists even when the higher mutation rate is held at least 100-fold &lt;em&gt;below&lt;/em&gt; the smaller selection coefficient — so it is emphatically not &quot;pressure&quot; in the classical sense
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The effect is largely independent of recombination rate
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The magnitude of selection coefficients (varying them 200-fold) has little effect on the bias in outcomes, only their &lt;em&gt;ratio&lt;/em&gt; matters
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The intuition behind the B/K approximation is elegant: under the origin-fixation framework (valid when Nμ &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 1, meaning mutations are rare enough that populations are usually monomorphic), the rate at which each peak gets reached is proportional to the mutation rate times the fixation probability (~2Ns). So the ratio of rates of reaching the two peaks is (μ₂s₂)/(μ₁s₁) = B/K.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Why the Old Models Got It Wrong&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper demonstrates this with a crucial control experiment (Figure 6): when alleles &lt;em&gt;A&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;B&lt;/em&gt; are assumed to be &lt;em&gt;already present&lt;/em&gt; in the population at even tiny initial frequencies (p = 0.005), mutation bias becomes almost entirely ineffective. This confirms that the New Synthesis conclusion was not wrong given its assumptions — it was wrong to make those assumptions. By presupposing a pre-existing &quot;gene pool,&quot; classical models defined mutation out of its novelty-introducing role and then correctly concluded it was unimportant in that diminished role.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Three Case Studies of Applicability&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. GC/AT bias and amino acid composition.&lt;/strong&gt; Genomes with high GC content encode proteins enriched in amino acids with GC-rich codons. The standard debate frames this as either selection &lt;em&gt;for&lt;/em&gt; GC content or neutral drift driven by mutational pressure. The authors argue this is a false dichotomy: mutation-biased adaptive evolution can simultaneously produce selection-driven protein optimisation &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; a mutation-driven shift in nucleotide and amino acid composition. In rough fitness landscapes (where first-come-first-served dynamics apply, e.g. cavity-filling in proteins), the mutationally favoured amino acid change need not be the most beneficial one to predominate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Parallel evolution in bacteriophage φX174.&lt;/strong&gt; Bull et al. (1997) observed striking parallelism in multiple independently evolving lineages and concluded (from the inadequacy of mutation pressure) that selection alone explains it. Yampolsky and Stoltzfus reanalyse their data and find that parallel amino acid replacements show a transition-to-transversion ratio of 21:7 — significantly higher than the 1:2 ratio expected under rate uniformity, and statistically indistinguishable from the known transition/transversion bias in the bacterial host &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;. This strongly implicates a mutational bias in the &lt;em&gt;origin&lt;/em&gt; of the parallel beneficial alleles, quite apart from whether selection then drives their fixation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Developmental biases in variation.&lt;/strong&gt; The argument extends beyond DNA mutation to developmental systems. If some phenotypes can arise through many different mutations (are &quot;mutationally accessible&quot; in many ways) while others are accessible through fewer routes, this constitutes a developmental bias in the introduction of variation. The authors model this as an L-fold increase in the effective mutation rate toward the developmentally favoured phenotype, and show its orienting effect is equivalent to a mutation bias of magnitude B = L. Alberch and Gale's (1985) findings on amphibian limb reduction — where experimentally perturbed and evolutionarily evolved phenotypes correlate — are cited as empirical support.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Broader Synthesis&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper concludes with three theoretical implications:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Against neo-Darwinism as a complete account.&lt;/strong&gt; Within the framework of &quot;introduction and sorting of variation&quot; (drawing on Vrba and Eldredge 1984), the neo-Darwinian view is a specific hypothesis: non-randomness in evolution stems entirely from biased &lt;em&gt;sorting&lt;/em&gt; (fitness differences), with the only concession being that impossible mutations cannot occur. The paper argues this is falsifiable and likely false, because homoplasy, parallelism, and directional trends may be substantially shaped by biased &lt;em&gt;introduction&lt;/em&gt; of variation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Against the neutralist framing of mutation bias.&lt;/strong&gt; Because it was assumed that mutation-biased evolution required neutral fixations, the relevance of mutation bias was held hostage to the selectionist-neutralist dispute. The paper dissolves this linkage: mutation bias can orient evolution even when all fixations are selective. This frees researchers to evaluate evidence for mutation bias on its own merits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Toward an &quot;internalist&quot; evolutionary biology.&lt;/strong&gt; The authors argue that the apparent tension between evo-devo and population genetics is resolvable. Population genetics has focused almost exclusively on the &lt;em&gt;sorting&lt;/em&gt; side of evolution; bias in the &lt;em&gt;introduction&lt;/em&gt; of variation is a largely unexplored but theoretically coherent topic within the same framework. A research programme built around characterising the spectrum of spontaneous variation, its molecular and developmental causes, and its correlation with evolutionary outcomes would be both mechanistically grounded and substantively distinct from neo-Darwinism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Summary of Core Claims&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Claim&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Substance&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The New Synthesis doctrine is based on a category error&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutation's role as the &lt;em&gt;origin&lt;/em&gt; of novelty was conflated with its role as a weak pressure on allele frequencies&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutational bias strongly orients evolution even under selection&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Demonstrated via population-genetic simulation; bias in outcomes ≈ B/K across wide parameter ranges&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The effect is not &quot;pressure&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reducing mutation rates can &lt;em&gt;increase&lt;/em&gt; the orienting effect; the mechanism is origin-fixation kinetics, not frequency-shifting&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The effect depends on the absence of pre-existing variation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When variants are pre-present, the effect vanishes — which is why classical models missed it&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Developmental biases work by the same logic&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Phenotypes accessible via many mutational routes enjoy an equivalent &quot;developmental mutation bias&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a general, distinct evolutionary cause&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It operates alongside selection, not instead of it, and applies to neutral and selective fixations alike&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper is widely regarded as an early and rigorous articulation of what would later be formalised in the &quot;mutation-biased adaptation&quot; and &quot;constructive neutral evolution&quot; literature, and as a key theoretical contribution to the Extended Evolutionary Synthesis.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
