        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_554"/>
        <dc:title>Mutation and selection in bacterial populations: alternatives to the hypothesis of directed mutation.</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Bacterial populations have served as model systems for studying evolutionary processes ever since the classic experiments of Luria and Delbruck, which demonstrated the occurrence of mutations prior to selection for the traits they conferred. However, several authors have recently presented experiments suggesting that bacteria may have mechanisms for directing which mutations occur, such that the rate of adaptive mutations is enhanced. Before the hypothesis of directed mutation is accepted, it is imperative to consider alternative hypotheses that might account for the same observations. To this end, we expand upon existing mathematical theory of the dynamics of mutation and selection in clonal populations for two cases of particular interest. The first case concerns selection against mutants before plating; this selection occurs as the result of differences in growth rate between mutants and nonmutants. We demonstrate that this selection model gives rise to distributions of mutants, obtained by plating from sister cultures, that are very similar to those expected when some mutations are induced by the selective environment. The second case concerns the sequential incorporation of two mutations as the result of selection for an intermediate genotype after plating. We demonstrate that this two-step mutation model also yields distributions that are similar to those expected when some mutations are induced by the selective environment. These two cases therefore provide alternatives to the hypothesis of directed mutation. We suggest experiments that might be used to examine our alternative hypotheses. We also contrast the hypothesis of directed mutation with the notion of inheritance of acquired characteristics.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>04/1989</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Mutation and selection in bacterial populations</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.86.8.2775</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-06-11 04:38:59</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>2775-2778</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_556">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Lenski, Slatkin &amp;amp; Ayala (1989) — Detailed Summary&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Background &amp;amp; Motivation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper was written in direct response to a wave of publications (principally Cairns et al., 1988) claiming that bacteria might possess mechanisms for &lt;strong&gt;directed mutation&lt;/strong&gt; — the idea that adaptive mutations occur at elevated rates specifically in the presence of the selective conditions that would favour them. Lenski et al. argue that before accepting such a radical hypothesis, two well-grounded alternative explanations must be rigorously excluded.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The classic Luria-Delbrück framework predicts that pre-existing, randomly occurring mutations produce a &lt;strong&gt;jackpot distribution&lt;/strong&gt; of mutant colony counts across sister cultures — high variance relative to the mean. The directed mutation hypothesis was supported by two observations that seemed inconsistent with this:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The variance-to-mean ratio of mutant distributions was &lt;em&gt;lower&lt;/em&gt; than the jackpot distribution predicts, better fitting a &lt;strong&gt;hybrid model&lt;/strong&gt; where some mutations are environmentally induced.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutant colonies appeared with a &lt;strong&gt;long delay&lt;/strong&gt; after selective plating, inconsistent with the known growth rate of those mutants.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Alternative Hypothesis 1: Selection Against Mutants Before Plating&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Core Idea&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The jackpot distribution assumes mutants and nonmutants grow at equal rates in liquid culture &lt;em&gt;prior&lt;/em&gt; to plating. This is often false. Many resistance mutations carry &lt;strong&gt;pleiotropic fitness costs&lt;/strong&gt; — the mutant genotype grows more slowly than wild-type under non-selective conditions. If mutants are growth-disadvantaged in pre-selection culture, early-arising jackpot mutants are partially purged, compressing the variance among sister cultures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Mathematical Treatment&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Building on Koch's (1982) modification of the Lea-Coulson equations, Lenski et al. parameterise the relative growth rate of mutant to wild-type as &lt;em&gt;g&lt;/em&gt; (where &lt;em&gt;g&lt;/em&gt; &amp;lt; 1 means the mutant is slower). They solve numerically for the distribution of mutant counts across sister cultures as a function of &lt;em&gt;g&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;m&lt;/em&gt; (the expected number of mutational events per culture).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Key Result&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The selection model produces cumulative mutant distributions &lt;strong&gt;nearly indistinguishable&lt;/strong&gt; from the hybrid mutation model of Cairns et al. when parameters are chosen to fit the same empirical data. Critically, this mimicry holds across the parameter ranges relevant to real &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; data. The apparent &quot;excess&quot; of low-mutant-count cultures — which Cairns et al. interpreted as directed mutation suppressing random events — is instead explained by pre-plating purifying selection on costly mutants.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;How to Falsify It&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Measure growth rates of mutant and wild-type clones under non-selective conditions with sufficient precision (using chemostat competition or similar designs). If the fitness difference is too small to account for the observed distributional compression, this hypothesis is excluded.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Alternative Hypothesis 2: Sequential Incorporation of Two Mutations&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Core Idea&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The jackpot distribution further assumes that observed colonies differ from the founding genotype by &lt;strong&gt;a single mutational step&lt;/strong&gt;. In reality, some adaptations require two mutations. If an intermediate genotype (A′) is selectively favoured on the plate itself, it grows into invisible &lt;strong&gt;microcolonies&lt;/strong&gt;, within which secondary mutations to the fully adaptive genotype (B) occur. The authors formalise this with a three-genotype model:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A&lt;/strong&gt;: founding genotype; cannot grow on selective medium
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A′&lt;/strong&gt;: intermediate; grows slowly on selective medium; arises from A at rate &lt;em&gt;u&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;B&lt;/strong&gt;: final adapted genotype; grows rapidly; arises from A′ at rate &lt;em&gt;v&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Mathematical Treatment&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The distribution of A′ cells at plating follows the standard jackpot distribution (parameterised by &lt;em&gt;m&lt;/em&gt;). Each A′ cell seeds a microcolony that grows at rate &lt;em&gt;c&lt;/em&gt;, reaching size &lt;em&gt;K = e^(ct′)&lt;/em&gt; at time &lt;em&gt;t′&lt;/em&gt; after plating. The probability that at least one A′→B mutation has occurred in a microcolony is &lt;em&gt;p = 1 − e^(−vK)&lt;/em&gt;, which increases with time. B cells then overgrow A′ progenitors, appearing as visible colonies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Key Results&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Late mutants&lt;/strong&gt;: Since &lt;em&gt;p&lt;/em&gt; increases over days as microcolonies expand, B colonies appear progressively and with apparent delays — mimicking the &quot;late mutant&quot; phenomenon used to support directed mutation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Distributional mimicry&lt;/strong&gt;: When &lt;em&gt;p&lt;/em&gt; &amp;lt; 1 (microcolonies have not yet fully saturated with secondary mutations), the distribution of B colony counts across sister cultures closely resembles the hybrid mutation model. The two panels of Figure 3 demonstrate this across two different parameter regimes drawn from Cairns et al.'s own data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cryptic intermediates&lt;/strong&gt;: With 10⁹ cells plated, 10³ A′ microcolonies each containing 10⁵ cells represent a negligible fraction of total plated cells, making A′ practically invisible without specifically looking for it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;How to Falsify/Confirm It&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose an elegant test: after several days on selective plates, sample clones that are &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; B (i.e., cannot grow rapidly on selective medium), culture them independently under non-selective conditions, then re-plate them on selective medium as quasi-sister cultures. If A′ has accumulated on the plates, these quasi-sister cultures should yield &lt;em&gt;more&lt;/em&gt; B mutants on average than true sister cultures founded from pure A. Crucially, if instead the elevated mutation rate is due to exposure to the selective environment (directed mutation), it should be erased by 40–60 generations of non-selective growth before the second plating.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Directed Mutation vs. Inheritance of Acquired Characteristics Distinction&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A secondary but pointed argument targets the framing in Cairns et al., who suggested directed mutation &quot;could provide a mechanism for inheritance of acquired characteristics.&quot; Lenski et al. reject this analogy on two grounds:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Lamarck's mechanism was use and disuse of organs&lt;/strong&gt; — a somatic, phenotypic process entirely distinct from environmentally-biased DNA mutation. Even if directed mutation were demonstrated, it would bear no resemblance to what Lamarck actually proposed.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The terminology is harmful&lt;/strong&gt;: conflating the two uses of &quot;inheritance of acquired characteristics&quot; risks lending spurious credibility to a long-refuted prescientific doctrine.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Conclusions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lenski et al. argue that both alternatives — pre-plating purifying selection on costly mutants, and two-step sequential mutation with selection on an intermediate — are grounded in well-established biological processes and each independently reproduces the distributional signatures used to argue for directed mutation. Their central conclusion is that invoking a novel directed mutation mechanism is &lt;strong&gt;premature&lt;/strong&gt; given these unexplored alternatives, and they call for:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Precise fitness measurements of mutant clones under non-selective conditions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fluctuation tests with quasi-sister cultures derived from plates
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Direct genetic characterisation of intermediate genotypes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Relevance to Your Work&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper is particularly pertinent to your &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; fluctuation assay experiments. The two fitness-cost arguments map directly onto what you're measuring: &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations are known to carry growth-rate costs (exactly what you're characterising across your 28 strains), and those costs would compress variance in a fluctuation assay in precisely the way Lenski et al. describe. If you're interpreting mutant distributions from your assays, accounting for the differential &lt;em&gt;mumax&lt;/em&gt; values you've measured is not optional — it's a necessary correction to avoid misattributing distributional compression to non-random mutation mechanisms.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
