        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_559"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_557"/>
        <dc:title>Excess of mutational jackpot events in expanding populations revealed by spatial Luria–Delbrück experiments</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Abstract
            The genetic diversity of growing cellular populations, such as biofilms, solid tumours or developing embryos, is thought to be dominated by rare, exceptionally large mutant clones. Yet, the emergence of these mutational jackpot events is only understood in well-mixed populations, where they stem from mutations that arise during the first few cell divisions. To study jackpot events in spatially structured populations, we track mutant clones in microbial populations using fluorescence microscopy and population sequencing. High-frequency mutations are found to be massively enriched in microbial colonies compared with well-shaken liquid cultures, as a result of late-occurring mutations surfing at the edge of range expansions. Thus, jackpot events can be generated not only when mutations arise early but also when they occur at favourable locations, which exacerbates their role in adaptation and disease. In particular, because spatial competition with the wild type keeps most mutant clones in a quiescent state, strong selection pressures that kill the wild type promote drug resistance.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2016-10-03</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms12760</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-06-12 00:53:19</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>12760</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_559">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Fusco et al. (2016) — Detailed Summary&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Core Question&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The classic Luria-Delbrück framework was developed for &lt;strong&gt;well-mixed liquid cultures&lt;/strong&gt;, where jackpot events arise from mutations that happen early during exponential growth. But most real biological populations — biofilms, solid tumours, developing embryos — are &lt;strong&gt;spatially structured&lt;/strong&gt;, growing outward from a centre rather than uniformly throughout a volume. Fusco et al. ask: how does spatial structure change the frequency and nature of mutational jackpot events?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Their central finding is that spatial growth dramatically &lt;strong&gt;amplifies&lt;/strong&gt; jackpot events beyond what the classic theory predicts, through a mechanism called &lt;strong&gt;gene surfing&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Experimental System&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Two Organisms, Two Complementary Approaches&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;E. coli (sequencing approach):&lt;/strong&gt; They used a &lt;em&gt;mutT&lt;/em&gt; deletion strain with an elevated mutation rate (~150× wild-type for A/T→C/G transversions). Populations were grown either as colonies on solid agar or as well-shaken liquid cultures, both reaching a comparable final size of ~3×10⁹ cells. They used deep population sequencing (&amp;gt;1000× coverage) to detect all SNPs present at frequencies above ~10⁻³ across the entire genome simultaneously — yielding around 600 high-frequency mutant clones per colony to characterise the statistics of jackpot events.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They also grew a third condition — &lt;strong&gt;well-mixed populations on plates&lt;/strong&gt; — where cells were spread every 90 minutes to prevent spatial structure from forming while maintaining the agar surface environment. This controlled for any effect of growth medium on mutation rate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;S. cerevisiae (fluorescence approach):&lt;/strong&gt; They used an engineered budding yeast strain that stochastically switches from red (RFP) to green (GFP) fluorescence at a rate of ~1.6×10⁻³ per cell division via Cre-loxP recombination. This allowed direct visual tracking of mutant clone size and spatial position during colony growth using fluorescence microscopy and time-lapse imaging across 343 colonies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Key Experimental Finding&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Colonies had approximately ten times more mutant clones above frequencies of 1% — corresponding to clones of at least 10⁷ cells — than well-mixed populations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Critically, the well-mixed populations grown on plates produced SNP distributions consistent with those grown in liquid culture, confirming that the mutation rate is not significantly affected by the mode of growth. The excess of jackpot events in colonies is therefore a consequence of &lt;strong&gt;spatial growth dynamics&lt;/strong&gt;, not a difference in mutation rate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Mechanism: Gene Surfing&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;What Is Gene Surfing?&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a colony growing on solid agar, most growth occurs at the colony edge — the interior is largely non-growing because nutrients are depleted. Only cells at the expanding frontier are actively dividing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This creates a fundamentally different dynamic from a well-mixed culture. A mutation arising &lt;strong&gt;near the growing front&lt;/strong&gt; can be carried along with the expanding edge, accumulating descendants with every generation of frontier advance. This is gene surfing — the mutant allele rides the wave of population expansion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Time-lapse movies showed that most high-frequency clones first arise near the front of the growing colony. The resulting clonal patches grow with the advancing frontier until they lose contact to the front, whereupon they become trapped as bubbles in the non-growing bulk of the population. Rarely, clones are able to surf at the front until the end of the experiment and give rise to sectors.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Two Morphological Outcomes&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This produces two distinct clone types visible under fluorescence:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sectors&lt;/strong&gt; — elongated wedge-shaped clones extending from near the centre to the colony edge, arising when a mutant lineage successfully surfs the expanding front for an extended period. These are rare but reach very high frequencies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bubbles&lt;/strong&gt; — elliptical, enclosed clones trapped in the non-growing colony interior, arising when a clone surfs briefly then loses contact with the front as wild-type cells outcompete it at the edge. These are more numerous but reach lower frequencies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Mathematical Theory&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Why Spatial Growth Generates More Mutants&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fusco et al. derive the expected total number of mutants for both growth modes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Well-mixed population:&lt;/strong&gt; Growth is uniform throughout the volume. The number of generations elapsed is log₂N, giving a total expected mutant count proportional to &lt;strong&gt;mN log₂N&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Range expansion (colony):&lt;/strong&gt; Only cells at the frontier divide. The frontier advances by one cell-layer thickness &lt;em&gt;l&lt;/em&gt; per generation, so the number of generations elapsed at the frontier during growth to size N scales as &lt;strong&gt;R/l ~ N^(1/2)/l&lt;/strong&gt;. The total expected mutant count is therefore proportional to &lt;strong&gt;mN^(3/2)/l&lt;/strong&gt; — which for large N is vastly greater than the well-mixed expectation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Note that, as in the classic Luria-Delbrück case, the mean is usually not a useful quantity because it is dominated by very rare, large events. Nevertheless, both the typical and the mean number of mutations exceed the well-mixed expectations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Clone Size Distribution&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a well-mixed population, the clone size distribution follows a single power law: P(x) ~ (Nx)⁻¹.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In spatially structured populations, the distribution crosses over between &lt;strong&gt;two distinct power-law regimes&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Low frequency regime&lt;/strong&gt; (bubbles): P(x) ~ x^(−α) with α &amp;lt; 1 — more shallow, indicating an excess of moderate-sized clones
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;High frequency regime&lt;/strong&gt; (sectors): P(x) ~ x^(−β) with β &amp;gt; 1 — steeper tail
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The crossover frequency &lt;em&gt;x_c&lt;/em&gt; separates these two regimes. The exponents α and β are not independent — they are linked by an exact scaling relationship determined by the dimensionality of growth and the roughness of the expanding front.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The KPZ Connection&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The roughness of the bacterial colony frontier follows &lt;strong&gt;Kardar-Parisi-Zhang (KPZ) interface growth dynamics&lt;/strong&gt;, which predicts a dynamical exponent z = 3/2 in 2D. This determines the fractal geometry of bubble and sector boundaries, which the authors model as &lt;strong&gt;annihilating random walks&lt;/strong&gt;. From the statistical properties of these random walks they derive the power-law exponents analytically, finding excellent agreement with both the sequencing data and the fluorescence clone size distributions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Their simulation results, covering over four orders of magnitude, reveal that the distribution crosses over between two power-law distributions with distinct exponents. Analysis of the clone shapes produced in their simulations shows that the power-law regimes of low and high frequencies characterize bubbles and sectors, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Drug Resistance and Competitive Release&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Costly Mutants Are Trapped as Bubbles&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Many resistance mutations carry fitness costs in the absence of the drug they resist — exactly the situation with many &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations. Since drug resistance often comes at a selective cost in the absence of the drug, most resistance mutant clones are expected to be small and hidden in the bulk of the susceptible wild-type population.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In spatial populations this has a specific geometric consequence. Costly mutants that arise near the front are rapidly overtaken by faster-growing wild-type cells and fall behind the advancing frontier. They become &lt;strong&gt;encased as bubbles&lt;/strong&gt; — completely surrounded by wild-type cells with no access to nutrients or space at the growing edge. They are not extinct, but they are trapped and quiescent.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;High Drug Doses Can Backfire&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This spatial encasement sets up a dangerous dynamic when antibiotics are applied. Upon a sudden environmental change, for example by a strong antibiotic attack killing the susceptible wild type, the trapped mutants may become unleashed, regrowing and thus rescuing the population from extinction. This evolutionary rescue is brought about by a particular kind of competitive release, in which the indispensable resource is space.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Their simulations show a striking non-monotonic relationship between drug dose and outcome. Simulations showed the smallest net population growth for intermediate death rates. In contrast, high death rates not only failed to eradicate the population, but promoted the spread of resistance by generating an entirely resistant population.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They confirmed this experimentally by embedding a droplet of chloramphenicol-resistant &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; (expressing YFP) within a larger droplet of susceptible cells (expressing CFP) at varying antibiotic concentrations. At intermediate concentrations the resistant clone stayed trapped. At high concentrations the susceptible wild-type was eliminated, releasing the resistant clone to spread freely across the plate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Implications Beyond Microbiology&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Multi-Step Mutations&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The excess of large-frequency clones promotes multi-step evolutionary processes, such as the emergence of double mutants. The relative probability of producing a double mutant is always higher in a colony, especially when the secondary mutation rate is low. This is because the first mutation produces a larger pool of intermediate cells in a spatial population, giving more opportunities for the second mutation to arise within that pool — directly relevant to the two-step model from Lenski et al.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Cancer and Solid Tumours&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors explicitly extend their framework to spherically growing 3D populations, which better model solid tumours. Their simulations show that the excess of jackpot events and the bubble/sector distinction persist in 3D growth with different but analytically predictable exponents. Our results urge for caution when employing the classic Luria-Delbrück theory as a general theory for neutral evolution... deviations from the classic Luria-Delbrück theory may simply indicate non-uniform growth rather than non-neutral evolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Relevance to Your Work&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper is directly relevant to your fluctuation assay design in several ways. If any of your &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutant experiments involve growth on solid agar — including the colonies you use to pick clones for sequencing or your selective plates — spatial jackpot events of the kind described here could be operating. The paper also reinforces Lenski et al.'s point about fitness costs compressing variance, but adds a new spatial dimension: costly mutants don't just get purged in liquid culture, they get geometrically trapped in colonies. And the competitive release finding matters for how you interpret your temperature-dependent fitness data — a strain that is costly at 37°C but has reduced cost at a different temperature could behave very differently when selective pressure is applied.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;You said: &quot;The excess of jackpot events in colonies is therefore a consequence of spatial growth dynamics, not a difference in mutation rate.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&quot;The excess of jackpot events in colonies is therefore a consequence of spatial growth dynamics, not a difference in mutation rate.&quot; why is this the case&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;11:33 am&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Claude responded: Great question.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Great question. The logic here is worth unpacking carefully.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What They Needed to Rule Out First&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When Fusco et al. saw ~10× more high-frequency mutant clones in colonies than in liquid cultures, the most boring explanation would be: &lt;strong&gt;colonies just have a higher mutation rate&lt;/strong&gt;. Maybe something about growing on agar — nutrient limitation, oxidative stress, slower growth — increases the per-cell mutation rate, and more mutations simply means more jackpots.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To rule this out they grew a third condition: &lt;strong&gt;well-mixed populations on plates&lt;/strong&gt;. These were grown on agar but spread with glass beads every 90 minutes, preventing any spatial structure from forming. The cells experienced the same surface environment as colony-grown cells, but without the non-uniform frontier growth.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The well-mixed on-plate populations produced SNP distributions &lt;strong&gt;identical to liquid cultures&lt;/strong&gt; — not to colonies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Same surface. Same medium. Same mutation rate. Completely different distribution from colonies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the controlled comparison that isolates spatial structure as the variable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;So Why Does Spatial Structure Cause More Jackpots?&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The key is understanding what actually generates a jackpot in the first place, and then seeing how spatial growth changes that mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;In a Well-Mixed Culture&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A jackpot requires a mutation to arise &lt;strong&gt;early&lt;/strong&gt;, giving it many generations of exponential amplification before plating. The earlier the mutation, the bigger the jackpot. But most mutations arise late, because most cell divisions happen late in exponential growth when the population is large. So jackpots are rare and depend entirely on lucky timing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The total number of generations elapsed during growth to size N is log₂N — a relatively modest number. Each generation adds the same multiplicative increment of mutants regardless of when during growth you are.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;In a Colony&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Growth is not uniform. Only cells at the &lt;strong&gt;expanding frontier&lt;/strong&gt; are actively dividing — the interior is metabolically quiescent because nutrients are consumed at the edge. This means the frontier is a thin active layer that sweeps outward generation by generation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Now consider a mutation that arises at the frontier. It does not get diluted into a large well-mixed population. Instead it is &lt;strong&gt;carried forward with the expanding edge&lt;/strong&gt;, accumulating descendants with every generation of frontier advance. It surfs the wave.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The number of generations elapsed at the frontier during growth to final size N scales as N^(1/2)/l rather than log₂N. For large N, N^(1/2) grows much faster than log N. So there are simply &lt;strong&gt;far more generations of frontier growth&lt;/strong&gt; during which surfing mutations can accumulate descendants, compared to the number of effective generations in a well-mixed culture of the same final size.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This means:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A late-arising mutation that would contribute almost nothing in a well-mixed culture can still generate a huge clone in a colony, &lt;strong&gt;if it arises at the right location&lt;/strong&gt; — near the frontier
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Timing is no longer the only route to a jackpot; &lt;strong&gt;spatial position&lt;/strong&gt; is an equally powerful amplifier
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The total expected number of mutants scales as mN^(3/2)/l in a colony versus mN log₂N in a well-mixed culture — a fundamental difference that grows with population size
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Conceptual Punchline&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the classic Luria-Delbrück framework, jackpots happen because &lt;strong&gt;time is unfair&lt;/strong&gt; — early mutations get exponentially more descendants than late ones.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fusco et al. show that in spatial populations, &lt;strong&gt;space is also unfair&lt;/strong&gt; — frontier mutations get exponentially more descendants than interior ones, regardless of when they arose.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is an entirely new route to jackpot events that has nothing to do with mutation rate. The mutation rate sets how often mutations occur, but spatial structure determines how large a clone each mutation can generate. You can have the same mutation rate in a colony and a liquid culture and still get dramatically different jackpot distributions, because the amplification mechanism is fundamentally different.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Apparent Contradiction&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The classic jackpot concept says a resistance mutation arising early gets amplified exponentially, flooding the culture with resistant cells. This implies the resistant clone becomes large and abundant.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But Fusco et al. say that in spatially structured populations, resistance mutations that carry a fitness cost end up &lt;strong&gt;trapped as bubbles&lt;/strong&gt; — completely encased by expanding wild-type cells, unable to grow, quiescent and hidden. These bubbles are small and spatially isolated.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These two pictures seem to directly contradict each other. How can a mutation both produce a jackpot and be trapped in a bubble?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The Resolution: They Are Describing Different Things&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The key is that the classic jackpot concept and Fusco et al.'s bubble concept apply to &lt;strong&gt;different growth regimes and different clone fates&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Classic Jackpot Applies to Well-Mixed Liquid Culture&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a flask of shaken liquid culture, there is no spatial structure. A resistance mutation arising early has unrestricted access to nutrients and space throughout the entire culture volume. Its descendants divide at whatever rate the mutation permits and mix freely with wild-type cells. If the fitness cost is small enough, the clone grows and expands. The jackpot is the large number of resistant cells present at the moment of plating.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Critically, in liquid culture &lt;strong&gt;there is no physical barrier between mutant and wild-type cells&lt;/strong&gt;. Competition is purely about relative growth rates, not spatial exclusion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Bubble Applies to Spatially Structured Colony Growth&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a colony on agar, growth is only happening at the frontier. A resistance mutation arising in the interior of a colony — even an early one — is immediately surrounded by wild-type cells that are themselves surrounded by more wild-type cells. The mutant lineage has no access to the frontier because it is physically enclosed. Wild-type cells at the frontier outgrow it spatially, not just numerically. The resistant clone stops expanding not because it loses a growth rate competition but because it literally has no space to expand into.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This spatial encasement is what Fusco et al. call a bubble — it is a &lt;strong&gt;geometric consequence of colony growth dynamics&lt;/strong&gt;, not just a fitness consequence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
