    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:1040-9238,%201549-7798">
        <prism:volume>53</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Critical Reviews in Biochemistry and Molecular Biology</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1080/10409238.2017.1394262</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>1</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Critical Reviews in Biochemistry and Molecular Biology</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1040-9238, 1549-7798</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_575">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Schroeder et al. (2018) — Detailed Summary&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Overview&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This review, published in &lt;em&gt;Critical Reviews in Biochemistry and Molecular Biology&lt;/em&gt;, covers the major sources of spontaneous mutagenesis in bacteria, both the experimental methods used to detect them and the cellular mechanisms responsible for generating them. It's directly relevant to your rpoB/rifampicin resistance work, as the paper explicitly discusses rpoB as a mutation reporter.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Methods for Detecting Spontaneous Mutations&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;The Fluctuation Test&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The fluctuation test remains the standard approach for estimating spontaneous mutation rates. The basic logic: parallel cultures grow without selection, mutations arise stochastically and clonally expand, then selection is applied and colonies counted. The characteristic &quot;jackpot&quot; cultures — where a mutation arose early and proliferated extensively — produce the Luria-Delbrück distribution, which mathematically distinguishes pre-selection from post-selection mutagenesis.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three main rate-calculation methods are discussed: the p₀ method (proportion of empty cultures), the Lea-Coulson median method, and the MSS maximum-likelihood method (Sarkar et al. 1992 — directly relevant to your work).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two key assumptions must hold: mutants must be selectively neutral during pre-selection growth, and mutations must not arise during the selection phase itself. Violations shift the distribution toward Poisson. Post-selection mutagenesis inflates rate estimates upward; phenotypic lag deflates them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Mutation Reporter Genes&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reporters fall into two classes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reverse mutation assays&lt;/strong&gt; detect gain-of-function mutations with a small, specific target size. The Cupples &amp;amp; Miller &lt;em&gt;lacZ&lt;/em&gt; reversion system can detect each of the six possible base substitutions individually. Crucially for your work, &lt;strong&gt;rpoB is classified here as a reverse mutation reporter&lt;/strong&gt;: specific non-synonymous substitutions render RNA polymerase rifampicin-insensitive, and the gene can be used in any bacterium without requiring special genetic constructs. Similarly, &lt;em&gt;gyrA&lt;/em&gt; serves as a reporter for fluoroquinolone resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Forward mutation assays&lt;/strong&gt; detect loss-of-function mutations, giving a much larger target size and capturing more mutation types (nonsense, missense, frameshifts). Classic examples include &lt;em&gt;lacI&lt;/em&gt; and the original Luria-Delbrück &lt;em&gt;tonB/tonA&lt;/em&gt; phage resistance assay. A notable design in &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt; used &lt;em&gt;thyP3&lt;/em&gt; (thymidylate synthase) with a temperature-sensitive buffer enzyme to prevent fitness defects during growth, and selected for trimethoprim resistance — explicitly selecting for survival rather than growth to avoid post-selection mutagenesis.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Mutation Accumulation Experiments&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Serial single-cell bottlenecking followed by whole-genome resequencing (MA/WGS) allows detection of essentially any mutation that isn't immediately lethal, bypassing the constraint of requiring a selectable phenotype. This approach gives a genome-wide target and captures intergenic mutations, synonymous substitutions, and large structural rearrangements invisible to reporters. The tradeoff is time and sequencing cost. A large compilation of mutation rates across bacteria is provided in Table 2, including rpoB-based estimates of ~0.33 × 10⁻¹⁰ per nucleotide per generation for both &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt; under LB conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Maximum-Depth Sequencing&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A newer method using unique molecular identifiers (UMIs) to generate consensus sequences from individual genomic molecules, reducing sequencing error to arbitrarily low levels with sufficient depth. Targeted to specific regions of interest rather than genome-wide; can even detect lethal mutations. Faster than MA/WGS for focused questions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Mechanisms of Spontaneous Mutagenesis&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;DNA Replication Errors&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Base pairing errors&lt;/strong&gt; arise when DNA Pol III misincorporates a non-cognate dNTP, evading both proofreading and mismatch repair (MMR). Two mechanistic explanations are discussed:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Rare tautomer hypothesis&lt;/em&gt;: Bases occasionally adopt alternative tautomeric forms with geometry close enough to canonical Watson-Crick pairs that they escape proofreading. Structural evidence from a C·A mispair crystal structure supports this.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Sequence context effects&lt;/em&gt;: Neighboring purines at the 3′ end of the primer strand increase mispair likelihood via base stacking. High GC content flanking a mispair reduces proofreading efficiency because stable base-pairing impedes 3′ terminus entry into the exonuclease active site. Mutation accumulation data confirm that neighboring G·C pairs elevate substitution rates genome-wide.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;8-oxo-dGTP incorporation&lt;/strong&gt;: Reactive oxygen species from respiration oxidise guanine to 8-oxo-G. This lesion preferentially adopts the &lt;em&gt;syn&lt;/em&gt; conformation, where it pairs with adenosine rather than cytosine, causing G·C→T·A transversions. The GO system (MutT, MutM, MutY) sanitises the nucleotide pool and repairs 8-oxo-G in DNA; loss of the GO system is one of the very few single-pathway knockouts that measurably increases the base pair substitution rate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ribonucleotide misincorporation&lt;/strong&gt;: NTPs vastly outnumber dNTPs, so Pol III incorporates ~2000 ribonucleotides per genome replication. These are normally removed by ribonucleotide excision repair (RER); loss of RER has a mild mutagenic effect in bacteria, in contrast to the dramatic effect in yeast (where Top1-mediated processing generates deletions).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Strand misalignment / template switching&lt;/strong&gt;: Accurate replication on a misaligned template produces &quot;encoded errors&quot; — insertions, deletions, substitutions — without any misinsertion per se. Quasipalindromic sequences form hairpins that promote template switching and are local mutation hotspots. Long homopolymer runs promote strand slippage, generating small indels; these are enriched in intergenic regions (consistent with purifying selection against frameshifting homopolymers in coding sequences).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;DNA Deamination&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cytosine in single-stranded DNA deaminates ~140× faster than in double-stranded DNA, making any process that transiently exposes ssDNA mutagenic. The result is C→U, which after replication causes G·C→A·T transitions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;During replication&lt;/strong&gt;: The lagging-strand template is transiently single-stranded, making it the primary site of deamination-driven G·C→A·T transitions. If Ung removes the uracil before replication, the resulting abasic site is bypassed by a translesion polymerase typically inserting adenosine — same outcome. This mechanism helps explain the nearly universal GC skew in bacterial genomes (enrichment of G over C on the lagging-strand template).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;During transcription&lt;/strong&gt;: The non-template strand is exposed as ssDNA during elongation. In Ung-deficient &lt;em&gt;Salmonella&lt;/em&gt;, G·C→A·T transitions are enriched on the non-template strand of highly expressed genes. Because most bacterial genes are transcribed co-directionally with replication, the lagging-strand template and the non-template strand are often the same physical strand — these two mechanisms are additive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During &lt;strong&gt;transcription initiation&lt;/strong&gt;, the promoter is melted but not paired with RNA, exposing both strands. The conserved A−7 position in the −10 element is left as ssDNA when T−7 is sequestered by sigma, making it susceptible to deamination to hypoxanthine, causing A·T→G·C transitions. This is a hotspot in both &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Replication-Transcription Conflict&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because replication forks move much faster than RNA polymerase, collisions are inevitable. Two orientations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Co-directional&lt;/strong&gt;: replication catches up to transcription from behind
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Head-on&lt;/strong&gt;: replication and transcription approach each other on opposite strands
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Head-on conflicts are more mutagenic, stalling the replication fork and potentially recruiting error-prone translesion polymerases (PolY1 in &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt;, though its specific role remains contested). Head-on conflicts also elevate indel rates at positions where the fork first meets RNA polymerase, and increase T→C transitions in promoter regions, possibly by prolonging ssDNA exposure during initiation. The enrichment of essential genes in co-directional orientation likely reflects strong purifying selection against head-on promoter mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Spontaneous SOS Induction&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;About 1% of exponentially growing &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; cells are undergoing the SOS response even without exogenous stress. Three SOS-induced polymerases are discussed:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pol IV (DinB)&lt;/strong&gt;: makes single-nucleotide deletions via misaligned primer extension; accurate on N2-G adducts but error-prone on undamaged DNA
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pol V (UmuCD)&lt;/strong&gt;: primarily responsible for SOS-induced mutagenesis, incorporating nucleotides across abasic sites and producing transversions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pol II&lt;/strong&gt;: has proofreading activity, largely anti-mutagenic, may correct Pol IV/V errors
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite spontaneous SOS induction in ~1% of cells, MA/WGS experiments in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; lacking Pol IV and/or V show no detectable change in mutation rate, suggesting they contribute negligibly to spontaneous mutagenesis during normal growth.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Homologous Recombination and Insertion Sequences&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Homologous recombination between non-allelic rRNA operons can cause large rearrangements; gene conversion between paralogous genes causes base substitutions. These are difficult to study with conventional reporters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Insertion sequences (ISs) are a previously underappreciated source. Analysis of 520 MA lines found IS insertion rates within an order of magnitude of the base substitution rate (~3.5 × 10⁻⁴ per genome per generation). IS1A and IS5A account for ~80% of movements in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; MG1655. Anaerobic growth specifically elevated IS150 mobilisation, mechanism unknown.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Takeaways Relevant to Your Work&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A few points worth highlighting for your rpoB fluctuation assay context:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The paper explicitly validates rpoB as a reverse mutation reporter and notes that selection on rifampicin plates selects for &lt;strong&gt;survival&lt;/strong&gt;, not growth — one of the cleaner fluctuation test designs because it avoids post-selection mutagenesis from slow colony growth.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Table 2 gives rpoB-based BPS rates of 0.33–0.34 × 10⁻¹⁰ per nucleotide per generation in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;B. subtilis&lt;/em&gt; on LB — useful benchmarks for your WT MG1655 rate.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The paper notes rpoB has a &lt;strong&gt;limited and specific target size&lt;/strong&gt; (only certain missense mutations confer resistance), which is both a feature (clean signal) and a constraint (you're sampling a subset of all possible mutations). This matters when comparing mutation spectra across your rpoB mutant strains.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The discussion of how growth media composition (LB vs minimal media) and temperature can shift mutation rates is directly relevant to your experimental design comparing M9gluc and LB across temperatures.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
