    <bib:Article rdf:about="#item_573">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf>
           <bib:Journal></bib:Journal>
        </dcterms:isPartOf>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_574"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_572"/>
        <dc:title>The origin of mutants</dc:title>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_574">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is the famous Cairns, Overbaugh &amp;amp; Miller paper from Nature (1988) that essentially launched the modern &quot;directed mutation&quot; (later &quot;adaptive mutation&quot;) controversy. Here's a detailed walkthrough.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Motivation and framing&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors open by questioning the field's near-universal assumption that mutations arise purely at random, independent of whether they'd be useful. They trace this assumption back to Luria and Delbrück's 1943 fluctuation test on phage-T1 resistance in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, which distinguished two models: mutants arising only after exposure to the phage (predicting a Poisson distribution of mutant counts across replicate cultures), versus mutants arising spontaneously at random times throughout prior growth (predicting wildly variable counts, since an early mutation expands clonally into a &quot;jackpot&quot;). Luria-Delbrück observed the jackpot pattern and concluded resistance mutations are spontaneous, occurring before any selection is applied. Replica plating later reinforced this by isolating mutants without ever exposing them to selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cairns, Overbaugh and Miller point out a loophole, though: phage and streptomycin resistance aren't phenotypically expressed until several generations after the underlying mutation occurs. So the resistant colonies people scored had actually originated many generations before the plating event — meaning these classic experiments only proved that some mutation is spontaneous and pre-selection; they couldn't have detected (or ruled out) a directed process operating right at the moment of selection. To actually test for that, you need a phenotype that's expressed immediately, plus a selection scheme gentle enough that non-mutant cells survive too (so they have the &quot;opportunity&quot; to attempt directed mutation rather than just dying).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The theoretical framework: two distributions and their composite&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This section is the statistical heart of the paper, and it's basically an extension of Luria-Delbrück machinery. If mutations arise randomly throughout each culture's growth history before plating, the resulting counts follow the Lea-Coulson distribution, whose upper tail obeys a simple power law: the probability of seeing at least &lt;em&gt;j&lt;/em&gt; mutants in a culture approaches &lt;em&gt;m&lt;/em&gt;/&lt;em&gt;j&lt;/em&gt;, where &lt;em&gt;m&lt;/em&gt; is the mean number of mutational events per culture. Plotted as log(P_x) against log(x), this produces a straight line with slope close to −1 at high x — the jackpot signature (Fig. 1a, shown for &lt;em&gt;m&lt;/em&gt; = 0.5–16).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If instead mutations only arise after plating, among cells already under selection, the counts follow a Poisson distribution — much narrower, falling off steeply as x increases (Fig. 1b).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If both processes operate simultaneously, you get a composite: a steep initial drop (dominated by the post-selection Poisson component) that flattens into the characteristic slope-−1 tail (dominated by rare pre-existing jackpots) at high x (Fig. 1c). This composite shape becomes their diagnostic tool — if you see it, you have evidence that mutants are still being generated after cells are exposed to selection, beyond whatever pre-existing jackpots account for.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lac+ reversion experiments&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using an &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; Δlac pro F′ lacZ_am strain (an amber mutation in lacZ, so reversion to Lac+ is immediately expressed), they compared it to a derivative carrying a deletion in the uvrB-bio region. Grown in yeast extract or M9-glycerol, then plated on M9-lactose, both strains showed the same upper-end jackpot behavior (same mutation rate during growth), but the uvrB-deletion strain produced a large excess of additional mutants matching a Poisson distribution — i.e., mutants apparently arising after plating (Fig. 2). These late colonies were also slower-growing than jackpot-derived ones, consistent with being more recent mutational events with less time to reach full colony size by 48 hours. The uvrB strain generated roughly 3-5 times as many of these late mutants per day as the uvrB+ strain, which made it their strain of choice for probing the phenomenon further.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They then tested whether this late-mutant accumulation actually depends on lactose itself (Fig. 3), rather than just being some fixed stationary-phase &quot;clock.&quot; Bacteria were buried in agar and then overlaid with lactose-containing top agar either immediately, or after a 1- or 3-day delay. The whole time-course of colony appearance shifted by exactly the corresponding delay — mutants didn't start accumulating until lactose was actually present, even with the non-hydrolyzable inducer IPTG also on the plate. As a specificity control, they monitored an unrelated, immediately-expressed phenotype (valine resistance) on the same lactose-selection plates and found ValR colony counts didn't increase over time the way Lac+ counts did — arguing against a trivial explanation of generalized hypermutation or slow leaky growth on lactose, and toward something that looks targeted at the relevant phenotype.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Shapiro deletion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is presented as their cleanest case. Building on a strain constructed by Shapiro (1984), araC is fused to lacZ but separated by a Mu phage DNA segment containing a transcription terminator, making the strain both Lac- and Ara-. Deleting that Mu segment creates an arabinose-dependent Lac+ phenotype (&quot;Lac(Ara)+&quot;), selectable on lactose-arabinose plates. Colonies of this phenotype appear conspicuously late on selective plates (much later than colonies seeded from pre-existing Lac(Ara)+ cells), and the background rate of spontaneous deletion during unselected growth is essentially unmeasurable, estimated below 10⁻¹¹. Control strains carrying simple point mutations instead of the Mu insertion showed no such delay, ruling out a generic stationary-phase expression-lag explanation — the delay is specifically about the time needed to excise the Mu segment. In liquid culture experiments, Lac(Ara)+ cells accumulated at a rate of at least 10⁻⁸ — several orders of magnitude above the unselected background — but only when both lactose and arabinose were present together; adding lactose to an already-arabinose-containing stationary culture triggered detectable Lac(Ara)+ cells within 1-2 hours. The combination of an essentially undetectable spontaneous rate, a complex rearrangement (not a simple point mutation), and an apparent dependence on the presence of both selective nutrients is what makes this case hard to wave away.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Activation of cryptic genes (the ebg system)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Their final example concerns the cryptic ebg operon, which can substitute for a deleted lacZ. Activation requires at least two specific mutations — one in the ebgR repressor and one at a particular site in ebgA — each occurring spontaneously at a frequency below 10⁻⁸, with neither mutation alone sufficient for lactose use. Yet colonies held on lactose minimal plates reliably produce Lac+ papillae within about two weeks. Under simple independent-mutation assumptions, the joint probability of both rare events co-occurring in a population within that timeframe should be vanishingly small, which is exactly why the authors flag it as a case &quot;of special interest.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Discussion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors are careful to frame their conclusion modestly: not that directed mutation is proven, but that the dogma of universal randomness has never actually been rigorously tested, and their handful of experiments offer circumstantial evidence worth taking seriously. They situate the question within an old &quot;reductionist vs. romantic&quot; debate about whether genomes can be shaped by experience, and note that since the central dogma was formulated, molecular biology had already accumulated several precedents for non-reductionist information flow — reverse transcription, stress-switchable genomic instability, localized hypervariability. The piece they see as still missing is feedback from protein back to mRNA. They sketch a speculative mechanism: a cell could generate a diverse pool of mRNAs, monitor which protein product functions best, and selectively reverse-transcribe that one back into the genome (loosely analogous to a retroviral gag-pol-like system), or more simply, switch on indiscriminate reverse transcription transiently at the moment growth resumes, so that whichever transcript happened to be associated with renewed growth gets preferentially copied back into DNA. Either route would amount to a mechanism for inheriting an acquired characteristic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Worth noting for context: this paper (along with Cairns and Foster's 1991 follow-up) is what kicked off the whole &quot;adaptive mutation&quot; field. Most of what they observed was eventually explained not by anything as exotic as reverse-transcription feedback, but by stress-induced mutagenesis pathways — RpoS-regulated responses in starving cells, SOS induction, and error-prone polymerases including Pol IV/DinB generating transient hypermutability in stressed subpopulations. That's the mechanistic resolution that connects this 1988 paper directly to the kind of DinB-dependent mutagenesis you've been working through in your &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; work — this is effectively the historical root of that whole line of inquiry.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
